:: \ Eagar, AZ Options {"position":"375,600","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/W3qvyGKF/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The world that greets you is both familiar and alien. Eagar, Arizona—or what remains of it—spreads before you under a harsh sun. The once-thriving town is now mostly ruins, with only a few structures still partially intact.

The air feels different—drier than it should be, carrying particles that sparkle strangely in the sunlight. To the east, what must once have been green mountains are now bare rock, scored with massive erosion channels. In the distance, you spot what appears to be an elongated, dried-up lakebed where no lake should exist.

Your Yellowstone coordinates are clear, but you'll need transportation and supplies to have any hope of reaching them. First, you'll need to explore what's left of this town.

Three locations look promising:

[[Explore the abandoned gas station|Abandoned Gas Station]] - A partially collapsed structure with faded signage, possibly containing fuel or vehicle parts.

[[Check out the residential area|Residential Area]] - A cluster of homes in varying states of decay, where you might find personal supplies or useful items.

[[Investigate the water collection facility|Water Collection Facility]] - A relatively intact building with large cisterns on its roof, potentially providing water for your journey.

(if: $canLeaveEagar)[
  You have everything needed to repair the ATV and continue your journey toward Yellowstone.
  
  [[Leave Eagar]] - Repair the ATV and depart for Fence Lake, New Mexico.
]


:: \ Leave Eagar {"position":"950,425","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/MHkSJc2s/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With the map showing the route north, the solar charging unit, and the ATV ignition chip all secured, you return to the abandoned gas station where you found the electric ATV. The vehicle represents your best chance at making the journey to Yellowstone.

You connect the solar charging unit to the ATV's battery system and place it in direct sunlight. After several hours, the charge indicators show enough power to begin your journey. You insert the ignition chip, and the ATV's systems come to life with a soft hum—the sound of possibility in a silent world.

Loading your gathered supplies securely onto the vehicle, you take one last look at what remains of Eagar. This place preserved you until you awakened, but it holds no answers about your past or your purpose. Those, you hope, await at Yellowstone.

The map indicates your next destination should be Fence Lake, New Mexico—a settlement that may offer additional supplies and information for your journey. With the sun still high in the sky, you set out, leaving Eagar and its mysteries behind.

The ATV handles the rough terrain well, its electric motor nearly silent as you navigate out of town and onto what once was a highway north. The transformed landscape stretches before you—beautiful and terrible in its alien familiarity.

[[Begin your journey to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Intro]]


:: \ Menu {"position":"425,100","size":"100,100"}
(b4r:"solid")+(b4r-colour:yellow)[(text-colour:yellow)[''Journey to Yellowstone'']]
By Ryan Godburn





























[[Start|Story Start]]


:: \ Search tool cabinets {"position":"2175,200","size":"100,100"}
Most of the tool cabinets have been ransacked for commonly useful items, but specialized drawers remain partially stocked. You find equipment designed specifically for working on aquatic vehicle modifications—unusual tools that would have had little value to most scavengers.

One drawer contains waterproof technical manuals detailing how vehicles were adapted after the floods. The modifications are extensive: raised air intakes, sealed electrical systems, reinforced chassis to handle the uneven terrain of collapsed infrastructure, and even flotation devices for crossing shallow water bodies that formed where roads once ran.

The manuals reference how the "Phase 3 Highland Evacuation" required vehicles capable of traversing both flooded lowlands and the rugged mountain terrain around sanctuary sites. Specialized modifications were apparently performed at designated facilities like this one, preparing vehicles for the final evacuations of essential personnel.

One document outlines "Sanctuary Transport Protocols," with specific routes marked for Yellowstone access. Notes indicate that only vehicles with the proper modifications and security clearance were permitted on these routes in the final days.

[[Return to the mechanic shop|Abandoned Mechanic Shop]]


:: 1l {"position":"6800,150","size":"100,100"}
The left path narrows as you follow it, the trees pressing closer on either side. Their bark has an unusual texture—almost scaled, like reptile skin—and occasionally seems to shift when viewed from the corner of your eye.

The moss underfoot grows thicker here, its faint luminescence creating pools of ghostly light. Your footsteps make no sound, as if the forest is absorbing all noise.

Through a rare gap in the canopy, you catch a glimpse of what should be sky—but instead of blue expanse or clouds, you see what appears to be a geometric pattern, angular and precise. You blink, and it's gone, leaving you to question whether it was real or a hallucination.

The path splits again, offering two choices. To your left, a route that descends slightly into deeper shadow. Ahead, a continuation of your current path toward what might be a clearing in the distance.

[[Go left toward the shadows|1la]]
[[Continue straight ahead|1r]]


:: 1la {"position":"6900,150","size":"100,100"}
The descending path grows darker as you follow it, the luminescent moss providing the only illumination. The trees here are different—their branches curve downward rather than reaching for the sky, creating a cathedral-like space of arching limbs.

You notice marks on some of the trunks—not natural patterns in the bark, but deliberate symbols etched into the wood. They resemble mathematical notations or circuit diagrams, complex and precise. When you touch one, a tingling sensation travels up your arm, and for a moment, you perceive the forest differently—as if seeing its underlying structure, a pattern of energy and information rather than matter.

The sensation fades, leaving you disoriented but with a strange sense that you're beginning to understand this place. It doesn't follow the natural laws you're accustomed to—it has its own logic, its own reality.

The path branches once more. A narrow trail winds upward to your right, while a broader path continues to your left.

[[Take the narrow upward path|3a]]
[[Follow the broader path|1l]]


:: 1r {"position":"6600,150","size":"100,100"}
The right path leads you past the rock formation, its surface covered in fungi that pulse with soft blue light. The air here feels different—slightly charged, as if before a lightning storm.

As you walk, you notice the trees becoming more uniform, their trunks perfectly straight and spaced at regular intervals. No forest grows this way naturally—this must be by design, though whose design and for what purpose remains unclear.

You hear a distant sound—almost like music but not quite, more like mathematical patterns translated into audio. It fades before you can identify its source.

The perfectly straight path suddenly turns at a precise right angle, presenting you with a choice—continue following it to the right, or take a less defined path that cuts through the trees to your left.

[[Follow the right angle turn|2da]]
[[Take the cut-through to the left|1l]]


:: 2da {"position":"6600,250","size":"100,100"}
The right-angle turn leads to an area where the forest's geometric qualities become even more pronounced. The trees here form perfect rows and columns, creating a grid that stretches in all directions. The pattern is so precise that it triggers a sense of vertigo—your mind struggles to accept such mathematical perfection in what should be an organic environment.

Through the grid-like arrangement, you spot movement in the distance—something large shifting between the trees. When you focus directly on it, it vanishes, visible only in peripheral vision. Whatever it is seems to be observing you, studying your progress through this labyrinth.

The grid-like path continues forward, while a less structured route branches off to your right, winding between trees that grow more chaotically.

[[Continue through the grid|2db]]
[[Take the winding path to the right|1r]]


:: 2db {"position":"6600,350","size":"100,100"}
As you progress deeper into the grid-patterned section of forest, the ground beneath you changes. The luminescent moss gives way to a surface that resembles soil but feels slightly yielding, like walking on a membrane. Each footstep causes subtle ripples to spread outward, as if the entire forest floor is connected as one organism.

The perfectly aligned trees here have begun to emit a faint humming vibration. When you place your hand on one trunk, you feel information flowing through it—not thoughts exactly, but organized data, as if the trees form a network exchanging signals.

Your perception of time begins to distort. Minutes might be passing, or hours—your watch has stopped functioning, its digital display frozen or showing impossible configurations of numbers.

The grid pattern offers two continuing paths—one that maintains the perfect geometry, and another where the pattern begins to break down into more chaotic arrangements.

[[Follow the perfect geometry|2dc]]
[[Move toward the chaotic section|2da]]


:: 2dc {"position":"6600,450","size":"100,100"}
The path of perfect geometry leads to what appears to be the epicenter of the grid pattern. Here, the trees form concentric circles around a small clearing, their trunks increasing in thickness as they approach the center—like a living mandala or the physical expression of a mathematical function.

In the clearing stands a single tree unlike any you've seen before. Its bark is covered in patterns that shift and change as you watch—transforming from something resembling circuit boards to organic cellular structures to mathematical symbols and back again. It emanates a soft light that pulses in a complex rhythm.

When you approach this central tree, a profound sense of recognition washes over you—not that you've seen this specific tree before, but that it represents something fundamental to your journey, perhaps even to your identity.

You feel drawn to continue forward, though the only apparent path leads back the way you came.

[[Return to the previous section|2db]]


:: 3a {"position":"6900,250","size":"100,100"}
The narrow upward path winds steeply between trees whose trunks twist together like braided rope. As you climb, the canopy thins slightly, allowing more of the strange indigo light to filter through. What you can see of the sky continues to defy normal explanation—instead of clouds, there are shifting patterns that resemble neural networks or flowing data visualizations.

You come across a small spring bubbling up from between exposed roots. The water has an odd clarity to it, reflecting light in prismatic patterns. When you touch it, brief flashes of images flood your mind—laboratories, people in environmental suits, monitoring equipment, and glimpses of what might be Yellowstone, though transformed in ways you don't recognize.

The path levels out onto a small plateau with three diverging routes—one continuing upward, one descending sharply, and one that winds around the contour of the hill.

[[Continue climbing upward|3b]]
[[Take the descending path|1la]]


:: 3b {"position":"6900,350","size":"100,100"}
The upward path brings you to a vantage point where the forest's pattern becomes more apparent. From this height, you can see that the entire forest is arranged in a spiral configuration, with different sections exhibiting varying patterns and geometries—like a massive natural processing system or the physical manifestation of an algorithm.

Stone formations here bear markings that resemble code or equations, weathered but still legible. They seem to be instructions or navigation aids of some kind, though their meaning remains elusive.

The air feels thinner here, the metallic scent stronger. Occasionally, the entire forest seems to shift slightly, like an image going momentarily out of focus before reestablishing itself.

Three paths lead away from this vantage point—one continuing to higher elevation, one descending toward what appears to be a dense cluster of unusual growth, and one returning the way you came.

[[Climb to the higher elevation|3d]]
[[Descend toward the unusual growth|3c]]
[[Return the way you came|3a]]


:: 3c {"position":"6800,350","size":"100,100"}
Descending toward the unusual growth, you find yourself in a section of forest unlike any other you've encountered. The trees here have partially merged with what appear to be technological components—circuitry embedded in bark, fiber-optic-like filaments running through branches, leaves that resemble solar panels.

This hybrid ecosystem pulses with energy that you can feel as a vibration in your chest. The ground is covered not with moss but with a network of root-like structures that occasionally emit spark-like flashes of light.

As you move through this techno-organic environment, you experience brief flashes of memory—not your own, but data fragments from elsewhere. You see glimpses of scientific facilities, experiments involving genetic modification, and plans for "human adaptation acceleration." These visions fade quickly, leaving you with a growing suspicion about what Yellowstone might truly represent.

The path continues downward toward an area where the hybrid growth is even more pronounced, or you can follow a side trail that appears to lead to a different section of the forest.

[[Continue downward through the hybrid zone|4a]]
[[Take the side trail|3b]]


:: 3d {"position":"6900,450","size":"100,100"}
The path to higher elevation brings you to what feels like the edge of the forest maze. Here, the trees thin out enough to provide a broader view of your surroundings. What you see defies easy comprehension—the landscape beyond the forest appears to be in flux, shifting between different states like a visual glitch.

One moment you see what might be a research facility built into a mountainside, the next it transforms into something that resembles a natural formation but with too much symmetry to be truly natural. The sky above shows similar instability, with patterns that suggest a simulation or projection rather than actual atmosphere.

You find a marker here—a metallic post embedded in stone, with directional indicators pointing to different sections of the forest below. One is labeled with a symbol that resembles the Yellowstone location on your map, though rendered in a script you don't recognize.

From here, you can either return to the path below or follow a narrow trail that runs along the ridge.

[[Return to the path below|3b]]


:: 4a {"position":"6800,250","size":"100,100"}
The downward path through the hybrid zone leads to an area where the boundaries between organic and technological become completely blurred. Trees grow in configurations that optimize some unknown function, their branches forming antenna-like arrays pointed toward the strange sky.

The ground beneath your feet has a subtle responsiveness, as if adjusting to your presence. Each step leaves momentary impressions that slowly fade, like ripples in a pond.

You come across what appears to be a monitoring station integrated into a cluster of trees. Its displays flicker with information—readouts of environmental data, genetic sequencing, and adaptation metrics. One screen briefly displays a map that resembles Yellowstone, but overlaid with a complex grid system and markers labeled "Integration Nodes."

Two paths lead away from this area—one following what appears to be a line of these monitoring stations deeper into the forest, and another that curves away toward a different section.

[[Follow the line of monitoring stations|4b]]
[[Take the curving path|3c]]


:: 4b {"position":"6700,250","size":"100,100"}
Following the line of monitoring stations, you enter a section of the forest that feels increasingly artificial. The trees here grow in perfect symmetry, their branches forming geodesic patterns that create domed spaces beneath. The monitoring equipment becomes more sophisticated and numerous, some of it actively scanning the environment with beams of light that seem to pass through solid matter.

One station larger than the others appears to be a central hub. Its screens display what looks like status reports on some massive experiment or project. References to "adaptation acceleration," "environmental simulation integrity," and "subject navigation patterns" scroll across the displays.

When you approach this hub, it seems to recognize your presence. A screen changes to display a simple message: "CANDIDATE Y-3742 - SIMULATION NAVIGATION: 43% COMPLETE"

The implication sends a chill through you—is this entire forest some kind of test or simulation? And if so, what is its purpose, and who or what is administering it?

From the hub, two paths lead onward—one that follows an illuminated track that appears to be a main route, and another less defined path that winds between the perfectly symmetric trees.

[[Follow the illuminated track|4c]]
[[Take the winding path|4a]]


:: 4c {"position":"6700,350","size":"100,100"}
The illuminated track glows with embedded lights that pulse in patterns suggesting directional flow. Following it, you pass through a section of forest where the trees have been augmented with what appear to be observation devices—camera-like structures that track your movement, sensors that measure environmental variables, and communication arrays linking them together.

The forest here feels less like a natural environment and more like a controlled research space—a laboratory disguised as wilderness. Yet despite this artificiality, you notice signs of the system breaking down or evolving beyond its original parameters. Some trees have grown around and incorporated their monitoring devices, transforming them into new hybrid forms.

You reach a junction where the illuminated track splits. One branch continues in an orderly fashion, its lights pulsing with regular timing. The other branch's lights flicker erratically, suggesting some kind of malfunction or alternative function.

[[Follow the orderly track|4d]]
[[Take the erratic path|4b]]


:: 4d {"position":"6700,450","size":"100,100"}
The orderly track leads to what appears to be a processing center integrated into the forest itself. Trees have been grown or modified to form a structure resembling a building, their trunks merging to create walls and their branches weaving together to form a canopy roof.

Inside this living architecture, you find evidence of the purpose behind this strange forest maze. Holographic displays show the progression of human adaptation to changing environmental conditions—both natural evolutionary responses and deliberately accelerated modifications. The forest itself appears to be a testing ground for human navigation through unfamiliar hybrid ecosystems—part natural, part engineered.

One display shows profiles of "adaptation candidates"—individuals selected for specific genetic traits that might allow survival in dramatically altered environments. With a shock, you recognize your own face among them, labeled "Y-3742: WILDERNESS ADAPTATION PROTOCOL."

As you absorb this revelation, you notice two exits from the processing center—one leading deeper into what appears to be the heart of the research installation, and another that seems to bypass it entirely.

[[Enter the research installation|4c]]
[[Take the bypass route|4e]]


:: 4e {"position":"6800,450","size":"100,100"}
Choosing the bypass route, you find yourself on a path that seems to skirt the edge of the controlled research environment. Here, the forest shows signs of breaking free from whatever parameters were established for it—trees grow in more natural patterns, the hybrid technology appears to be evolving in unexpected ways, and the rigid geometry of the constructed maze gives way to more organic arrangements.

As you follow this boundary between the controlled and uncontrolled aspects of the forest, you begin to see increasing glimpses of sky through the canopy. The indigo color has started to shift toward more natural blues, suggesting you might be approaching the edge of whatever zone or simulation encompasses this strange place.

The path climbs gradually, bringing you to higher ground where the trees thin out. Ahead, you can see what appears to be genuine daylight—unfiltered and recognizable. The forest behind you still pulses with its strange energies and patterns, but before you lies what seems to be a way out.

You've found your way through the maze, though the revelations about your own identity and purpose raise as many questions as they answer. Yellowstone—and whatever awaits you there—seems increasingly complex and mysterious.

[[Leave the forest maze|Exit Maze]]
[[Return to explore more|4d]]


:: Abandoned Farm {"position":"1425,225","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/yxWStbKh/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The abandoned farm sits on a plot of land that must have once been fertile but is now cracked and barren. The two-story farmhouse and adjacent barn have weathered decades of harsh conditions, their structures still largely intact though clearly abandoned.

Wind whistles through broken windows and gaps in the wooden slats. You notice unusual modifications to the property—water collection systems on the roof, reinforced doors and windows, and what appears to be a machine of some kind next to the barn, partially covered by a tarp.

The farmhouse doors are secured with a heavy padlock, but the barn doors hang partially open, creaking in the wind.

[[Enter the farmhouse|Investigation Inside]] - Try to find a way into the main house.

[[Explore the barn interior|Look Inside barn]] - Check what might have been left behind.

[[Examine the covered machine|Look at Machine]] - Investigate the strange apparatus near the barn.

[[Return to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Options]] - Continue exploring elsewhere.


:: Abandoned Gas Station {"position":"525,400","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/fbLZcnfv/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The abandoned gas station stands like a skeleton of pre-collapse commerce. Its roof has partially caved in, and desert plants have reclaimed portions of the cracked concrete lot. Ancient vehicles, their components stripped for parts long ago, rust quietly in the perpetual sun.

The main building is accessible, though you'll need to watch your step among the debris. Dust-covered shelves and broken display cases hint at what once was a convenience store. Behind a counter, you see the remains of a cash register—worthless now—and what might be some kind of documentation.

The garage area at the back seems relatively intact, and could potentially house useful mechanical components. There's also a pile of debris in one corner that might be worth examining.

[[Search behind the counter|Search behind the counter]] - Look for maps or information about the area.
[[Check the garage area|Check the garage area]] - Search for vehicle parts or tools.
[[Rummage through debris|Rummage through debris]] - Dig through the pile for potentially useful items.

After you've finished exploring:
[[Return to town exploration| Eagar, AZ Options]] - Explore other locations in Eagar.


:: Abandoned Mechanic Shop {"position":"2175,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/9XvGsc4r/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The large garage facility still bears the faded sign "Rio Puerco Auto & Truck Repair." Its main doors are secured with a heavy chain, but a side entrance has been forced open at some point, allowing you access.

Inside, the cavernous space contains several vehicles in various states of disrepair. Most notable is a Toyota Tacoma pickup truck that appears to be in remarkably good condition. It's positioned on a hydraulic lift that's long since lost power, leaving the truck suspended a few feet off the ground.

Tool cabinets line the walls, though most have been ransacked for their more valuable contents. An office area occupies one corner, with a desk covered in paperwork. The entire facility shows signs of having been used well after the initial collapse, with evidence of makeshift repairs and adaptations to equipment.

[[Examine the Tacoma|Examine the Tacoma]] - Check if the truck could be operational.
[[Look through the tool cabinets| Search tool cabinets]] - Search for useful tools or parts.
[[Investigate the office|Check office area]] - Look for information or keys.
[[Check beneath the lift|Look under the lift]] - Examine the area under the suspended truck.

[[Return to Gallup|Gallup Options]] - Explore other locations in the city.


:: Access Emergency Bunker {"position":"5925,350","size":"100,100"}
The Emergency Bunker lies beneath the commercial district, accessed through hardened elevator shafts designed to withstand extreme conditions. Security systems still function, requiring specific access codes or emergency override sequences to grant entry to this most protected of Alamosa's facilities.

Descending into the bunker complex, you enter a self-contained environment designed for continuity of essential functions through crisis conditions. Unlike the repurposed spaces above ground, this facility was built specifically for its final purpose—maintaining critical systems and preserving key resources when normal operations became impossible.

Find the city's backup generator system, which could be used to restore partial power to systems not currently active. The redundant power infrastructure includes advanced fusion technology that continues to function decades after installation, providing energy to maintain the bunker's operations and critical external systems indefinitely.

The most significant discovery is the nature of what was considered essential to preserve—not human life primarily, but data. The central chamber houses massive server arrays containing comprehensive genetic sequences, environmental monitoring records, and research data from all of Alamosa's facilities. The priority was preserving information rather than individuals, knowledge rather than its knowers.

Command centers for managing automated systems throughout the settlement line the main corridor, their interfaces still active, monitoring conditions in empty laboratories and unoccupied streets. The bunker effectively serves as the autonomous brain of Alamosa, maintaining functions according to priorities established before human departure—a technological ghost continuing to operate the empty shell of civilization.

The fundamental isolation of the bunker—physically separated from the world it monitors, preserving information without understanding its significance, maintaining systems without purpose—mirrors the broader disconnection that characterized humanity's final approach to crisis. In attempting to insulate essential functions from environmental collapse, they created a metaphor for their own separation from natural systems—protection that became a form of entombment.

[[Return to the commercial district|Commercial District]]


:: Access back room {"position":"1400,700","size":"100,100"}
The back room of the post office appears to have been converted into a small museum in the years before abandonment. Display cases contain artifacts showing the evolution of mail delivery through American history, with a special section dedicated to the "Final Postal Era."

The exhibits show how delivery routes changed as coastlines vanished and new inland seas formed. Maps track the progressive abandonment of service to regions deemed "non-viable," with routes consolidating toward highland areas.

One display, titled "Communications During Transition," details how the postal service became the default communication network after digital infrastructure failed. It explains how specially trained carriers maintained connections between isolated survival communities and remaining government outposts.

A final exhibit, incomplete and apparently rushed, shows the establishment of "Emergency Repository Services" at certain post offices, where critical genetic and historical records were centralized before transport to "permanent archival facilities in stable regions."

This suggests the government was attempting to preserve essential human knowledge and genetic diversity as society collapsed—with Yellowstone potentially serving as one such repository.

[[Return to the main post office area|USPS]]


:: Access computer terminal {"position":"750,750","size":"100,100"}
A dust-covered terminal in the administrative office still has power, running on some form of emergency system. The screen flickers to life as you approach, displaying a login prompt.

After several attempts at common passwords, you manage to access the system using an administrator override sequence you find written on a note taped under the desk—apparently security practices deteriorated as the crisis worsened.

The files are fragmented, but you piece together that this area was once controlled by "AquaTech Industries," a corporation that gained control of water resources during the climate crisis. Records show how they restricted access during shortages, prioritizing certain neighborhoods and facilities over others based on payment ability and "strategic importance."

One document details how water became the ultimate currency in the region, with AquaTech effectively governing through control of this essential resource. As conditions worsened, there are references to "civil unrest incidents" and "resource protection protocols."

The final entries mention a government takeover of AquaTech facilities for "Emergency Continuity Operations" and references to "Phase 1 Highland Relocation"—seemingly connected to the Yellowstone destination that keeps appearing in your findings.

[[Continue exploring the facility|Water Collection Facility]]


:: Access digital archives {"position":"125,1625","size":"100,100"}
The digital archives occupy a secure room within the visitor center, its infrastructure designed to preserve electronic records despite power fluctuations and environmental degradation. Specialized storage systems, redundant power supplies, and climate control equipment maintain the digital information in accessible condition despite decades without human maintenance.

View satellite imagery showing the evolution of the earth and the floods. These time-lapse visualizations document the transformation of the global landscape—coastlines redefined by rising seas, inland geography altered by changing precipitation patterns, and vegetation zones shifted by migrating climate boundaries. The progression reveals the acceleration of change as environmental tipping points were crossed, with transformations that might naturally take millennia occurring within decades.

What's most striking is how the information preservation priorities shifted as the crisis deepened—comprehensive global monitoring giving way to focused attention on regions designated for continued human habitation, and finally to detailed observation of specific territories surrounding sanctuary locations like Yellowstone.

The final data sets concentrate exclusively on environmental conditions along the routes to primary sanctuaries, with particular attention to factors affecting human adaptation requirements. Atmospheric composition, radiation levels, water quality, and ecosystem transformations are documented with precision that suggests continued monitoring until very near the end of organized operations.

The archives themselves represent a remarkable feat of technological preservation—electronic information maintained in accessible form decades after the broader digital infrastructure collapsed. The isolation of these knowledge systems from the networks they once connected to creates a poignant reminder of how completely the information environment fragmented as physical and social systems failed.

[[Return to the visitor center|Visitor Center & Transportation Hub]]


:: Access manager's office {"position":"2225,550","size":"100,100"}
The warehouse's massive food storage area once held enough supplies to feed thousands. Most perishables are long gone—consumed or rotted during the initial crisis. What remains are scattered preserved items in specialized packaging designed for extreme long-term storage.

You find educational materials explaining how food preservation methods evolved as the flooding intensified. As traditional agriculture collapsed, techniques were developed to extend shelf life from years to decades. These methods combined ancient preservation knowledge with modern technology—vacuum sealing, radiation treatment, and specialized inert gas environments.

A partially intact display explains how diet needed to adapt to changing environmental conditions, with new nutritional guidelines reflecting both available food sources and the body's changing needs in the altered atmosphere.

You gather some of the still-viable preserved food packets, noting their "Military-Grade Preservation" labels and 100-year shelf life claims. These will supplement your supplies for the journey ahead.

[[Return to the warehouse|Costco Warehouse]]


:: Access the Basement Laboratory {"position":"5475,500","size":"100,100"}
The Basement Laboratory is accessible only through a heavily reinforced elevator shaft, its entrance secured by multiple verification systems that have defaulted to emergency access protocols in the absence of human operators. As you descend, the temperature drops noticeably, and the air takes on a sterile, chemical quality maintained by still-functioning environmental controls.

Unlike the research spaces above, this underground facility was clearly designed for long-term preservation and containment of its subjects. Rows of transparent chambers line the walls, each a self-contained environment with independent life support systems. Most stand empty, but several contain what appear to be human forms—modified to varying degrees by the adaptation procedures developed here.

Discover sealed specimens of evolved humans, along with notes suggesting some mutations were beneficial while others proved deadly. The research logs classify adaptations by type and viability—respiratory modifications for high-altitude environments, dermal changes for radiation resistance, metabolic alterations for different nutrient sources. The successful candidates show harmonious integration of these changes; failed ones display grotesque imbalances where adaptations conflicted with basic biological functions.

As you move through the facility, you notice something disturbing—one of the occupied chambers shows signs of active function rather than stasis. Its occupant appears more extensively modified than the others, with physical characteristics that seem to blend human and non-human attributes. Monitoring equipment indicates ongoing biological processes—this subject is not preserved but alive, and has been for decades.

Suddenly, the chamber's status indicators shift from stable to active. The being inside moves, its eyes opening to reveal pupils adapted for low-light conditions. It focuses on you with unmistakable awareness, its expression revealing intelligence and purpose. The containment system begins an automated release sequence, pressure equalizing as seals prepare to disengage.

You have two choices:

[[Flee the laboratory|Fight]] - Escape before the chamber opens.
[[Stay and confront the being|Fight]] - Discover what this adapted human has become.


:: Access the Computer Lab {"position":"5125,400","size":"100,100"}
The Computer Lab is housed in a bunker-like structure with reinforced walls and multiple security checkpoints. Unlike more accessible campus buildings, this facility shows no signs of natural reclamation—its isolation systems have maintained a sterile environment protecting sensitive equipment.

The primary security door stands ajar, emergency protocols having overridden locks during the final evacuation. Inside, rows of advanced computing equipment hum with persistent energy, cooling systems working tirelessly to maintain optimal temperatures. The fact that these systems remain operational after decades suggests they were deemed essential enough to warrant permanent power allocation—whatever data they process must have been considered vital to preserve.

Terminal screens flicker with activity, running automated programs that continue analysis without human supervision. When you access one of the workstations, you find partial records of test subjects, including one whose profile matches your fragmented memories, labeled "Prometheus Candidate."

Your file contains disturbing details—genetic modification procedures, cognitive conditioning programs, and memory suppression protocols. Notes reference "identity disassociation" to prevent psychological rejection of physical adaptations. Most significantly, your designation as a "Prometheus Candidate" appears to indicate someone selected to carry modified genes to Yellowstone, where you would serve as a "genetic seed" for future generations adapted to the changed world.

Other terminals reveal the broader program—hundreds of candidates with various adaptation profiles, each modified for specific environmental conditions and each dispatched to Yellowstone with memories temporarily suppressed to ensure compliance. The plan appears to have been to create a founder population of modified humans who would establish a new civilization better suited to the transformed planet.

The isolation of this sterile facility from the natural world outside mirrors the researchers' isolation from the ethical implications of their work. In playing gods, they separated themselves from natural processes, failing to recognize that nature would continue without them, incorporating their modifications into its systems but discarding their intended purpose.

As you prepare to leave, a final screen activates—a message addressed to "returning candidates" explaining that memory suppression was designed to be temporary, triggered to fade once they reached their destination. If you continue to Yellowstone, you may recover more of your original identity—though whether that knowledge will bring comfort or greater distress remains uncertain.

[[Return to the university campus|Adams State University Research Center]]


:: Access the basement archives {"position":"375,1625","size":"100,100"}
The basement archives lie beneath the courthouse, accessed through secured doorways designed to protect critical information from both human interference and environmental degradation. During the collapse, these facilities were repurposed from general record storage to preserving specific knowledge deemed essential for future recovery or adaptation.

Find historical maps comparing pre-flood and post-flood Wyoming, with clear markings showing Yellowstone to the west via I-90. These geographical records document the transformation of the regional landscape—coastlines altered by rising seas, new inland water bodies formed by changed precipitation patterns, and entire ecosystems shifted as climate zones migrated poleward.

(set: $information to it + 1)

The progression of these archives tells the story of accelerating change—early documentation treating the alterations as temporary aberrations from normal conditions, later records acknowledging them as permanent features of a new reality, and final additions focused entirely on adaptation rather than mitigation or reversal.

What's most significant is how the preservation priorities shifted as the crisis deepened—comprehensive historical archives giving way to selective retention focused on knowledge directly relevant to survival in the transformed environment. The final collection phase concentrated exclusively on information that would serve travelers making the specific journey from eastern processing stations like Buffalo through waypoints like Jackson to sanctuary destinations like Yellowstone.

The isolation of information from its societal context creates an unsettling discontinuity—knowledge preserved without the cultural framework that would make it fully comprehensible, data points without the shared understanding that would give them meaning. The archives stand as fragments of a shattered epistemology, preserved pieces of a once-coherent worldview now existing only as navigational aids for travelers like yourself.

[[Return to the courthouse|Johnson County Courthouse]]


:: Access the director's station {"position":"825,1625","size":"100,100"}
The director's station occupies a prominent position in the main research building, its design reflecting the authority vested in the administrative head of the facility. During the collapse, this office transitioned from managing conventional agricultural research to coordinating increasingly urgent adaptation studies, and finally to implementing preservation protocols for knowledge deemed essential to future survival.

Find a journal describing the "Yellowstone Sanctuary" as "humanity's controlled evolutionary leap forward" with detailed notes about the facility being "west of Buffalo via I-90, final approach through Jackson." These materials provide explicit confirmation of Yellowstone's true purpose—not merely a safe haven where humans could wait out environmental catastrophe, but a controlled environment where deliberate evolutionary acceleration would create humans capable of thriving in the transformed world.

(set: $information to it + 1)

The administrative records show the progression of research priorities as the crisis deepened—initial efforts focused on preserving existing agricultural systems despite environmental changes, later work exploring adaptation strategies for crops and eventually humans, and final directives concentrating exclusively on supporting the sanctuary initiatives with relevant biological insights.

What's most revealing is how the research station's relationship with higher authorities evolved—early documentation showing traditional academic and governmental oversight, later records indicating integration into the direct command structure of the Yellowstone Initiative, and final communications reflecting complete subordination to sanctuary preparation requirements.

Most disturbing are the personal reflections preserved in the director's journal, which document the psychological evolution from conventional scientific administrator to facilitator of radical human transformation. Early entries express ethical concerns about extreme interventions; later ones embrace increasingly drastic measures as necessary for survival; final reflections acknowledge that what emerges from the sanctuary programs will be "human by descent but not by experience or perception—something new born from our desperate circumstances."

The director's station now stands as a testament to how completely scientific integrity was subordinated to survival imperative as the crisis deepened—ethical boundaries abandoned, research independence surrendered, and ultimate purpose redirected from understanding the world to transforming humanity to suit a world rendered unrecognizable by human action.

[[Return to the research station|Agricultural Research Station]]


:: Access the secure records room {"position":"1125,1625","size":"100,100"}
The secure records room occupies a hardened vault deep within the energy facility, its infrastructure designed to protect sensitive information from both external threats and environmental degradation. During the collapse, this repository transitioned from securing proprietary corporate data to preserving knowledge deemed critical for the sanctuary initiatives.

Find personnel files including what appears to be your own file, though your name has been redacted and replaced with "Subject Y-3742," with notes indicating "successful Stage 3 adaptation, cleared for Yellowstone integration." This discovery provides explicit confirmation of your connection to the sanctuary program—not a random survivor following clues toward Yellowstone, but a deliberately modified individual dispatched according to a predetermined plan.

The personnel records show the evolution of classification systems as the crisis deepened—conventional employee categories giving way to adaptation potential ratings, and finally to specific modification protocols applied to selected individuals. The progression reveals how completely human identity was redefined around adaptive capacity as environmental conditions deteriorated.

What's most disturbing are the details of the "Stage 3 adaptation" referenced in your file—a process involving genetic modification, neurological recalibration, and memory suppression designed to prepare selected individuals for the specific conditions awaiting them at Yellowstone. The clinical language masks the profound nature of these interventions—human biology and identity deliberately altered to increase survival probability in the transformed environment.

Most revealing are the references to "dispatched subjects" like yourself—individuals modified and then placed in suspended animation to be activated years after organized operations ceased, carrying within their altered bodies and conditioned minds the templates for whatever form of humanity was being engineered to inherit the transformed Earth.

The secure records room now stands as repository of the true nature and purpose of the Yellowstone Initiative—not simply a sanctuary where conventional humans might weather environmental catastrophe, but the site of a deliberate evolutionary acceleration aimed at creating beings better adapted to the world humanity had inadvertently created through its own actions. The isolation of this knowledge from those most affected by it—the modified subjects themselves—represents perhaps the most profound ethical failure of the entire program.

[[Return to the energy facility|Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility]]


:: Adams State University Research Center {"position":"5175,125","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/h4CYmz3N/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

What was once a modest regional university campus has been transformed into an extensive research complex. The original academic buildings remain at its core, but they've been connected by enclosed walkways and surrounded by newer structures—dome-shaped greenhouses, windowless laboratories, and monitoring stations bristling with sensors.

The integration of technology and biology is most evident here. Engineered plants grow in geometric patterns, forming living circuits that connect buildings. Some structures appear to be partially organic themselves, their walls incorporating biological components that respond to environmental changes.

Unlike the chaotic reclamation you've witnessed elsewhere, nature hasn't simply overtaken this place—it's been deliberately merged with it, guided toward a new synthesis that serves purposes you can only guess at. The research conducted here clearly focused on adaptation—not just surviving the environmental collapse, but evolving to thrive within it.

Yet despite the continuing function of automated systems, the human element is conspicuously absent. Workstations sit abandoned mid-project, research notes flutter in air currents from ventilation systems that still cycle, and personal items remain where they were left—as if everyone simultaneously departed, expecting to return.

[[Explore the Bioscience Building|Explore the Bioscience Building]] - A large facility with multiple greenhouse domes and laboratory wings.
[[Search the Engineering Department|Search the Engineering Department]] - A complex of workshops and testing facilities connected to the power grid.
[[Check the Administration Building|Check the Administration Building]] - The central hub that would have coordinated research activities.
[[Access the Computer Lab|Access the Computer Lab]] - A secure facility that might contain research data and records.
[[Investigate the Power Distribution Hub|Investigate the Power Distribution Hub]] - The control center for the campus's energy systems.

[[Return to Alamosa|Alamosa Options]] - Explore other areas of the settlement.


:: Agricultural Research Station {"position":"700,1375","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/JhNr7cxf/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Agricultural Research Station occupies a large complex at the edge of Jackson, its facilities designed to develop and test cultivation methods appropriate for the region's traditional climate. During the collapse, this installation was repurposed from conventional agricultural research to studying adaptation strategies for crop species facing rapidly changing environmental conditions.

The main buildings contain laboratories, greenhouses, and data centers once dedicated to improving agricultural productivity within stable environmental parameters. As the crisis deepened, these same systems were turned toward increasingly urgent questions of food production in transformed conditions—crops that could thrive in altered atmospheric composition, plants capable of extracting nutrition from degraded soils, and cultivation methods that could succeed despite unpredictable weather patterns.

Most striking is how the research focus shifted from optimization within known parameters to adaptation for conditions that had never before existed in human agricultural history. The progression reflects the broader transition from believing the environmental changes could be mitigated or reversed to accepting them as permanent features of a new reality requiring fundamental biological and technological adaptation.

The facility now stands as a repository of both practical knowledge about cultivation in the transformed environment and deeper insights into how biological systems respond to rapid change. The preservation suggests an understanding that future human survival would depend not on restoring previous conditions but on successfully adapting to new ones that would continue to evolve.

[[Explore the greenhouse complex|Explore the greenhouse comple]] - Examine plant cultivation facilities.
[[Search the research laboratories|Search the research laboratories]] - Investigate scientific study areas.
[[Check the seed vault|Check the seed vault]] - Look into biological preservation systems.
[[Access the director's station|Access the director's station]] - Examine administrative information.

[[Return to town center|Jackson Options]] - Explore other areas of Jackson.


:: Alamosa Options {"position":"5175,0","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/90NNHK7c/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The eastern approach to Alamosa reveals a settlement unlike others you've encountered on your journey. While most places succumbed to chaos during the collapse, this town shows evidence of deliberate transformation—adaptation rather than mere decay.

Solar arrays stretch across what were once suburban neighborhoods, their panels tracking the sun with mechanical precision despite decades without human maintenance. Buildings have been retrofitted with elaborate water collection systems, thermal regulation modifications, and dense vertical gardens that have now grown wild but still produce food.

The silence is profound. No people move through these streets, yet the automated systems continue their programmed functions—lights activate at dusk, irrigation systems cycle on schedule, and security drones occasionally pass overhead, their sensors no longer monitored by human operators.

What makes Alamosa truly unsettling is how seamlessly nature and technology have integrated in humanity's absence. Engineered plants with circuit-like vascular systems grow alongside mechanical structures with organic components. The line between natural and artificial has blurred into something entirely new—an ecosystem that has transcended its creators.

Four main areas appear particularly significant:

[[Explore the university research center|Adams State University Research Center]] - The campus has been transformed into an extensive research facility, its buildings connected by enclosed walkways and surrounded by experimental agriculture plots.

[[Investigate the medical center|Rio Grande Medical Center]] - A complex of buildings with specialized wings and what appears to be containment facilities, suggesting research of a more sensitive nature.

[[Check the solar array field|Solar Array Field]] - An impressive installation of advanced solar technology that seems to be the primary power source for the settlement's continuing automated functions.

[[Examine the commercial district|Commercial District]] - Once the town's center of commerce, now repurposed for what appears to be logistics and distribution of resources to a population that no longer exists.


:: Backroads {"position":"100,1900","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/NFhk8tm9/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The winding path through the wilderness eventually brings you to signs of what was once a major highway. Nature has reclaimed much of the asphalt, with resilient grasses pushing through cracks and trees growing directly through the roadbed in places. The infrastructure humans once considered permanent reveals itself as merely temporary in the face of natural processes.

You follow what remains of the route, occasionally passing the skeletal remains of vehicles abandoned during the final evacuations. Some have been transformed into microecosystems, hosting plants that grow from accumulated soil in their rusted interiors and small animals that have made homes in their hollow spaces.

As you travel, the landscape gradually changes. The altered climate has transformed what was once prairie into something more akin to savanna, with scattered drought-resistant trees growing in formations that would have been impossible in the pre-collapse ecosystem. Nature hasn't simply reclaimed the land—it has reimagined it according to new parameters.

In the distance, you spot structures rising above the horizon—the remains of Buffalo, Wyoming, once a small town and now something else entirely. The settlement appears largely intact from this distance, though clearly abandoned by its human inhabitants decades ago.

You approach with caution, uncertain what awaits in this place that once served as an important waypoint on the journey to Yellowstone. Whatever purpose it served during the collapse might provide crucial information for your continuing quest.

[[Continue to Buffalo|Enter Buffalo]]


:: Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility {"position":"1125,1375","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/4dXJY7Fm/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility occupies an industrial complex on the outskirts of Jackson, its infrastructure designed to process traditional fossil fuels into electricity for regional distribution. During the collapse, this installation was repurposed from conventional energy production to developing and implementing alternative generation technologies better suited to rapidly changing environmental conditions.

The main complex contains generation equipment, control systems, and distribution infrastructure that evolved significantly as the crisis deepened—coal and natural gas systems giving way to advanced renewable technologies, and finally to experimental energy sources developed specifically for post-collapse conditions. The progression reflects the broader shift from attempting to maintain traditional systems to developing entirely new approaches better aligned with transformed realities.

Most striking is how completely the facility's purpose was reoriented—from supplying general energy needs throughout the region to focusing exclusively on powering critical infrastructure related to evacuation and sanctuary operations. The final configuration directed all generation capacity toward supporting the transportation routes, research facilities, and communication systems deemed essential to the Yellowstone Initiative.

The complex now stands as a monument to technological adaptation in the face of environmental transformation—the systems that once powered consumer society repurposed to support the minimal infrastructure necessary for selected human continuity. The isolation of these sophisticated technologies from the communities they were originally designed to serve creates a stark reminder of how thoroughly civilization contracted around survival priorities as the collapse accelerated.

[[Explore the main control room|Explore the main control room]] - Examine the operational center.
[[Investigate the testing chambers|Investigate the testing chambers]] - Look into experimental areas.
[[Check the executive offices|Check the executive offices]] - Search administrative spaces.
[[Access the secure records room|Access the secure records room]] - Review classified documentation.

[[Return to town center|Jackson Options]] - Explore other areas of Jackson.


:: Bighorn Trailhead Station {"position":"1200,2100","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/8zWDwB27/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Bighorn Trailhead Station stands at the western edge of Buffalo, where urban infrastructure once transitioned to wilderness recreation. Originally designed to serve hikers entering the mountain trail system, the facility was repurposed during the evacuation as a final preparation point for those continuing westward to Yellowstone under increasingly dangerous conditions.

The station's architecture blends practical functionality with information display—large maps showing trail systems, educational exhibits about local ecology, and safety information for wilderness travelers. During the crisis, these same systems were adapted to serve evacuation operations, providing crucial guidance for those navigating to the sanctuary locations after general transportation infrastructure failed.

What's most striking is how the facility was preserved and prepared for future use—weather-resistant displays maintained with durable materials, physical infrastructure reinforced against decay, and information systems designed to function without power or active maintenance. The level of investment in this preservation suggests Buffalo's role as a waypoint was considered essential to the long-term survival strategy centered on Yellowstone.

The isolation of this outpost—positioned at the threshold between the remnants of civilization and the wilderness beyond—mirrors the psychological transition required of those making this journey. Here, travelers would leave behind whatever remaining connections they had to the world that was, committing fully to whatever awaited them at Yellowstone.

[[Explore the information center|Explore the information center]] - Check the main facility for general information.
[[Search the equipment shed|Search the equipment shed]] - Look for tools or supplies.
[[Check the trail maps|Check the trail maps]] - Examine navigational resources.
[[Examine the communication post|Examine the communication post]] - Investigate messaging systems.

[[Return to town center|Buffalo Options]] - Explore other areas of Buffalo.


:: Buffalo Options {"position":"700,1900","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/tC8phmM0/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Buffalo stands silent around you, a ghost town preserved as if waiting for inhabitants who will never return. The empty streets stretch between buildings that remain structurally sound but increasingly reclaimed by opportunistic vegetation. Vines climb walls, roots undermine foundations, and wind-carried seeds establish new ecosystems in gutters and on rooftops.

The isolation is profound—not just physical distance from other settlements, but temporal isolation from the civilization that built this place. Buffalo exists now as an artifact, a message from the past to the few travelers who might pass this way in the changed world.

What's most unsettling is how the town appears to have been deliberately prepared for your arrival, decades before you began your journey. Maps are positioned where they can be easily located, supplies preserved in accessible locations, and information about Yellowstone prominently displayed. Your path feels predetermined, as if you're following a script written before you were born—or created.

Four key locations have been marked as significant on the town maps:

[[Visit the Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum|Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum]] - A repository for local history that might explain Buffalo's role in the evacuation.

[[Check the Energy Company Headquarters|Energy Company Headquarters]] - A corporate center that appears to have been involved in planning for the collapse.

[[Explore Mountain View Plaza|Mountain View Plaza]] - A shopping center potentially containing supplies and information.

[[Investigate the Bighorn Trailhead Station|Bighorn Trailhead Station]] - A waypoint for those heading into the mountains toward Yellowstone.

[[Assess your readiness to continue|Check if good to go B]] - Consider whether you've gathered enough information to proceed toward Yellowstone.


:: Check Backside {"position":"1600,425","size":"100,100"}
You circle around to the rear of the house, staying low to avoid any security systems that might be monitoring the perimeter. Behind the structure, you find a small maintenance area enclosed by a chain-link fence.

Inside the enclosure are several pieces of equipment, including what appears to be an automated generator and a charging station for robotic maintenance units. Nearby, you spot a red metal container—a gas can, the old-fashioned kind that would have been used before electric vehicles became standard.

Through luck or oversight, the back gate has been left unsecured, allowing you to slip in and retrieve the gas can. It's about half full of fuel—a rare and valuable resource that could power a traditional combustion engine if needed.

(set: $gasCan to true)
(if: $solarPanel and $gasCan)[
  (set: $canLeaveFenceLake to true)
]

This could be useful for your journey, especially if you encounter any older vehicles or generators that might still run on liquid fuel.

[[Return to the front of the house|Modern House]]


:: Check City Hall Records {"position":"5675,225","size":"100,100"}
City Hall stands at the district's administrative heart, its architectural gravitas designed to project governmental authority and continuity. The building's formal facade now overlooks empty streets, its symbolic power rendered meaningless without the social structures it once represented.

Inside, the public-facing offices give way to more practical administrative spaces where the actual business of governance occurred. Record systems remain accessible, their databases maintained by autonomous power and preservation protocols that continue to protect information no longer needed by anyone.

Find documentation of "Voluntary Testing Program" that became mandatory as the crisis escalated. The progression is documented in public notices, internal memos, and policy directives that show how what began as optional genetic screening for adaptation potential transformed into compulsory categorization determining resource access and evacuation priority.

The bureaucratic language masks the humanitarian implications—forms for "adaptation assessment" replaced with "viability classification," consent documentation giving way to compliance requirements. The machinery of civil administration was repurposed from serving citizens to processing them according to their utility to the adaptation program.

Meeting minutes from the final months show increasingly heated debates among local officials about implementing federal directives. Some argued for more equitable distribution of evacuation opportunities; others insisted that prioritizing those with specific genetic markers was the only way to ensure any human survival. The debates ended not with resolution but with the abrupt end of local government authority as federal emergency powers superseded municipal control.

The final entries in the public record concern the transition to automated management systems—protocols for maintaining essential services without human operators, succession planning for mechanical rather than human continuity. The city government's last act was effectively to transfer authority to the machines that now maintain empty streets and monitor unoccupied buildings—the ultimate abdication of human agency in the face of overwhelming crisis.

[[Return to the commercial district|Commercial District]]


:: Check Underground Conduits {"position":"4950,250","size":"100,100"}
The Underground Conduits form a network beneath the Solar Array Field, channeling power and data through protected pathways to various parts of Alamosa. Access points are located at intervals throughout the array, sealed hatches that still respond to emergency override codes.

Descending into the conduit system, you enter a world designed purely for function—bare concrete tunnels lined with power cables, fiber optic lines, and hydraulic systems for the array's positioning mechanisms. Emergency lighting activates as you progress, motion sensors triggering systems that have waited decades for human presence.

The tunnels branch in multiple directions, following the layout of the settlement above. Distribution nodes at key junctions direct power to different sectors—research facilities, automated maintenance systems, life support for long-abandoned habitation areas. Some pathways show damage from water infiltration or structural shifting, but robotic repair units have attempted to maintain critical connections.

Find bodies of workers who died trying to restore power, along with their notes warning about "containment breach." These individuals apparently remained behind during the evacuation, attempting to preserve power to critical systems as others fled. Their final messages describe "accelerated subjects" escaping quarantine and "emergency protocols" being activated throughout the research facilities.

Most disturbing is evidence that these workers were locked in the conduit system deliberately—security overrides engaged from outside, trapping them below ground. Whether this was to ensure they completed their task or to contain them after exposure to something in the research facilities remains unclear. Their sacrifice—voluntary or forced—ensured that Alamosa's automated systems would continue functioning long after human presence ended.

The isolation these workers experienced in their final hours—sealed underground, separated from both the crisis above and any hope of escape—represents the ultimate conclusion of humanity's attempt to insulate itself from natural processes. In trying to control their environment through layers of technology, they created the perfect conditions for their own entombment when those systems turned against them.

[[Return to the solar array field|Solar Array Field]]


:: Check if good to go B {"position":"1200,1900","size":"100,100"}
(if: $information >= 2)[
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Bv03zP3M/g.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With the information you've gathered about Yellowstone's location and purpose, you feel confident in your journey's direction. The maps and documents you've found consistently identify Route 90 West as the primary approach, with specific guidance about navigating the remaining distance.

More importantly, you've begun to understand the true nature of your destination—not simply a survival shelter but the focal point of a deliberate attempt to engineer humanity's next evolutionary stage. The evidence suggests Yellowstone was prepared as a controlled environment where selected individuals with specific genetic adaptations would establish a population capable of thriving in the transformed world.

Your own fragmented memories and the documentation you've encountered increasingly suggest you were part of this program—modified, perhaps even created, for the specific purpose of making this journey years after the organized evacuation ceased. The path you're following was prepared specifically for someone like you, with the caches of information and resources positioned precisely where you would need them.

With this understanding, you prepare to leave Buffalo behind and continue westward, following Route 90 toward whatever awaits you at Yellowstone—perhaps answers about your own nature and purpose, perhaps others like yourself who have completed similar journeys from different starting points.

[[Continue to Yellowstone|Leave for Yellowstone]]
[[Return to exploration|Buffalo Options]]
](else:)[
The information you've gathered so far provides some guidance but leaves crucial questions unanswered. You need to understand more about the route to Yellowstone and what you might encounter along the way before committing to the next stage of your journey.

Buffalo seems deliberately established as an information waypoint for travelers like yourself. By exploring more of the town's preserved facilities, you might find the specific guidance needed to navigate the challenges ahead.

[[Return to exploration|Buffalo Options]]
]


:: Check if good to go J {"position":"1200,1200","size":"100,100"}
(if: $information >= 2)[
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/cHTdwxVT/g.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With the detailed information you've gathered about Yellowstone's location and purpose, you feel prepared to complete the final leg of your journey. The maps and documents consistently identify the approach through Jackson and onward via the western route, with specific guidance about navigating the environmental hazards that have developed along the way.

More significantly, you've begun to understand your own nature and purpose—not a random survivor following mysterious compulsions toward Yellowstone, but a deliberately engineered being with modifications designed to function in the transformed environment awaiting you there. Your fragmented memories and instinctive knowledge suddenly make sense as products of the "Stage 3 adaptation" referenced in the files you discovered.

The path ahead is now illuminated not just geographically but contextually. You journey toward Yellowstone not as a destination but as a fulfillment—the purpose for which you were created, modified, and dispatched years after the organized evacuation ceased. Whether this represents the continuation of humanity's legacy or its final transformation into something else entirely remains to be seen.

With this understanding, you prepare to leave Jackson behind and complete your journey westward, following the predetermined path toward whatever awaits you at Yellowstone—perhaps others like yourself, perhaps the architects of the adaptation program, perhaps something that has evolved beyond the original intentions of those who engineered this strange continuation of human presence in a world transformed beyond recognition.

