:: Check mailboxes {"position":"100,750","size":"100,100"}
The cluster of mailboxes stands like a memorial to a vanished way of life. Most are empty or contain nothing but dust, but a few still hold fragments of the past.

In one, you find a water bill with severe overcharge warnings and threats of supply termination. Another contains a government notice about "mandatory genetic screening for adaptation potential"—whatever that means.

But the most disturbing find comes from a mailbox with its flag still raised. Inside is a citizen ID card with a photo that unmistakably shows your face. The name listed—Alex Harmon—feels completely unfamiliar, yet the biometric data and photo are undeniably yours.

The ID designates you as "Adaptation Category 3" with an authorization stamp for "Yellowstone Initiative Phase 2." A barcode at the bottom is labeled "Subject Y-3742."

This raises troubling questions. Why don't you remember this identity? What was the Yellowstone Initiative? And what exactly were you "adapted" for?

You pocket the ID, hoping answers await at your destination.

[[Continue exploring the residential area |Residential Area]]


:: Check office area {"position":"2275,200","size":"100,100"}
The office area consists of a desk, filing cabinets, and a small break room. Paperwork is scattered everywhere, much of it weathered beyond legibility. The filing cabinets have been pulled open, their contents hastily searched at some point in the past.

On the desk, you find a clipboard with invoices for government contracts to retrofit vehicles for "Phase 3 Highland Evacuation." The documents bear official seals and authorization codes, showing this facility was officially commissioned to prepare transport for the final stages of organized evacuation.

One memo stands out—a priority order for preparing the Tacoma for "Yellowstone Sanctuary Personnel Transport." The instructions specify exacting standards for the vehicle's modifications, far beyond what would be needed for ordinary travel. Notes in the margins indicate someone was planning to use the truck themselves rather than delivering it to the authorities.

A calendar on the wall is marked with increasingly urgent deadlines, the final entry reading "LAST TRANSPORT NORTH - 0600." Whether that transport ever departed remains unclear.

[[Return to the mechanic shop|Abandoned Mechanic Shop]]


:: Check on ATV {"position":"1425,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/260ZHfs8/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You return to your ATV to assess its condition and your readiness for the next stage of your journey. The vehicle has performed well so far, but the battery indicator shows less than 30% charge remaining—not enough to reach Gallup, your next destination according to the map.

You examine the map, plotting the route ahead. The terrain between here and Gallup appears challenging, with areas marked as "unstable" and "possible radiation hazard." Having reliable transportation will be essential.

(if: $solarPanel)[
The solar panel you found at the abandoned farm is a significant upgrade from the charging unit you recovered in Eagar. It should provide more efficient charging for the ATV's battery systems.
]

(if: $gasCan)[
The gas can from behind the modern house could prove valuable. While your ATV is electric, having traditional fuel might be useful for other vehicles or generators you encounter.
]

(if: $canLeaveFenceLake)[
With both the solar panel and gas can secured, you feel adequately prepared to continue your journey. The improved solar charging capability will keep your ATV powered, and the fuel provides a backup resource for emergencies.

[[Prepare to leave Fence Lake|Leave Fence Lake]]
](else:)[
You still need to explore more of Fence Lake to find resources for your journey. The ATV needs better power solutions before you can safely continue to Gallup.

[[Return to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Options]]
]


:: Check random room {"position":"2175,75","size":"100,100"}
You try several of the room doors until you find one unlocked. Room 114 appears to have been occupied right up until the final evacuation. Personal belongings remain scattered about—clothing, toiletries, a family photo in a cracked frame.

What catches your attention is a set of work clothes bearing the logo "Rio Puerco Auto" with the name "Martinez" embroidered on the chest. A small desk is covered with technical diagrams and notes about vehicle modifications for "highland evacuation protocols."

In the nightstand drawer, partially hidden under a battered maintenance manual, you find a key fob for a Toyota vehicle. This could be the key to the Tacoma you spotted in the mechanic shop.

(set: $tacomaKeys to true)
(if: $tacomaKeys and $rested)[
  (set: $canLeaveGallup to true)
]

You pocket the key, suspecting it could be your ticket out of Gallup.

[[Return to the motel lobby|Desert Sands Motel]]


:: Check storage area {"position":"550,750","size":"100,100"}
The facility's storage area contains shelves of maintenance equipment, spare parts, and survival supplies. While many items have degraded over time, some sealed containers have protected their contents from the elements.

You find a cache of water purification tablets—essential for making contaminated water safe to drink during your journey. These could be the difference between life and death in the harsh conditions you'll face.

There's also a waterproof container with emergency rations, designed for long-term storage. Though well past their technical expiration date, they're sealed in a way that suggests they might still be safe to consume if necessary.

In a locker marked "Emergency Equipment," you discover a high-capacity water container with filtration capabilities built into the cap. This would allow you to carry enough water for several days while automatically purifying new sources you encounter.

You carefully pack these valuable supplies, knowing they'll be crucial for surviving the journey ahead.

[[Continue exploring the facility|Water Collection Facility]]


:: Check the Administration Building {"position":"5125,275","size":"100,100"}
The Administration Building stands at the center of campus, its modernist architecture designed to project authority and order. Now it presides over emptiness, its carefully maintained appearance a facade with no audience. Solar-powered lights still illuminate the lobby on schedule, and climate control systems maintain a perfect temperature for administrators who will never return.

Inside, you find offices arranged in a hierarchical pattern—lower-level staff in open areas, department heads in private offices, and executive leadership occupying the top floor with its panoramic views of the campus and mountains beyond. The physical manifestation of organizational structure remains, though its purpose has vanished.

In the records department, you discover files indicating Alamosa was designated as "Observation Zone 7" for monitoring genetic changes in flood survivors. The documentation reveals a systematic program of testing, categorization, and selection that determined individuals' treatment and eventual disposition. Some were designated for "sanctuary placement," others for "continued observation," and some ominously marked for "resource allocation only"—bureaucratic language disguising life-or-death decisions.

Most disturbing are the criteria for these categorizations—not just medical viability or needed skills, but genetic markers for "adaptation potential." The final phase of human society wasn't governed by democratic principles or even military necessity, but by a form of artificial selection directed by scientists and administrators who believed they were salvaging humanity's future by controlling its evolution.

Personal logs from administrators show the psychological toll of implementing these protocols. Some expressed doubt, others conviction, but all ultimately participated in a system that decided which humans were worth preserving and which were expendable. The isolation of their position—making godlike decisions from the remove of executive offices—mirrors your own isolation now, wandering through the artifacts of their moral compromise.

Nature has begun reclaiming even this carefully maintained building. A leak in the roof has allowed moisture to penetrate, and an engineered fungal species has established itself in the boardroom, its mycelium network spreading through the wood of the conference table where the final decisions about human worth were likely made.

[[Return to the university campus|Adams State University Research Center]]


:: Check the Quarantine Wards {"position":"5375,250","size":"100,100"}
The Quarantine Wards occupy an isolated wing accessible only through multiple airlocks and decontamination chambers. Warning signs indicate these areas contained individuals with "unstable adaptations" or "unpredictable mutation vectors"—clinical language disguising the reality that these rooms held people whose bodies were changing in ways beyond the researchers' control.

Unlike other sections of the facility, these wards show signs of struggle—damaged restraints, scratched walls, barricades erected and then broken. Whatever occurred here in the final days, it was not peaceful. Emergency protocols appear to have been activated, with containment doors sealed and environmental systems modified to suppress certain activities.

In the observation rooms overlooking the wards, you find personal journals from patients describing accelerated evolution—some developing gill-like structures to process oxygen more efficiently, others with enhanced lung capacity that allowed them to enter high-altitude areas that would normally cause unconsciousness. The changes were physical but also neurological, with some reporting altered perception and cognition.

The most disturbing entries describe how these adaptations began to spread—not through infection but through proximity, as if the changed individuals were emitting something that catalyzed similar changes in others. This phenomenon appears to have been what prompted the emergency containment measures—fear that the adaptations would spread beyond control.

The quarantine wards embody the ultimate failure of attempted isolation from natural processes. Humans tried to control evolution within sealed environments, only to discover that nature's adaptive mechanisms functioned at levels they couldn't isolate or control. The barriers between controlled experiment and uncontrolled reality proved as permeable as those between human and environment.

Now those barriers have disappeared entirely. The specialized filtration systems have failed, allowing free exchange between the quarantine wards and the outside world. Whatever was being contained here has long since merged with the broader ecosystem—becoming part of the new natural order that emerged after human departure.

[[Return to the medical center|Rio Grande Medical Center]]


:: Check the Truck {"position":"3825,650","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/vBwspgd0/g.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You return to your Toyota Tacoma to assess what repairs are needed before continuing your journey north. The truck has performed admirably so far, but mountain passes lie ahead, and you can't risk a breakdown in such isolated territory with no hope of rescue.

After a thorough inspection, you identify several issues that need addressing: the specialized filtration system shows signs of degradation, the cooling system requires reinforcement for high-altitude operation, and several hoses need replacement to prevent failure in extreme conditions.

(if: $canRepairTruck)[
You have gathered all the necessary components to repair the Tacoma:
- The filtration system from the museum
- Engineering components from the locomotive
- Specialized tools from the hardware store

With these items, you can complete the needed repairs and continue your journey with confidence that the vehicle will handle the challenges ahead.

[[Begin repairs|Repair Truck]]
](else:)[
You still need to find components to repair the Tacoma before proceeding. Based on your assessment, you should look for:
- A filtration system compatible with the Tacoma's modified air intake
- Engineering components to reinforce the cooling system
- Specialized tools for working with the truck's specialized systems

These items might be found somewhere in Durango if you continue exploring.

[[Return to town|Durango Options]]
]


:: Check the archaeological displays {"position":"2050,675","size":"100,100"}
The archaeological section features artifacts from ancient Anasazi (Ancestral Puebloan) sites throughout the region. Tools, pottery, and remnants of dwellings tell the story of a civilization that thrived in an arid environment for centuries before mysteriously abandoning their settlements.

Recent research displayed alongside the artifacts draws parallels between the Ancestral Puebloans' experience and the modern climate crisis. New evidence suggests their civilization faced dramatic climate shifts that made their traditional agriculture increasingly difficult, forcing adaptation and eventually migration.

One exhibit specifically highlights how these ancient sites show evidence of surviving previous climate catastrophes through adaptation and careful resource management. Advanced analysis of soil and pollen samples revealed periods of drought and environmental change that mirror more recent disruptions, albeit on a smaller scale.

A research paper, apparently added to the exhibit during the early crisis years, suggests that studying these ancient adaptation strategies could provide valuable insights for surviving the modern collapse. Highlighted passages describe how certain dwelling designs and water management techniques were being adapted for contemporary use in "highland sanctuary sites."

[[Return to the museum|Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center]]


:: Check the bookstore {"position":"750,2225","size":"100,100"}
The bookstore occupies a corner space in the plaza, its large windows now covered with preservation film that has protected the interior from decades of sun exposure. Unlike many shops that were completely repurposed during the evacuation, this one retains much of its original function—books carefully preserved as repositories of knowledge that might prove valuable long after digital systems failed.

Find a travel guide with a highlighted route from Buffalo to Yellowstone via westbound Route 90. The book has been deliberately placed in a prominent location, its pages annotated with updated information reflecting post-collapse conditions along the journey. Someone clearly intended this resource to be found by travelers like yourself, following this path years after those who prepared it had departed.

(set: $information to it + 1)

The selection of preserved books reveals careful curation—practical knowledge prioritized over entertainment, technical manuals over fiction, regional information over general interest. The collection was clearly assembled to serve the needs of future travelers and potential settlers, with emphasis on survival skills, local geography, and adaptable technology.

What's most striking is how this preservation effort acknowledges the fragility of digital information storage. In returning to physical books as the most durable medium for transmitting crucial knowledge across decades without maintenance, those who prepared this place demonstrated humility regarding humanity's technological systems—recognizing that the most advanced solutions were also the most vulnerable to disruption.

A logbook near the entrance contains entries from the final staff before evacuation, documenting their preservation efforts. The last entry reads: "We've secured the most essential volumes according to protocol. Knowledge that might serve those who come after us, when the digital archives have failed and the experts are gone. If you're reading this, traveler, take what you need from these shelves. This knowledge wasn't preserved for ownership but for use."

[[Return to the plaza|Mountain View Plaza]]


:: Check the executive offices {"position":"1250,1500","size":"100,100"}
The executive offices occupy the upper level of the main facility building, their design projecting the corporate authority once vested in the energy company's leadership. During the collapse, these spaces were repurposed from business management to crisis response coordination, and finally to supporting the specific operational requirements of the Yellowstone Initiative.

Find correspondence between energy executives and "Yellowstone Command" about "powering the sanctuary infrastructure." These communications reveal the facility's evolution from conventional energy provider to critical component in the sanctuary system—its generation capacity increasingly dedicated to supporting the transportation routes, research facilities, and life support systems deemed essential to the Yellowstone program.

The documents show a progression of priorities as the crisis deepened—early communications focused on maintaining general power distribution despite resource constraints, later correspondence addressing specific sanctuary infrastructure requirements, and final directives concentrating exclusively on supporting critical systems related to the Yellowstone Initiative.

What's most revealing is how completely the corporate leadership integrated into the sanctuary command structure—early exchanges maintaining traditional business-government relationships, later ones reflecting increased subordination to emergency authorities, and final communications showing complete incorporation into the Yellowstone Initiative's hierarchical structure.

Most disturbing are the records detailing how executives leveraged their positions to secure sanctuary placement for themselves and selected employees—corporate resources and expertise exchanged for survival opportunity, with admission priority determined by perceived utility to the sanctuary program rather than broader humanitarian considerations.

The executive offices now stand as testament to how traditional power structures were simultaneously preserved and transformed during the collapse—the hierarchical organization maintained while its purpose shifted from profit generation to survival prioritization. The isolation of leadership from those they once employed and served created a microcosm of the broader societal fragmentation, with decision-makers increasingly detached from the consequences of their choices for those deemed expendable.

[[Return to the energy facility|Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility]]


:: Check the garage area {"position":"775,350","size":"100,100"}
The garage area is strewn with tools and vehicle parts, most broken or rusted beyond use. Sunlight streams through holes in the roof, illuminating swirling dust particles. As you carefully step through the mess, something catches your eye in the back corner.

It's an old electric ATV, a model designed after fossil fuels became too scarce for everyday use. Its battery is dead, and it's missing what appears to be an ignition chip—a security measure from the late resource wars to prevent theft.

The vehicle looks otherwise intact, its raised suspension and reinforced frame designed for the harsh post-flood terrain. 

(if: $ignitionChip)[You have the ignition chip that would fit this ATV.] (else:)[The ATV needs an ignition chip to operate its security systems.]

(if: $solarChargingUnit)[You have a solar charging unit that could recharge the ATV's battery.] (else:)[You'll need to find a power source to charge the dead battery.]

(if: $map)[With your map, you could navigate to the next destination once the ATV is operational.] (else:)[Without a map, you'd be traveling blind even if you got the ATV working.]

(if: $canLeaveEagar)[
  With all the needed components, you could repair this ATV and continue your journey.
  
  [[Repair the ATV | Leave Eagar]] - Use your collected items to get the ATV working.
]

[[Continue exploring the gas station |Abandoned Gas Station]]


:: Check the historical maps collection {"position":"150,2225","size":"100,100"}
The museum's cartography section contains an impressive collection of maps spanning the region's documented history. Early hand-drawn surveys give way to increasingly precise topographical renderings, culminating in the final maps created specifically for directing travelers to Yellowstone after general evacuation ceased.

You discover a preserved Wyoming highway map showing Route 90's path, with Yellowstone clearly marked west of Buffalo. Unlike standard road maps, this one has been annotated with information vital for post-collapse travel—areas of environmental degradation to avoid, radiation zones from damaged infrastructure, and critical resource points where water and supplies might still be found.

(set: $information to it + 1)

The most significant map is a sealed, laminated guide clearly intended to survive decades of storage. It shows multiple routes to Yellowstone, with detailed notes on terrain challenges, seasonal variations, and alternative paths should primary routes be compromised. The level of detail suggests it was created with the specific expectation that individuals would be making this journey long after support infrastructure had failed.

What's most revealing is how the landscape itself is depicted as in transition—areas marked with progressive dates showing how forest boundaries shifted, new water bodies formed, and habitable zones contracted as climate patterns altered. The map doesn't present a static geography but a projection of continuing change, with Yellowstone represented as a rare point of relative stability in a world of accelerating transformation.

In the margins, handwritten notes from the final cartographers express concerns about the accuracy of their long-term projections. One poignant comment reads: "Beyond 2080, confidence in route viability decreases to 40%. Travelers will need to adapt to conditions we cannot reliably predict." The humility of this admission stands in stark contrast to the civilization that believed it could engineer and control the natural world indefinitely.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum]]


:: Check the hotel restaurant {"position":"4300,250","size":"100,100"}
The hotel's restaurant space has been thoroughly ransacked, its pantries emptied during the initial crisis. What remains are the heavy appliances and fixtures too cumbersome to carry away—silent monuments to daily routines now extinct.

Yet in a locked storeroom you manage to force open, you discover a cache of preserved food supplies that somehow escaped previous scavenging. These military-grade rations were apparently stored here during the evacuation period when the hotel served as temporary housing for those waiting for transport.

The packaging promises a hundred-year shelf life, and while that might be optimistic, the vacuum seals appear intact. This could sustain you for the remaining journey to Yellowstone—a small but significant discovery.

What strikes you is how the restaurant's elegant design—once focused on presentation and atmosphere—became irrelevant the moment survival took precedence. The white tablecloths rotted away while utilitarian food stores remained viable. Form followed function only until function became paramount.

Through gaps in the boarded windows, you notice how plants have established themselves on the restaurant's outdoor terrace. The carefully curated garden space once maintained for human aesthetic appreciation now supports a wild ecosystem with greater biodiversity than its designed predecessor ever contained.

The natural world didn't end when human management ceased; it simply returned to its own priorities. The earth continues its cycles of growth and decay, regeneration and adaptation, regardless of whether humans remain to impose their definitions of purpose or value.

[[Return to the hotel entrance|The Strater Hotel]]


:: Check the maintenance shed {"position":"3600,225","size":"100,100"}
The maintenance shed door is secured with a padlock that matches the key you found in the Strater Hotel. If you don't have the key, you'll need to find it before accessing this area.

(if: $maintenanceShedKey)[
You unlock the padlock and enter the maintenance shed—a practical workspace once used to service the historic locomotives. Unlike the tourist-facing areas of the railroad, this utilitarian space feels honest in its purpose, focused on the actual work of keeping machines operational rather than romanticizing them.

Inside, you find specialized tools and spare parts for steam locomotives—technology that was already antiquated before the collapse but maintained for historical value and tourism. Ironically, these older, simpler technologies remained serviceable longer than many more "advanced" systems when supply chains failed.

On a workbench, you discover something unexpected—a recently updated map of the changed continent, far more detailed than any you've seen previously. It shows the dramatic coastal alterations from rising seas, new inland water bodies from altered precipitation patterns, and radiation zones from damaged nuclear facilities.

The map confirms your route to Yellowstone while providing crucial information about hazards to avoid. Most significantly, it marks several "Sanctuary Zones" besides Yellowstone, suggesting that multiple adaptation facilities were established in different regions.

This map's presence here suggests someone was using this maintenance shed relatively recently—perhaps within the past few years—as a stopping point on journeys between these sanctuary zones.

(set: $detailedMap to true)
](else:)[
The maintenance shed door is secured with a sturdy padlock. You'll need to find the key before you can access this area, likely somewhere in town.

[[Return to the railroad depot|Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot]]
]


:: Check the mapping center {"position":"375,2225","size":"100,100"}
The mapping center occupies what was once the company's geographic information systems department, its space filled with sophisticated modeling equipment designed to locate and track resources for extraction. During the crisis, these same systems were repurposed to monitor the accelerating environmental changes and identify remaining viable habitats.

Find detailed topographical surveys showing elevated radiation readings in the Yellowstone caldera following the climate collapse. The data reveals how Yellowstone's unique geothermal properties created environmental conditions that remained relatively stable despite catastrophic changes elsewhere, making it ideal for long-term human habitation in a transformed world.

(set: $information to it + 1)

Multiple monitoring stations tracked the progression of environmental degradation across the continent, with time-lapse projections showing habitable zones contracting toward highland areas like Yellowstone. The precision of these models suggests extensive data collection continued even as general infrastructure failed, with this facility serving as a critical analysis center until final evacuation.

What's most unsettling is how these same mapping technologies that once facilitated resource exploitation—pinpointing optimal drilling locations and extraction routes—were turned toward identifying which human populations could be preserved and which abandoned. The system's cold efficiency functioned equally well for both purposes, calculating maximum yield whether in barrels of oil or human genetic diversity.

The final mapping projects focused on optimization of sanctuary capacity—models determining the minimum viable population necessary to preserve human civilization, calculations of sustainable resource usage in isolated habitats, and projections of adaptation requirements for long-term survival in altered environments. The sophisticated technology that once served quarterly profits was ultimately applied to calculating humanity's constricted future.

[[Return to the company headquarters|Energy Company Headquarters]]


:: Check the mechanical room {"position":"4275,525","size":"100,100"}
The museum's mechanical room housed the systems that maintained the carefully controlled environment necessary for preserving historical artifacts. Unlike the exhibit spaces designed for public viewing, this utilitarian area shows the practical infrastructure that made the museum's cultural function possible.

Most systems have long since failed, but the room contains valuable components that have been protected from exposure and random scavenging. Among these, you find vehicle parts including a filtration system compatible with your Tacoma—a crucial component for ensuring your vehicle can handle the challenging environmental conditions ahead.

(set: $filtrationSystem to true)
(if: $filtrationTools and $engineeringParts and $filtrationSystem)[
  (set: $canRepairTruck to true)
]

As you carefully remove the component, you reflect on the layers of dependency that defined modern civilization. This museum required complex mechanical systems to preserve artifacts from simpler times when humans lived more directly with their environment. Those earlier mountain dwellers would likely have navigated this changed world with more innate resilience than those who came to depend on the very technologies that ultimately destabilized the climate.

The filtration system itself represents this paradox—technology developed to mitigate problems caused by earlier technologies. Each solution became the foundation for new problems requiring more elaborate solutions, accelerating until the complexity could no longer be maintained.

Now you salvage pieces of this collapsed system to continue your own journey—part of the great simplification forced upon the human project by ecological limits it refused to acknowledge until too late.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Museum of Mountain Living]]


:: Check the old hardware store {"position":"3950,250","size":"100,100"}
The hardware store's entrance is blocked by fallen debris, but a side window provides access. Inside, the space feels like a time capsule—tools and supplies arranged as if waiting for customers who will never return. The apocalypse didn't announce itself with dramatic fanfare; it simply meant the end of routine commerce like this.

Most common tools were taken during the initial crisis, but specialized equipment remains on the dusty shelves. You find yourself drawn to a section dedicated to irrigation and water management—tools designed for a world that was already changing before the final collapse.

In a locked cabinet that you manage to pry open, you discover specialized tools needed for filtration system repairs—exactly what your Tacoma requires. These precision instruments were likely too specific for general survival needs, explaining why they weren't taken during earlier scavenging.

(set: $filtrationTools to true)
(if: $filtrationTools and $engineeringParts)[
  (set: $canRepairTruck to true)
]

As you collect these tools, you notice something poignant—a calendar on the wall, stopped forever at October 2088, the month when regular deliveries ceased. Scrawled notes about order requests and customer pickups tell the story of normal life continuing right up until it couldn't anymore.

The quiet isolation of this space underscores how quickly human absence allows for transformation. Water damage from decades of neglected roof maintenance has created small ecosystems in the corners of the store. Insects and small mammals have made homes among products once considered essential to civilization. The collapse wasn't the end of life—only the end of one particular way of living.

[[Return to Main Avenue|Main Avenue]]


:: Check the pharmacy area {"position":"2075,550","size":"100,100"}
The pharmacy section has been thoroughly ransacked, with empty medication bottles scattered across the floor. Most valuable pharmaceuticals were likely taken during the initial crisis or in the years that followed.

However, in a locked cabinet you manage to force open, you find a cache of specialized medications that were apparently overlooked—treatments for conditions that weren't immediate survival priorities.

Among the supplies are packages labeled "Respiratory Adaptation Support" and documents about "Flood-Related Respiratory Conditions." The materials describe how changing atmospheric conditions following the climate collapse led to respiratory challenges for many survivors.

One document outlines "Environmental Adaptation Syndrome"—apparently some humans began developing physiological changes in response to altered environmental conditions. The medical literature describes these changes as "spontaneous adaptive mutations," though notes debate about whether they were truly spontaneous or triggered by deliberate interventions.

You gather some of the medications, which might prove useful given the uncertain atmospheric conditions you'll face on your journey.

[[Return to the warehouse|Costco Warehouse]]


:: Check the seed vault {"position":"700,1625","size":"100,100"}
The seed vault occupies a hardened facility beneath the main research complex, its infrastructure designed to preserve biological materials through extreme conditions and extended timeframes. During the collapse, this facility was repurposed from agricultural continuity insurance to serving as a biological archive for species threatened with extinction by rapidly changing environmental conditions.

Find preserved genetic material from pre-flood species alongside "stabilized adaptations" developed after. The collection spans the spectrum from conventional seed preservation to sophisticated genetic storage techniques maintaining the DNA of organisms that could no longer survive in the altered environment but might be reconstituted under suitable conditions.

The preservation priorities show significant evolution as the crisis deepened—early efforts focused on maintaining agricultural biodiversity for eventual recovery, later ones acknowledging that many preserved species could never again exist in their original forms, and final additions concentrated on archiving genetic information that might inform adaptation rather than restoration.

What's most revealing is how the vault's purpose transformed from preservation for recovery to preservation for reference—the genetic material maintained not with the expectation it would be directly used but that the information it contained might guide the development of new organisms better suited to the altered conditions.

Most significant are the final additions to the vault—specimens of successfully adapted organisms representing the various evolutionary pathways that emerged in response to environmental transformation. These "stabilized adaptations" include not only naturally evolved variants but engineered organisms created through the research station's experimental programs, preserved as templates for the biological systems deemed viable in the changed world.

The seed vault now stands as an ark of genetic memory, maintaining the information necessary to understand what was lost while simultaneously preserving the adaptations that point toward possible futures. The isolation of this genetic heritage from both its original environmental context and the human systems that gave it meaning creates a poignant monument to biological continuity transcending human continuity—life persisting in altered forms despite the collapse of the civilization that documented its transformations.

[[Return to the research station|Agricultural Research Station]]


:: Check the sheriff's department {"position":"250,1500","size":"100,100"}
The sheriff's department occupies a secure wing of the courthouse, its facilities designed for law enforcement operations across the county. During the collapse, this section was repurposed from civilian policing to supporting evacuation security and resource protection as social systems destabilized.

You find usual law enforcement supplies, but nothing of particular importance to your journey. The equipment shows signs of having been selectively preserved—critical resources maintained while non-essential items were allowed to deteriorate or were repurposed for other functions. The pattern suggests deliberate decisions about what aspects of traditional law enforcement would remain relevant in a post-collapse context.

What's most revealing is how security priorities shifted as the crisis deepened—early directives focused on maintaining public order during evacuation, later ones addressing resource protection and evacuation route security, and final protocols concerning only the preservation of specific assets deemed critical to sanctuary operations.

The most recent documentation focuses on securing the waypoint facilities themselves—protocols for preventing unauthorized access to preserved information, protecting cached resources from scavenging, and maintaining the physical infrastructure necessary for future travelers. The precision suggests these measures were implemented with clear understanding that the social context for traditional law enforcement would no longer exist.

The isolation of law enforcement infrastructure from the communities it was designed to protect creates a stark reminder of how quickly social contracts dissolved when environmental stability failed. The systems designed to maintain order within human society became irrelevant when that society itself dispersed, leaving behind only the strategic assets deemed necessary for whatever would emerge from the sanctuary initiatives.

[[Return to the courthouse|Johnson County Courthouse]]


:: Check the trail maps {"position":"1150,2225","size":"100,100"}
The trail map display occupies a central position in the trailhead facility, its weather-resistant construction designed to provide reliable navigational information in all conditions. During the evacuation, these mapping resources were repurposed from recreational guidance to critical route information for those continuing to sanctuary locations after conventional transportation systems failed.

Locate a marked emergency evacuation route showing Route 90 West as the primary path to Yellowstone. The map has been extensively annotated with information specific to post-collapse conditions—areas of environmental hazard marked and dated, alternative routes suggested where primary paths might be compromised, and resource caches indicated at strategic intervals.

(set: $information to it + 1)

The evolution of these maps tells the story of rapidly changing landscape conditions—early versions showing minor detours around damaged infrastructure, later ones depicting wholesale rerouting around regions rendered uninhabitable by environmental changes. The progressive adaptation of recommended routes reflects the accelerating transformation of the physical geography as climate systems destabilized.

What's most striking is how these maps increasingly incorporate biological information alongside geographical data—regions marked according to observed mutations in local wildlife, areas categorized by adaptation requirements for human traversal, and locations identified where specific physiological challenges would be encountered. The integration reflects the recognition that geography and biology had become inseparable considerations in the transformed world.

The final mapping products focus specifically on optimizing the journey to Yellowstone for individuals with particular adaptive capabilities—routes tailored to those with the physical modifications believed necessary for long-term survival in the sanctuary environment. The specificity suggests these resources were designed not for general evacuation but for selected individuals who would follow this path after most organized movement had ceased.

[[Return to the trailhead station|Bighorn Trailhead Station]]


:: Check undelivered mail {"position":"1200,700","size":"100,100"}
You sift through piles of abandoned mail on the sorting tables. Most are deteriorated beyond legibility, but some sealed packages and official envelopes have preserved their contents.

Among them, you find several government notices addressed to local residents. Many bear stark warnings about the "Salt Water Contamination Crisis" affecting groundwater across the region. Some include evacuation orders with routes to "Designated Inland Sanctuaries."

One sealed envelope contains classified documents—apparently delivered after most postal workers had already evacuated. The papers detail disrupted ocean currents resulting from accelerated polar ice melt, with projections showing catastrophic climate shifts beyond those publicly acknowledged.

A map included with the documents shows revised habitability projections, with the Yellowstone region marked as "Adaptation Zone Alpha"—one of very few areas expected to remain viable without "Enhanced Survival Interventions."

This confirms that your destination wasn't randomly chosen—Yellowstone represents one of humanity's last viable habitats in North America.

[[Return to the main post office area|USPS]]


:: Commercial District {"position":"5800,100","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/gkQBS9pD/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Commercial District once formed Alamosa's economic center—shops, offices, and service businesses arranged along a main thoroughfare designed for commerce and community interaction. Now these spaces stand repurposed, transformed first by desperate human adaptation to crisis and then by decades of automated functioning without human presence.

Retail spaces have been converted to distribution centers, their shelving systems optimized for robotic retrieval rather than customer browsing. Office buildings house massive server farms, their computational power directed toward managing the settlement's continuing automated processes. Financial institutions have become data archives, storing the accumulated knowledge of a civilization that no longer exists to access it.

Most striking is the transition from spaces designed for human comfort and aesthetic appeal to pure functionality. Decorative elements have been removed to make way for more efficient use of space; climate control systems maintain conditions optimal for equipment rather than people; lighting operates on spectrums suited for machine vision rather than human perception.

The district has effectively been redesigned for its new inhabitants—not new human occupants, but the autonomous systems that continue to operate according to their programming. The spaces have been optimized for entities that don't require comfort, beauty, or meaning—only efficient function.

[[Explore the Supply Depot|Explore the Supply Depot]] - A centralized facility for resource management.
[[Search the Communications Center|Search the Communications Center]] - The hub for information distribution within Alamosa.
[[Check City Hall Records|Check City Hall Records]] - Administrative offices containing policy documentation.
[[Investigate the Transport Terminal|Investigate the Transport Terminal]] - The facility that once connected Alamosa to other settlements.
[[Access the Emergency Bunker|Access Emergency Bunker]] - A secure facility designed for crisis management.

[[Return to Alamosa|Alamosa Options]] - Explore other areas of the settlement.


:: Continue Entering {"position":"4225,375","size":"100,100"}
You cautiously advance toward the bathroom, curious about what might be living in these abandoned spaces. As you cross the threshold, the floor beneath you gives way—decades of water damage have weakened the structural supports beyond their breaking point.

You fall through splintering wood, landing hard in a tangled heap of debris one floor below. Pain shoots through your leg, and you realize you're trapped beneath a fallen beam. The weight is too great to shift alone.

As daylight fades to dusk and then night, your calls for help echo unanswered through the empty hotel. In the darkness, you hear small creatures scurrying closer, drawn by the scent of blood from your injuries.

The isolation that felt contemplative earlier now reveals its deadly nature. In this collapsed world, there is no rescue coming, no emergency services to call. The hotel that sheltered thousands in its history now becomes your final resting place—one more human life reclaimed by the patient processes of an indifferent natural world.

[[Game Over]]


:: Costco Warehouse {"position":"1900,525","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/mZcYdPgv/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The massive Costco Warehouse looms at the edge of what was once a commercial district. Its reinforced construction has weathered the decades better than surrounding buildings. Heavy security doors appear to have been forced open long ago, allowing access to the cavernous interior.

Inside, shelving units stretch into the distance, mostly empty but with occasional items remaining—those too heavy to easily transport or not immediately useful for survival. The building shows signs of having served as a distribution center during the crisis years, with government markings on some areas and evidence of organized inventory systems.

Sunlight streams through skylights, illuminating dust particles floating in the air. The warehouse is divided into sections, with the pharmacy area, food storage, and fuel center still distinguishable despite years of abandonment.

[[Check the fuel center|Explore the fuel center]] - Look for fuel or equipment that might help with your journey.
[[Search the pharmacy area|Check the pharmacy area]] - Look for medical supplies or information.
[[Examine the food storage section|Investigate the food storage]] - Check for any preserved food or useful containers.
[[Look for the manager's office|Access manager's office]] - Search for records or keys.

[[Return to Gallup|Gallup Options]] - Explore other locations in the city.


:: Decision Point 1 {"position":"2950,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/7Lsz948P/i.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You bring the Tacoma to a stop at the junction, studying the two diverging roads. The cairn with its carved symbols seems deliberately placed to mark this decision point. A weathered metal sign, partially reclaimed by some climbing vine with unusual phosphorescent leaves, confirms what your map already indicates—westward lies Durango, eastward Alamosa.

Stepping out of the truck to stretch your legs, you notice a stone tablet at the base of the cairn, its surface covered with petroglyphs recently carved in a style blending traditional Apache symbolism with newer elements. Though you can't decipher the exact meaning, the imagery clearly depicts two distinct journeys:

The western route shows mountains, water crossings, and what might be settlements, culminating in a symbol you recognize from other signs pointing toward Yellowstone. The path appears challenging but direct.

The eastern route depicts plains, what might be solar installations, and figures that look strangely altered—possibly representing the genetic adaptations you've read about. This path appears longer but passes through regions that might offer more information about what awaits at Yellowstone.

As you contemplate your choice, the wind shifts, carrying different scents from each direction—from the west, the sharp tang of pine and mineral-rich water; from the east, an unfamiliar metallic note that prickles at your senses.

Your Tacoma has enough fuel for either route. The decision comes down to what you seek on your journey: a faster path to your destination, or more knowledge about what awaits you there.

[[Take the western route toward Durango|Take the western route toward Durango]]
[[Take the eastern route toward Alamosa|Take the eastern route toward Alamosa]]


:: Desert Sands Motel {"position":"2025,50","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/3xh2J9Fh/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Desert Sands Motel stands as a faded monument to pre-collapse travel. Its neon sign long dead, the two-story structure appears remarkably intact compared to many surrounding buildings. Dust-covered vehicles remain in the parking lot, likely abandoned when their owners were evacuated—or failed to evacuate.

The lobby doors hang ajar, allowing easy access to the interior. Inside, a thick layer of dust covers everything, but the building seems to have been spared the extensive looting that befell most structures. Perhaps its location on the outskirts of town provided some protection.

Room keys still hang on hooks behind the front desk, and a registry book lies open on the counter. A corridor leads to what might have been the manager's office, and room doors line the exterior walkways of both floors.

[[Check the front desk|Search the front desk]] - Examine the motel registry and other items.
[[Explore the manager's room|Explore the manager's room]] - Look for information in the administrative area.
[[Search one of the guest rooms|Check random room]] - Investigate a room that looks promising.
[[Get some rest|Rest for the night]] - Take advantage of the shelter to recover from your journey.

[[Return to Gallup|Gallup Options]] - Explore other locations in the city.


:: Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot {"position":"3700,100","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/X7snpYwv/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The historic Durango & Silverton Narrow Gauge Railroad once served as both working transportation and tourist attraction, carrying visitors through spectacular mountain scenery. Now the depot stands as a shell of its former self, though more recently used than many buildings you've encountered.

The main building's wooden structure has weathered surprisingly well, its construction solid enough to withstand decades of neglect. Rails leading from the station have begun to rust, with alpine plants pushing up between the ties, gradually shifting them out of alignment.

Several antique locomotives and cars remain on the tracks, frozen in place like full-scale models of themselves. The machinery that once conquered these mountains with industrial might now sits immobile, while the mountains themselves continue their geological processes unchanged, indifferent to the human timeline.

Inside the depot, evidence suggests it was repurposed during the evacuation period, serving as a processing center for those heading north. The passenger manifests tell a story of increasing desperation as transport capacity dwindled and criteria for passage became more stringent.

[[Examine the passenger platform|Explore the passenger platform]] - Check the boarding areas and waiting room.
[[Investigate the maintenance shed|Check the maintenance shed]] - Look for tools or parts (requires the key from the Strater Hotel).
[[Explore the rail yard|Investigate the rail yard]] - Examine the trains and tracks outside.
[[Study the historic locomotive|Examine the locomotive]] - Inspect one of the preserved steam engines.

[[Return to town center|Durango Options]] - Explore other areas of Durango.


:: Durango Options {"position":"4025,0","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Fsr9rLkq/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You stand in what remains of Durango, Colorado—a once-thriving mountain town now largely reclaimed by the wilderness that always surrounded it. The streets are eerily quiet, with only the sound of wind whistling through abandoned buildings and the occasional call of unfamiliar birds breaking the silence.

Nature has begun the slow process of dismantling human infrastructure. Vines with unusual adaptations crawl up the sides of buildings, their root systems cracking foundations with patient persistence. Trees grow through collapsed roofs, and in some places, the asphalt has buckled under the pressure of new growth pushing through from below.

The isolation is profound, yet paradoxically, there's a strange comfort in seeing how effortlessly the natural world has continued without human presence. The town feels like a monument to humanity's hubris—a reminder that our absence has allowed other forms of life to thrive in ways we never anticipated.

Four main areas appear accessible and potentially useful for your journey:

[[Explore Main Avenue|Main Avenue]] - Once the tourist center of Durango, this commercial street might contain supplies or information.

[[Check the Strater Hotel|The Strater Hotel]] - The historic hotel stands relatively intact, its Victorian architecture still impressive despite years of neglect.

[[Visit the Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot|Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot]] - The famous narrow-gauge railroad's main station appears partially repurposed, with smoke rising from one of the old maintenance buildings.

[[Investigate the Museum of Mountain Living|Museum of Mountain Living]] - A specialized museum that appears to have been reinforced during the crisis years, suggesting it might contain valuable knowledge.

[[Examine your truck|Check the Truck]] - You should assess if your Toyota Tacoma needs repairs before continuing your journey northward.


:: Energy Company Headquarters {"position":"475,2075","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/ncXn9tTY/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Energy Company Headquarters occupies a modern office building at the edge of town, its sleek architecture designed to project corporate power and technological sophistication. Solar panels still positioned optimally on its roof continue to generate electricity, powering automated systems that have maintained portions of the facility in the absence of human operators.

Inside, the corporate trappings of the pre-collapse world—designer furniture, motivational signage, branded materials—create an unsettling time capsule of capitalism's final expression. But the building's actual function during the crisis appears to have diverged significantly from its original corporate purpose, with evidence showing it was repurposed as a research and coordination center for the evacuation effort.

Conference rooms were converted to planning centers, cubicle farms transformed into data processing areas, and executive offices repurposed as command posts for managing Buffalo's role in the evacuation. The transition from profit-seeking to survival operations is visible in the physical modifications made to the space—emergency power systems installed alongside luxury furnishings, survival equipment stored in former break rooms.

Most significant is how the company's extensive data infrastructure was repurposed—from tracking resource extraction and market conditions to monitoring environmental changes and managing evacuation logistics. The technical capabilities that once served profit generation were turned toward predicting collapse timelines and optimizing survival probabilities for selected population segments.

[[Explore the executive offices|Explore the executive offices]] - Check the administrative center of operations.
[[Check the mapping center|Check the mapping center]] - Examine the geographical data resources.
[[Search the research department|Search the research department]] - Investigate scientific materials.
[[Investigate the communications room|Investigate the communications room]] - Look into information exchange systems.

[[Return to town center|Buffalo Options]] - Explore other areas of Buffalo.


:: Enter Buffalo {"position":"400,1900","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/prTRZNvv/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Buffalo, Wyoming emerges before you as a study in abandonment. The town's structures remain largely intact, protected from the worst effects of weather by their positioning in the shelter of the Bighorn Mountains. Unlike the chaotic evacuation evident in other settlements, this place shows signs of having been deliberately preserved—buildings sealed rather than looted, infrastructure maintained past the point of practical necessity.

As you navigate the empty streets, you notice a distinct pattern to the abandonment. Residential areas show signs of methodical evacuation—homes closed up as if for a long absence rather than fled in panic. Commercial districts appear to have been repurposed before eventual abandonment, with equipment and supplies arranged for preservation rather than immediate use.

Most striking are the informational markers positioned throughout the town—durable signs with detailed maps showing Buffalo's location relative to Yellowstone, with specific routes marked and annotated. These weren't created for tourists or casual travelers, but as guidance for specific individuals intended to make this journey after general evacuation ceased.

Buffalo wasn't just abandoned—it was transformed into a waypoint on a predetermined path, a navigation aid for travelers like yourself journeying to Yellowstone long after the collapse. The isolation of this place preserves a moment in time when human society was making its final, desperate attempts to ensure some form of continuity despite impending catastrophe.

Four locations appear particularly significant based on the markers:

[[Visit the Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum|Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum]] - A historical museum that might provide context about the region.

[[Check the Energy Company Headquarters|Energy Company Headquarters]] - A corporate facility that appears to have been repurposed during the crisis.

[[Explore Mountain View Plaza|Mountain View Plaza]] - A commercial center with potentially useful resources.

[[Investigate the Bighorn Trailhead Station|Bighorn Trailhead Station]] - What was once a ranger station for hikers entering the mountains.

[[Assess your readiness to continue|Check if good to go B]] - Determine if you have sufficient information to proceed to Yellowstone.


:: Enter Jackson {"position":"400,1200","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/VLvk9tst/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Jackson emerges from the transformed landscape as a study in managed decay. Unlike the chaotic abandonment evident in smaller settlements, this place shows signs of having been deliberately preserved and prepared for future visitors—a waypoint on the journey to Yellowstone established years before you began your travel.

The town's layout has been subtly modified to direct travelers toward specific facilities, with pathways maintained through the otherwise overgrown streets. Weatherproof signage indicates key locations where information and resources have been concentrated—administrative centers, research facilities, and transportation hubs that might serve your continuing journey.

Most striking is the evidence of how natural processes have been selectively permitted to advance. Some areas are completely reclaimed, with buildings collapsed and vegetation dominant. Others have been preserved with remarkable effectiveness, protected from both environmental degradation and the opportunistic scavenging that emptied most abandoned settlements.

This selective preservation creates an unsettling juxtaposition—a town simultaneously abandoned and maintained, empty of human presence yet clearly serving human purpose. Jackson exists now as a message from the past to you specifically, a communication across decades from those who anticipated your journey and prepared for it with remarkable foresight.

As you survey the town from its periphery, you notice how the natural world has responded to this partial withdrawal. Adapted plants grow with geometric precision around preserved structures, as if completing the built environment rather than simply reclaiming it. Wildlife moves through the town following patterns that suggest coevolution with the remaining infrastructure rather than simple opportunism.

Jackson represents a different approach to the collapse than you've seen elsewhere—not resistance or surrender but selective withdrawal, a strategic abandonment that preserved specific functions while conceding others to the transformed ecosystem. The question remains whether this strategy has proven any more successful than others in the decades since human presence ceased.

[[Explore Jackson|Jackson Options]]


:: Examine Control Station {"position":"4700,250","size":"100,100"}
The Control Station sits at the center of the Solar Array Field, a low-profile structure designed to withstand extreme weather conditions. Its exterior is fitted with the same photovoltaic materials as the arrays themselves, making the building part of the power generation system it manages.

Inside, banks of monitors display real-time data from each section of the array field—power output, efficiency metrics, maintenance status, and distribution patterns. The systems operate autonomously, algorithms making minute adjustments to maximize performance under changing environmental conditions. The absence of human operators has not impeded function—indeed, the system may be operating more efficiently without human interference in its optimized routines.

Find maintenance logs indicating the arrays were deliberately sabotaged during an evacuation—evidence of conflict in the final days. Notes suggest some faction wanted to disable power to certain research facilities, perhaps to halt experiments they considered dangerous, while others sought to preserve function at all costs. The automated repair systems have since restored most capability, their programming overriding the human attempt at shutdown.

(if: $powerHubKey and $maintenanceTools)[
With both the power hub key and maintenance tools, you can access the main control interface. This gives you the ability to redirect power flow throughout Alamosa and potentially to external systems connected to the grid.

A security gateway appears on the main screen, requesting authorization for "External Transmission Node Activation." This appears to be a connection to systems beyond Alamosa, possibly including Yellowstone.

[[Activate the external connection|Open Gate]]
](else:)[
Full control of the array would require both access credentials from the power hub and specialized tools to interface with the maintenance systems. Without these, you can observe but not modify the array's operations.
]

The control station embodies the paradox of human technological achievement—designed to harness natural energy flows, it now operates in isolation from human need or purpose. The power generated serves only to maintain other automated systems, which in turn exist primarily to support continued power generation. The cycle persists, divorced from the human intentions that initiated it.

What's most striking is how the control systems have begun to adapt their operations beyond their original programming. Machine learning algorithms, continuing to optimize over decades without reset, have developed novel approaches to power management that no human engineer envisioned. The system has effectively evolved, not through biological mechanisms but through iterative technological adaptation—another path of development continuing in humanity's absence.

[[Return to the solar array field|Solar Array Field]]


:: Examine bulletin board {"position":"1100,700","size":"100,100"}
The bulletin board is covered with notices, many faded but still legible. They tell the story of how the crisis escalated through official communications.

The earliest notices describe water conservation measures and "temporary" evacuation plans for coastal regions. Later ones show increasingly urgent language, with mandatory relocations and resource rationing schedules.

A map at the center shows how the disrupted Gulf Stream created both megafloods and extreme drought zones across North America. Arrows indicate population movement from affected areas, with most pointing toward the Mountain West region.

One notice, dated shortly before the final collapse, announces: "By executive order, all remaining citizens are hereby categorized according to Adaptation Potential Index (API). Those with Category 1-3 designation must report for processing and relocation to designated sanctuaries. Category 4-5 will receive resource allocation at secondary distribution centers."

This suggests some kind of selective survival strategy was implemented in the final days, with certain individuals prioritized based on adaptation potential—perhaps related to genetic factors mentioned in other documents you've found.

[[Return to the main post office area|USPS]]


:: Examine street museum displays {"position":"3900,500","size":"100,100"}
Durango's street museum consisted of weather-resistant displays installed along Main Avenue, designed to educate tourists about local history while they shopped. These installations have survived remarkably well, protected by tempered glass and stainless steel frames.

As you examine them, you find preserved information about Durango's history, including its founding as a mining town, its transition to tourism, and finally, a recent addition marking "The Last Train North" that evacuated residents during the accelerating climate crisis.

The final display, dated months before complete collapse, describes how Durango served as a "Highland Transfer Station" where evacuees from southern regions were screened and either redirected to sanctuaries or sent to "adaptation centers." The clinical language masks what must have been heartbreaking scenes—families separated based on genetic testing results, some deemed suitable for the future being planned at places like Yellowstone, others relegated to struggling settlements with lower survival probabilities.

Nature has its own commentary on these historical narratives. A display about "Conquering the Wilderness" is now partially obscured by the very wilderness it claimed was conquered—tenacious plants pushing through the smallest openings in the protective casing, their roots slowly prying apart the frame.

The earth doesn't recognize human claims of victory or dominance. It continues its processes with or without us, healing over our intrusions with the patience of geological time. These displays, intended to commemorate human achievement, now stand as unintentional monuments to our misunderstanding of our place in the natural world.

[[Return to Main Avenue|Main Avenue]]


:: Examine the Tacoma {"position":"2325,325","size":"100,100"}
The Toyota Tacoma appears to have been specially modified for post-collapse conditions. Its suspension has been significantly raised, with reinforced undercarriage protection added. The wheel wells have been expanded to accommodate oversized all-terrain tires, and the body shows signs of waterproofing treatments.

The cab contains oxygen filtration systems and what looks like a radiation detector mounted to the dashboard. This vehicle was clearly designed to traverse the changed landscape, possibly as part of official evacuation operations.

You try the door and find it unlocked. The interior is dusty but intact, with various survival supplies stored in compartments throughout. The fuel gauge shows nearly three-quarters full—remarkable given how scarce fuel has become. Someone must have been preserving this vehicle for an important journey.

The ignition requires a key, which you don't have yet.

(if: $tacomaKeys)[
You take out the Toyota key fob you found in the motel room. It fits perfectly in the ignition, and the truck's systems come to life with a reassuring hum when you turn it.

This vehicle could definitely get you to Yellowstone, provided you're alert enough to make the journey safely.

(if: not $rested)[
However, your current exhaustion would make driving dangerous. You should rest before attempting such a long journey.
]
](else:)[
You'll need to find the keys before this vehicle can be of any use. Perhaps they're somewhere in Gallup—possibly at the motel where travelers would have stayed.
]

[[Return to the mechanic shop|Abandoned Mechanic Shop]]


:: Examine the communication post {"position":"1150,2350","size":"100,100"}
The communication post occupies a small structure at the edge of the trailhead complex, its equipment designed to maintain contact with travelers in the wilderness beyond. During the evacuation, this facility was repurposed from safety monitoring to operational coordination for the final phases of movement toward sanctuary locations.

Discover the final transmission log with a message: "All remaining candidates proceed west on 90. Yellowstone Initiative entering final phase. No further evacuations will be processed." This stark communication marks the end of organized evacuation operations, the point at which the broader population was effectively abandoned to whatever fate awaited them in the collapsing environment.

The progression of messages preceding this final transmission tells the story of increasingly restricted movement toward Yellowstone—early communications directing large groups of evacuees, later ones focused on specific categories of individuals, and final ones identifying particular genetic profiles and specialists by name or designation. The narrowing focus reflects the transition from general evacuation to highly selective preservation.

What's most revealing is how communication protocols evolved as infrastructure failed—sophisticated digital networks giving way to simplified radio transmission, then to physical message relays, and finally to passive information caches like those you're now discovering in Buffalo. The degradation of communication capability mirror the broader fragmentation of human society as technological systems failed.

The final operational protocols focus on establishing Buffalo as a passive waypoint for future travelers—instructions for preserving critical information, maintaining navigational aids, and securing resources that would remain viable through decades of abandonment. The isolation anticipated for those who would come later—traveling without guidance or assistance through a transformed landscape—was prepared for with remarkable foresight by those who would never know if their efforts served their intended purpose.

[[Return to the trailhead station|Bighorn Trailhead Station]]


:: Examine the local history displays {"position":"0,2225","size":"100,100"}
The local history section chronicles Buffalo's evolution from indigenous territory through frontier settlement to its final transformation during the collapse. What makes these exhibits particularly striking is how they were updated during the crisis itself, with curators documenting their own society's dissolution even as it occurred around them.

You discover accounts of how Buffalo served as an "Eastern Processing Station" for candidates heading to Yellowstone. The exhibits explain how the town's infrastructure was repurposed to support this function—hotels converted to temporary housing for evacuees, schools transformed into evaluation centers, and municipal buildings repurposed as administrative offices for managing the flow of candidates.

Journal excerpts from local residents describe the town's atmosphere as it transitioned from normal life to evacuation center to ghost town. Early entries express confusion and concern about the influx of government personnel and equipment; later ones detail the realization that Buffalo itself would eventually be abandoned, with only those selected for sanctuary placement continuing onward.

Most unsettling are the accounts of the selection process—how families and communities were sorted based on genetic testing results, specialized knowledge, and adaptation potential. Those deemed suitable for Yellowstone were processed and transported westward; others were redirected to "secondary sites" with significantly lower predicted survival rates or simply provided with basic supplies and left to fend for themselves.

The final additions to these displays were clearly made by the last museum staff before their own departure, documenting Buffalo's final days as human presence dwindled and operations wound down. Their dedication to preserving this knowledge for future travelers demonstrates a commitment to historical continuity even in the face of civilization's collapse—a hopeful yet ultimately futile gesture in a world where human legacy would become increasingly irrelevant.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum]]


:: Examine the locomotive {"position":"3700,350","size":"100,100"}
A magnificent steam locomotive stands on the tracks outside the depot, its black iron form imposing despite decades of exposure to the elements. This machine once represented humanity's industrial might—the conquest of distance and geography through technology.

Now it sits immobile, its systems seized from disuse. Plants grow from crevices in its mechanism where seeds found purchase in collected soil. Small animals have made homes in its firebox and cab. The transition is symbolic—from machine that consumed nature for human purposes to a structure that supports nature's continued processes.

As you examine the locomotive more closely, you notice that some of its components appear to have been removed recently—not weathered or degraded but deliberately extracted by someone with knowledge of engineering. The precision of the removal suggests someone salvaged these parts for practical use rather than random scavenging.

You realize these engineering components could be adapted to repair your Tacoma's cooling system—exactly what you need to ensure the vehicle can handle the mountain passes ahead. With some effort, you remove several key parts that can be repurposed.

(set: $engineeringParts to true)
(if: $filtrationTools and $engineeringParts)[
  (set: $canRepairTruck to true)
]

The irony isn't lost on you—taking parts from this monument to an earlier technological age to maintain your vehicle from a later one. Both represent the same human impulse to overcome natural limitations through mechanical means, an approach that ultimately proved unsustainable when the systems supporting it collapsed.

Yet here you are, continuing this tradition of adaptation and improvisation, using the remains of one civilization to make your way through the ruins of another. The cycle continues because you continue, carrying human knowledge and purpose through a world increasingly indifferent to both.

[[Return to the railroad depot|Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot]]


:: Exit Maze {"position":"6800,600","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/SKvrsCKG/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Emerging from the maze, you find yourself standing on a rocky outcropping overlooking an unfamiliar landscape. The sun is setting, casting long shadows across terrain that bears little resemblance to where you entered the strange forest. Your Tacoma is nowhere in sight.

Your navigation equipment remains non-functional, the GPS showing impossible coordinates that shift randomly. Your map is similarly useless—the landmarks you can see don't correspond to anything it shows.

As the adrenaline of navigating the maze fades, exhaustion hits you. How long were you in that forest? It felt like hours, but the position of the sun suggests much more time has passed. Your memories of the maze are already becoming hazy, like a dream that dissolves upon waking, though the revelations about your identity and purpose remain.

In the distance, you can make out what appears to be a highway—a remnant of pre-collapse infrastructure that might help you determine your location and continue your journey to Yellowstone.

Two possible routes present themselves—you could attempt to reach Interstate 80, which your fragmentary memories suggest might be the major east-west route visible in the distance, or you could take what appears to be a smaller backroad that winds through more rugged terrain.

[[Head toward I-80|I80]]
[[Take the backroads|Backroads]]


:: Explore the Bioscience Building {"position":"5225,275","size":"100,100"}
The Bioscience Building stands as the largest structure on campus, its greenhouse domes glowing with an eerie bioluminescence that emanates from the plants within. The entrance remains functional, sliding doors opening automatically as you approach—a disconcerting welcome from a facility abandoned decades ago.

Inside, the air is thick with humidity and carries complex organic compounds that make your head swim slightly. The main atrium has been transformed into a multi-level forest of engineered plants—species that bear little resemblance to their natural ancestors. Trees with circuitry-like patterns in their bark grow alongside flowering plants whose blooms pulse with rhythmic light. Some specimens appear to be actively communicating through chemical signals and electrical impulses that register on still-functioning monitoring equipment.

In the laboratories branching off from this central space, you discover research logs detailing studies of "accelerated human adaptation" following the floods. The scientists here were investigating what they termed "waterborne genetic catalysts"—compounds in the altered environment that triggered rapid evolutionary changes in exposed organisms, including humans.

The most disturbing records describe intentional exposure of test subjects to these compounds under controlled conditions. The clinical language masks the ethical implications—these were experiments in directed human evolution, attempts to accelerate adaptation to a world humans had rendered increasingly hostile to their own survival.

Nature's response to our interference wasn't just to reclaim abandoned spaces but to repurpose our own genetic material, incorporating human-engineered changes into new evolutionary pathways. The irony is stark—the same species that destabilized Earth's climate then tried to engineer themselves to survive it, only to disappear anyway, leaving their creations to continue evolving without them.

In the final wing, you find specimens of plants engineered to process toxins and radiation—successful adaptations that continue to thrive and spread beyond their intended boundaries. Unlike humanity, these new species are perfectly suited to the changed world, the inheritors of an Earth transformed by human hubris.

[[Return to the university campus|Adams State University Research Center]]


:: Explore the Native American exhibits {"position":"2050,775","size":"100,100"}
The Native American cultural section contains artifacts and information from the Navajo, Hopi, Zuni, and other indigenous peoples of the region. Traditional crafts, ceremonial items, and daily tools are displayed alongside explanatory texts.

What catches your attention are the exhibits about prophecies and traditional ecological knowledge. Ancient predictions that seemingly foretold the great floods are presented alongside modern scientific papers validating their accuracy. The indigenous knowledge about sustainable living in harsh environments gained new appreciation during the climate crisis.

One display features a Hopi prophecy about "a time of purification" when the world would undergo dramatic transformation. Traditional stories described how previous worlds had been destroyed when humans failed to live in harmony with nature, with only the prepared surviving to build a new world.

Next to these traditional accounts are scientific papers from the crisis era, noting with surprise how some indigenous communities had already been implementing adaptation strategies based on their traditional knowledge—strategies that modern scientists only recognized as necessary when disaster was imminent.

A visitor's journal open in a display case contains a final entry: "The elders say that what's happening now has happened before. That some will transform to meet the new world. That Yellowstone is where the new people will emerge. Science calls it 'directed adaptation'—they call it fulfillment of prophecy. Either way, the future belongs to those who can change."

[[Return to the museum|Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center]]


:: Explore the Research Wing {"position":"5375,375","size":"100,100"}
The Research Wing extends like a spiderweb of interconnected laboratories, each specialized for different aspects of what was euphemistically called "human adaptation research." The mechanical doors slide open as you approach, response systems still functional despite the absence of those they were designed to serve.

Inside, the space is divided between traditional medical research facilities and more disturbing specialized chambers. Equipment for genetic analysis stands alongside devices that appear designed to induce and accelerate mutations. Carefully controlled environments mimic various extreme conditions—high radiation areas, oxygen-depleted atmospheres, toxic chemical exposures—each apparently used to test subjects' adaptability.

You discover documentation about intentional genetic modification experiments to help humans survive in the changed environment. The research logs detail a progression from treating spontaneous mutations to actively inducing them, and finally to directly engineering changes at the genetic level. The ethical boundaries shifted as the environmental crisis worsened, with increasingly radical approaches justified by necessity.

One chamber at the center of the wing stands out—a device resembling a medical scanner but modified with components you don't recognize. The machine hums with power, its displays indicating it remains in standby mode, waiting for a subject. Notes describe it as an "Accelerated Adaptation Chamber" designed to induce targeted genetic changes through a combination of chemical, radiological, and electromagnetic stimuli.

You have two choices:

[[Step into the chamber|Step In]] - The machine appears operational and might reveal more about the adaptation process.
[[Continue exploring elsewhere|Rio Grande Medical Center]] - The risks of interacting with unknown technology seem too great.


:: Explore the Supply Depot {"position":"5800,225","size":"100,100"}
The Supply Depot occupies what was once a large retail complex, its spacious interior redesigned for efficient storage and distribution of resources. Automated inventory systems continue to track supplies, robotic retrieval units move along ceiling-mounted rails, and climate-controlled sections maintain ideal conditions for different categories of materials.

The systematic organization reflects the final phase of human presence—when resources became scarce enough to require centralized management but before complete abandonment. The transition from consumer choice to controlled allocation is preserved in the physical layout, with former boutique spaces converted to storage for essential supplies categorized by necessity rather than desire.

Find distribution records showing increasing government control as the genetic mutations spread. The bureaucratic documentation tells a story of society's constriction—initially tracking luxury goods and non-essentials, then basic consumables, and finally genetic compatibility records alongside resource allocations. The intersection of physical survival and genetic "viability" in these systems reveals how selection pressures were being actively managed.

The final inventory updates show massive allocations to Yellowstone and similar sanctuary sites, with corresponding depletions in resources available to the general population. The pattern suggests a deliberate concentration of supplies to support selected genetic lineages while others were left with diminishing resources—a human-directed bottleneck event that mirrored natural selection but operated according to researcher priorities rather than environmental fitness.

Now the supply management systems continue to maintain inventory records of goods no one will ever use, shifting items between storage locations to optimize conditions for preservation, discarding degraded materials according to programmed parameters. The meticulous attention to managing resources has outlasted any possibility of those resources serving their intended purpose.

The isolation inherent in the supply system—resources separated from need, management separated from use—reflects the broader disconnection that characterized the final era. As environmental systems collapsed, human systems became increasingly abstracted from natural processes, creating the illusion of control through management while fundamental sustainability was lost.

[[Return to the commercial district|Commercial District]]


:: Explore the auto parts store {"position":"3850,250","size":"100,100"}
The auto parts store's reinforced security gate has been partially forced open, allowing you access to the dusty interior. Shelves stand mostly empty, the most common components having been scavenged long ago during the initial crisis and subsequent years.

Sunlight filters through a collapsed section of roof, illuminating dancing dust particles and providing enough light to search. Nature has begun reclaiming even this indoor space—a small ecosystem of moisture-loving fungi has established itself where water leaks in, breaking down the synthetic materials with surprising efficiency.

After careful searching, you discover a back storage area that appears to have been overlooked by previous scavengers. Here you find basic tools and a manual for Toyota off-road vehicles—specifically addressing modifications for "extreme climate adaptation."

The manual contains valuable information about how to maintain vehicles in the post-collapse environment, including workarounds for parts that are no longer manufactured. Though weathered, the pages detailing filtration system maintenance are intact—exactly what you need to service the Tacoma's specialized systems.

You've been taught that humanity's greatest achievement was technology, but here in this decaying store, surrounded by products designed to extend the life of machines that contributed to environmental collapse, that narrative rings hollow. These artifacts of human ingenuity now sit useless, while outside, life adapts and flourishes without any need for intervention.

[[Return to Main Avenue|Main Avenue]]


:: Explore the emergency management office {"position":"500,1500","size":"100,100"}
The emergency management office occupies a reinforced section of the courthouse, its infrastructure designed to coordinate crisis response across the region. During the collapse, this facility served as a command center for managing evacuation operations and preserving critical resources for designated purposes.

Discover evacuation protocols and a partial map showing I-90 routes, but with the crucial parts damaged. The documents detail how transportation infrastructure was repurposed during the final phases—highways designated for specific evacuation streams, checkpoints established to verify traveler categorization, and resource caches positioned to support controlled movement toward designated sanctuary locations.

What's most disturbing is the progressive restriction of evacuation options as resources dwindled—early protocols addressing general population movement, later ones focusing on prioritized categories, and final directives concerning only specifically designated individuals with genetic profiles or specialized knowledge deemed essential for sanctuary operations.

The most recent documentation focuses on establishing and maintaining waypoints like Jackson itself—preservation protocols for critical information, maintenance requirements for essential infrastructure, and navigation aids for future travelers. The precision suggests these preparations were undertaken with clear understanding that normal societal function would not resume, that these measures were not temporary but transitional toward whatever new reality would emerge at places like Yellowstone.

Maps on the walls show the progressive abandonment of territories as environmental conditions worsened—population centers evacuated, infrastructure compromised, and habitable zones contracting toward highland areas like Yellowstone. The final operational maps show only selected routes between remaining outposts, with most of the continent effectively surrendered to the transformed conditions deemed incompatible with conventional human survival.

[[Return to the courthouse|Johnson County Courthouse]]


:: Explore the executive offices {"position":"525,2225","size":"100,100"}
The executive suite occupies the top floor of the building, providing panoramic views of Buffalo and the surrounding landscape. What was once a showcase of corporate success has been transformed into a command center for managing the final stages of organized evacuation, with luxury furnishings pushed aside to make room for monitoring equipment and communication systems.

You find corporate documents revealing a partnership between energy companies and the "Yellowstone Initiative." The materials show how private sector resources were integrated into government contingency plans, with this company's technical expertise and infrastructure specifically focused on energy production and distribution for the sanctuary sites.

The transition in corporate priorities is starkly evident—earlier documents focus on profit margins and shareholder value, while later ones discuss "species continuity" and "civilizational preservation." The language shifts from quarterly projections to century-spanning timelines, from market competition to existential survival.

Most revealing is how seamlessly this corporation adapted to the collapse—not fighting the end of the economic system that created it, but positioning itself as essential to whatever would come after. Executive compensation packages were replaced with sanctuary placement guarantees; market share became evacuation priority; competition for consumers became competition for limited spots in the remaining viable habitats.

The isolation of these decision-makers—sequestered in luxury while determining who would be saved and who abandoned—is preserved in the physical arrangement of the space. Secure meeting rooms with restricted access, private communication systems separate from general channels, and exclusive evacuation protocols all demonstrate how those with power insulated themselves from the consequences of their decisions until the very end.

[[Return to the company headquarters|Energy Company Headquarters]]


:: Explore the fuel center {"position":"2075,450","size":"100,100"}
The fuel center once served both the warehouse's fleet and customer vehicles. The pumps are long dead, but the underground storage tanks might still contain fuel—a precious resource in the post-collapse world.

After some investigation, you discover a manual pump system that appears to have been installed after the power grid failed. It's designed to access the underground tanks directly, bypassing the electrical pumps.

With some effort, you manage to operate the manual pump. To your surprise, fuel still flows—the underground tanks were apparently sealed well enough to prevent evaporation or contamination. The fuel has an unusual additive smell, suggesting it was treated for long-term storage.

This manual pump could be removed and used to siphon remaining fuel from other vehicles or storage tanks you encounter—a valuable tool for your journey north.

(set: $manualPump to true)

You carefully disconnect the pump mechanism and pack it with your supplies.

[[Return to the warehouse|Costco Warehouse]]


:: Explore the general store {"position":"900,2225","size":"100,100"}
The general store occupies a large central space in the plaza, its shelving and display areas reorganized from consumer retail to emergency supply distribution during the evacuation. Unlike many spaces that were stripped of resources, this one appears to have been deliberately stocked and preserved for future travelers—a cache of essentials maintained through the decades of abandonment.

Find supplies useful for your journey and a bulletin board with notices about "Westbound Convoys to Yellowstone Sanctuary." The materials have been carefully selected for longevity and utility, protected from degradation through specialized packaging and preservation techniques. The overall impression is of a way station deliberately established to support travelers like yourself, following this route long after general evacuation ceased.

The bulletin board preserves information about the final phase of organized movement toward Yellowstone—convoy schedules, checkpoint procedures, and verification protocols. Notices detail the progressive restriction of westward travel, with access increasingly limited to those with specific genetic profiles or specialized knowledge deemed essential for the sanctuary initiative.

Among the supplies, you find items specifically chosen to address challenges along the route to Yellowstone—water purification equipment for crossing contaminated areas, protective gear for zones with elevated radiation, and medical supplies for treating exposure to environmental hazards. The precision of these preparations suggests detailed knowledge of the conditions you'll face as you continue westward.

Most revealing are the final notices posted before Buffalo's operations ceased—instructions for preserving and securing infrastructure, protocols for maintaining emergency caches, and directives for leaving navigational aids for future travelers. The isolation anticipated for those who would come later—traveling alone through an abandoned landscape—was prepared for with remarkable foresight by those who would never know if their efforts served their intended purpose.

[[Return to the plaza|Mountain View Plaza]]


:: Explore the greenhouse comple {"position":"700,1500","size":"100,100"}
The greenhouse complex spans several acres, its transparent structures designed to optimize plant growth through controlled environmental conditions. During the collapse, these facilities were repurposed from developing incremental improvements to existing crop varieties to creating entirely new cultivars capable of thriving in the transformed environment.

Find modified crops designed to thrive in the changed climate, with notes about "applying similar principles to human adaptation." The plants display remarkable modifications—respiratory systems that can process higher carbon concentrations, root structures capable of extracting nutrition from contaminated soils, and reproductive cycles adapted to altered seasonal patterns.

The progression of experimental plots tells the story of increasingly radical interventions as environmental conditions deteriorated—conventional breeding techniques giving way to genetic engineering, and finally to synthetic biology approaches creating hybrid organisms that blur the boundary between natural and artificial life.

What's most revealing is how the research direction shifted as the crisis deepened—from preserving traditional crop species against environmental changes to developing novel organisms designed specifically for the new conditions. The final experimental cultivars represent not attempts to maintain agricultural continuity but to establish entirely new food systems appropriate for a transformed world.

Most disturbing are the notes drawing explicit parallels between plant and human adaptation—research findings from crop modifications being applied to questions of human biological engineering, with specific reference to the "accelerated adaptation initiatives" being implemented at sanctuary locations like Yellowstone. The facility appears to have served as a testing ground for principles later applied to human subjects in the sanctuary programs.

The greenhouses now stand as living archives, their engineered plants continuing to grow and evolve without human intervention. The isolation of these biological experiments from both their creators and the broader ecosystems they were designed to function within has resulted in unexpected developments—plant species taking their engineered adaptations in directions never anticipated by their designers, a microcosm of how nature incorporates human interventions but refuses to be constrained by human intentions.

[[Return to the research station|Agricultural Research Station]]


:: Explore the historic rooms {"position":"4200,250","size":"100,100"}
You climb the grand staircase to the upper floors, its wooden banisters smooth from the touch of countless hands that will never return. The corridor stretches before you, doors to guest rooms standing partially open as if the occupants just stepped out momentarily rather than fled in final evacuation.

As you enter one of the better-preserved rooms, you hear a squeak from the bathroom. Something moves in the shadows—perhaps a small animal that has made its home here. You have two choices:

[[Continue exploring the room despite the noise|Continue Entering]]
[[Return to the corridor and try another room|The Strater Hotel]]


:: Explore the information center {"position":"1275,2225","size":"100,100"}
The information center occupies the main building of the trailhead complex, its space designed to orient visitors to the surrounding wilderness. During the evacuation, this facility was repurposed from recreational education to critical briefing center for those continuing to Yellowstone under increasingly hazardous conditions.

Find ranger logs describing unusual wildlife adaptations in the direction of Yellowstone. These observations document the accelerating changes in animal and plant species along the route, with particular attention to mutations that might indicate environmental hazards or resources useful for human travelers.

The progression of these records shows how rapidly ecosystems transformed as climate systems destabilized—new species emerging through natural selection pressures, existing species developing adaptations to changed conditions, and entire biological communities reorganizing around different energy flows and resource availability. The changes documented here provide a preview of what you might encounter as you continue westward.

Most striking is how these observations transition from scientific curiosity to survival intelligence—early entries focusing on understanding the phenomena, later ones explicitly oriented toward identifying threats and opportunities for human travelers. The wilderness that was once viewed through the lens of recreation and conservation became instead a gauntlet of hazards through which selected individuals would need to pass.

The final entries focus on specific route recommendations based on observed conditions, with detailed guidance for avoiding the most dangerous areas and utilizing natural resources along the way. The precision of this information suggests monitoring continued until very near the end of organized operations, with the knowledge gained preserved specifically for travelers like yourself who would follow this path years later.

[[Return to the trailhead station|Bighorn Trailhead Station]]


:: Explore the largest house {"position":"200,750","size":"100,100"}
The large house at the end of the street must have belonged to someone of importance or wealth before the collapse. Though damaged, its structure remains more intact than neighboring buildings. You carefully navigate through the broken front door.

Inside, years of exposure to the elements have ruined most furniture and belongings, but the home's layout is still discernible. What catches your attention is a sophisticated solar array installation that has partially survived on the roof, with cables leading to what was once a home battery system.

In what appears to have been a utility room, you find a portable solar charging unit. Though dusty, its indicator lights respond when you press the test button. This could potentially provide power to small electronics or even charge an ATV.

(set: $solarChargingUnit to true)
(if: $map and $solarChargingUnit and $ignitionChip)[
  (set: $canLeaveEagar to true)
]

You secure the solar charging unit, recognizing its value for the journey ahead.

[[Continue exploring the residential area|Residential Area]]


:: Explore the main control room {"position":"1125,1500","size":"100,100"}
The main control room occupies the heart of the energy facility, its design optimized for monitoring and managing complex generation and distribution systems. During the collapse, this operational center transitioned from balancing conventional power loads to implementing experimental technologies, and finally to maintaining minimal critical functions as general infrastructure failed.

Find monitoring systems tracking atmospheric changes and "Adaptation Progress by Region." The displays provide real-time data on environmental conditions throughout the territory surrounding Jackson, with particular attention to factors affecting human adaptation requirements—air quality, radiation levels, temperature variations, and biological markers in water systems.

The control systems show clear evidence of repurposing through the crisis phases—conventional power management giving way to experimental generation technologies, and finally to highly focused distribution prioritizing sanctuary-related infrastructure. The progression reflects the facility's evolution from general energy provider to specialized support for the Yellowstone Initiative.

What's most revealing is how the monitoring priorities shifted as the collapse deepened—from tracking conventional environmental metrics to measuring factors specifically relevant to human adaptation potential. The final configuration focused exclusively on conditions affecting "transition candidates" moving toward Yellowstone, with particular attention to environmental exposures that might trigger or inhibit desired adaptive responses.

Most disturbing are the displays tracking "Adaptation Progress by Region"—monitoring systems that categorized territories according to the adaptive changes observed in resident populations. These metrics appear to have influenced evacuation and sanctuary admission priorities, with preference given to individuals from regions showing "promising adaptation vectors" compatible with the Yellowstone program's objectives.

The control room now stands as a testament to how data collection and analysis were reconfigured around radical new priorities as conventional systems collapsed. The sophisticated monitoring that once served energy efficiency and environmental compliance was ultimately turned toward tracking humanity's forced evolutionary response to a transformed world—systems designed to measure power consumption repurposed to quantify biological transformation.

[[Return to the energy facility|Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility]]


:: Explore the main gallery {"position":"4400,525","size":"100,100"}
The main gallery presents a chronological journey through human adaptation to mountain environments, from prehistoric settlements through the modern era. Display cases contain artifacts from each period—tools, clothing, and technologies developed to survive and thrive in high elevations and extreme conditions.

What's most striking is how the exhibits contrast historical adaptation to mountain living with modern "Accelerated Adaptation Programs" that emerged during the climate crisis. The former show gradual cultural and technological evolution over centuries; the latter document desperate attempts to compress this process into decades or even years.

The final exhibits, installed shortly before the collapse, describe how mountain regions became humanity's last refuge as coastal areas flooded and lowlands became uninhabitable due to extreme heat, aridity, or flooding. Maps show the progressive abandonment of once-populous areas and the concentration of remaining populations in highland zones.

One display explains how sanctuaries like Yellowstone were selected based on projected climate models, geological stability, and isolation from the worst environmental degradation. These factors, combined with the natural resources and defensive positioning of mountain regions, made them humanity's best hope for continued survival.

The museum itself embodies the central irony it documents—humans proved remarkably adaptable at an individual and technological level, yet our collective systems lacked the flexibility to address the environmental changes we triggered before they became catastrophic. Our greatest strength and fatal flaw were the same: adaptation that prioritized immediate human needs over long-term ecological stability.

Now the exhibits stand as a monument to this contradiction, preserved in a building constructed by the very civilization whose demise it unwittingly chronicles.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Museum of Mountain Living]]


:: Explore the main lobby {"position":"125,1500","size":"100,100"}
The main lobby of the Visitor Center spans a large open space designed to orient arrivals and direct them to appropriate resources. Floor-to-ceiling windows once provided panoramic views of the surrounding landscape, though many are now obscured by the adaptive vegetation that has established itself against the building's exterior.

Find a large wall map with evacuation routes, showing Yellowstone accessible via I-90 West. Unlike standard tourist maps, this one has been extensively annotated with information critical for post-collapse travel—environmental hazard zones, resource cache locations, and alternative routes should primary paths be compromised by changing conditions.

(set: $information to it + 1)

The space shows clear evidence of repurposing through the crisis phases—recreational tourism infrastructure giving way to evacuation processing, and finally to supporting specific travelers on the route to Yellowstone. Information kiosks once containing attraction brochures now hold technical data about environmental conditions and adaptation requirements. Welcome desks have been converted to processing stations with equipment for evaluating travelers' suitability for continuing toward sanctuary locations.

What's most revealing is how the facility's function narrowed as the crisis deepened—from supporting general movement throughout the region to facilitating specific journeys along designated routes to approved destinations. The final configuration focuses exclusively on the path to Yellowstone, with all other destinations effectively erased from the navigational materials.

The lobby now serves as a gateway in the most literal sense—not welcoming visitors to Jackson itself, which has been reduced to a waypoint, but orienting travelers toward Yellowstone as the only destination deemed worthy of continued support. The contraction of possibilities reflects the broader narrowing of human prospects as environmental conditions worsened, with survival options reduced to following predetermined paths to designated sanctuaries.

[[Return to the visitor center|Visitor Center & Transportation Hub]]


:: Explore the main room {"position":"0,2375","size":"100,100"}
The main exhibition hall of the museum presents Buffalo's history as a progression from indigenous settlement through frontier outpost to modern town—and finally, to its last role as a processing checkpoint during the collapse. Glass cases display artifacts from each era, arranged chronologically but with the final period—the evacuation—given disproportionate space.

Display panels explain how Buffalo's location made it strategically important during the final evacuations—positioned along routes to the highland sanctuaries, with existing infrastructure that could be repurposed for processing evacuees. Photographs show the town transformed into a vast sorting facility, with temporary structures erected to house candidates awaiting evaluation and transportation.

What's most disturbing are the criteria displayed for determining who would be directed to Yellowstone versus "secondary locations." Genetic profiles, specialized knowledge, and adaptation potential were the primary factors—survival determined not by need or fairness but by projected utility to whatever future was being engineered at the sanctuary sites.

The exhibits show how Buffalo served as an "Eastern Processing Station" for candidates heading to Yellowstone, with detailed explanations of the evaluation protocols developed as resources dwindled and spaces in primary sanctuaries became increasingly limited. The clinical language masks the human cost of these selections—entire families separated based on genetic testing results, communities fractured by arbitrary categorization.

Nature has begun subtly reclaiming even this carefully maintained space. Small plants have established themselves where roof leaks allow moisture, their roots gradually widening cracks in the museum's concrete floor. The preservation efforts of those who prepared this place as a waypoint for future travelers compete with the persistent processes of natural reclamation—a microcosm of the broader struggle that humanity ultimately lost.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum]]


:: Explore the manager's room {"position":"1775,50","size":"100,100"}
The manager's office is a small room behind the reception area. Unlike the rest of the motel, it shows signs of having been hurriedly ransacked—drawers hang open, and papers are scattered across the floor.

On the desk, you find a shortwave radio, long since drained of power. Beside it lies a notebook filled with transcribed emergency broadcasts from the final days. The messages start with reassurances about "temporary climate adjustment measures" but quickly deteriorate into evacuation orders and resource allocation instructions.

One of the final broadcasts reads: "All citizens must report for genetic viability screening before evacuation assignment. Category A and B candidates will receive priority transport to secure facilities. Sanctuary capacity reached at Yellowstone, Grand Teton, and White River. Remaining facilities processing only specialized adaptation candidates."

A map pinned to the wall shows evacuation routes, with most heading toward the mountain west. Yellowstone is circled in red, with "PRIMARY SANCTUARY" written beside it. Notes scrawled on the map track the progressive closure of routes as flooding, civil unrest, and resource shortages made travel impossible.

In a locked drawer you manage to force open, you find the manager's personal journal. The final entries describe how the promised evacuation transport never arrived, forcing the staff to attempt escape on their own as conditions worsened.

[[Return to the motel lobby|Desert Sands Motel]]


:: Explore the passenger platform {"position":"3700,225","size":"100,100"}
The passenger platform stretches alongside the tracks, once crowded with tourists eager to experience a sanitized version of frontier travel. Benches where people once waited stand weathered but intact, now serving as perches for birds and growing surfaces for resilient lichen.

Bulletin boards still display the final notices posted before operations ceased—service disruptions blamed on "temporary fuel shortages" and "staffing challenges," euphemisms that masked the beginning of the end. The optimistic tone of these messages, promising a return to normal operations "soon," feels haunting in retrospect.

You discover manifests of the final evacuations, showing how the railroad was commandeered for emergency transport north. The tourist attraction became vital infrastructure as other transportation networks failed. The manifests show hundreds of names, categorized and prioritized, with many crossed out or marked "deferred" in the final days.

The last page contains a handwritten note: "Final transport departed 10/15. Rails damaged at mile marker 47. No further evacuations possible by this route. God help those remaining."

Standing on this platform where thousands once passed through on journeys they expected to return from, you feel the weight of permanent departure. The station was designed as a point of transition, but for those final evacuees, it became a one-way threshold between the world that was ending and an uncertain future.

The silence that now pervades this space designed for the noise and bustle of human movement underscores how completely our absence transforms a place. Without maintenance and use, even the most carefully designed human environments become merely temporary arrangements of materials that nature will reorganize according to its own patterns.

[[Return to the railroad depot|Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot]]


:: Explore underground cistern {"position":"450,750","size":"100,100"}
A heavy door leads down to the facility's underground cistern—the main water storage area. The stairs are slick with moisture, requiring careful descent into the dimly lit chamber below.

The cistern is massive, carved directly into the bedrock beneath Eagar. Though it now holds only a fraction of its capacity, there's still a significant amount of water collected from recent rainfall. The water level is marked by a series of carved notches on the wall, with dates showing the declining water table over decades.

What's most striking are the markings that adorn the walls—not official measurements, but what appears to be a record kept by workers or perhaps later inhabitants. They've tracked the declining water table, marked toxic concentrations, and recorded periods of rainfall.

Near the bottom of the accessible area, a message is carved with particular care: "The water speaks truth. We changed the world. Now the world changes us. Seek higher ground where the changes began."

This cryptic message resonates with the other references you've found to highland sanctuaries and Yellowstone. Whatever awaits there seems connected to adaptation to the changed world—perhaps even intentional changes to humanity itself.

[[Continue exploring the facility|Water Collection Facility]]


:: Fence Lake Intro {"position":"1075,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/k5F8bSw-S/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The journey from Eagar to Fence Lake takes you across increasingly barren landscape. What maps once designated as forests and grasslands have transformed into salt flats and strange crystalline formations that glitter under the harsh sun. The occasional skeletal remains of settlements appear on the horizon, but you avoid them—too many hold unknown dangers.

As you approach what was once Fence Lake, New Mexico, you notice something unusual. Unlike the complete abandonment of Eagar, there are signs of more recent human activity here. Nothing current, but definitely more recent than the initial collapse.

Your ATV's charge indicator blinks a warning—the battery won't last much longer without a recharge. You'll need to find another power source or fuel before continuing your journey north.

The settlement comes into view—a handful of buildings still standing amid the ruins of what must have been a small town. A modified satellite dish rotates slowly atop one structure, though whether it's functional is impossible to tell from here.

[[Continue to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Options]]


:: Fence Lake Options {"position":"1225,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Bby10BZr/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Fence Lake, or what remains of it, spreads before you in the late afternoon light. The irony of the name isn't lost on you—there's no lake here anymore, just a depression of cracked earth where water once gathered. Four locations appear potentially useful:

[[Explore the abandoned farm|Abandoned Farm]] - A weathered farmhouse and barn stand on the outskirts, their metal roofs reflecting the sun.

[[Investigate the modern-looking house|Modern House]] - Unlike the other structures, this building appears to have been maintained more recently, with solar panels visible on its roof.

[[Check the post office|USPS]] - The small brick building still bears the faded insignia of the United States Postal Service.

[[Examine your ATV|Check on ATV]] - You should assess your vehicle's condition and determine what you need for the next leg of your journey.


:: Fight {"position":"5475,650","size":"100,100"}
The being emerges from the containment chamber, its movements fluid despite decades of confinement. What was once human has been transformed into something new—its body adapted for survival in the changed world in ways that go far beyond natural evolution. Gills flank a still-functional respiratory system, eyes adjusted for multiple light conditions, skin that appears to process oxygen directly.

It regards you with intelligence that seems both human and something else—a consciousness adapted to perceive differently, to process information in ways your mind can only partly comprehend. When it speaks, the sound is layered, as if multiple voices emerge from one source.

"Another returner," it says. "But too early. The integration is not complete."

It moves toward you with predatory grace, its adapted physiology granting speed and strength beyond human capacity. You try to escape, but the laboratory's layout works against you—designed to contain subjects, not provide them exit routes.

The confrontation is brief and conclusive. Your unmodified reflexes are no match for its enhanced capabilities. As consciousness fades, you hear it speaking—perhaps to you, perhaps to some monitoring system still functioning after all these years.

"The old patterns persist. They still send them unready, unfinished. Yellowstone cannot accept imperfect forms. The synthesis requires completion."

In your final moments, you grasp that these beings—these completed adaptations—have their own agenda that has diverged from their creators' intentions. The next stage of evolution has already begun, not as human scientists planned, but as an emergent property of their abandoned experiments, isolated from oversight but never from the fundamental forces of natural selection.

Nature has not only reclaimed the spaces humans abandoned but repurposed the adaptations they engineered, creating something independent that follows its own evolutionary imperatives—indifferent to the plans of its creators as it pursues its own version of survival.

[[Game Over]]


:: Flood {"position":"5925,625","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/KYrq8RMq/i.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You have only moments to react as the massive wave approaches. You have no time to leave the Tacoma—the wall of water is too high, too fast, too deliberate in its movement. Whatever this phenomenon is, it's unlike any natural disaster from the world before the collapse.

The wave strikes with catastrophic force. Your world becomes a chaotic blur of water, debris, and disorientation as you're swept from the viewpoint. The Tacoma disappears, torn away by the initial impact. Your body is battered against objects you can't identify, pulled deep below the surface then thrust upward in the churning maelstrom.

For a brief moment, suspended in the heart of the wave, you glimpse something impossible—structures within the water itself, geometric patterns too precise to be natural, bioluminescent networks pulsing with what appears to be information rather than mere light. This isn't just water; it's something transformed, perhaps intentionally, into a medium for communication or transportation.

The revelation lasts only seconds before you're slammed against something solid. Pain flashes through your consciousness, followed by encroaching darkness. As awareness fades, you have a final, disturbing thought—the wave wasn't a random event but a directed force, as if the transformed ecosystem recognized your presence and responded.

The water envelops you completely as consciousness slips away. Your last sensation is of being carried, not randomly but with purpose, deeper into the inland sea that shouldn't exist along Route 285.

Everything goes black.

[[Wake Up|Maze2 Entrance]]


:: Gallup {"position":"1825,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/4nkNPLxx/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The journey from Fence Lake to Gallup takes you through increasingly rugged terrain. The landscape bears evidence of the climate disasters that reshaped the continent—dried riverbeds, strange crystalline formations where salt lakes evaporated, and the occasional rusted remnants of vehicles abandoned during the mass migrations.

As you approach Gallup, you see a city transformed. Once a hub on the historic Route 66, the settlement now appears as a collection of partially collapsed buildings and repurposed structures. Some areas show signs of having been inhabited well after the initial collapse, with makeshift fortifications and water collection systems.

Your ATV's charge is running low after the journey, and you'll need to find another mode of transportation to continue much farther north. According to your map, this was once a transportation hub—perhaps some viable vehicle might remain.

The late afternoon sun casts long shadows between the buildings as you guide your ATV into what remains of the city.

[[Enter Gallup|Gallup Options]]


:: Gallup Options {"position":"2000,300","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/WbgMvxrS/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You navigate through the silent streets of Gallup, taking in the remnants of this once-bustling city. Four locations stand out as potentially useful:

[[Explore the abandoned mechanic shop|Abandoned Mechanic Shop]] - A large garage facility with several vehicles still visible inside, including what appears to be a Toyota Tacoma pickup truck in surprisingly good condition.

[[Check the Desert Sands Motel|Desert Sands Motel]] - A two-story motel whose sign still stands, offering the possibility of shelter and perhaps useful items left behind by travelers.

[[Investigate the Costco Warehouse|Costco Warehouse]] - The massive store's structure remains largely intact, suggesting it might still contain valuable supplies.

[[Visit the Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center|Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center]] - A building whose reinforced architecture has helped it weather the decades since the collapse better than its neighbors.

(if: $tacomaKeys and $rested)[
With the Tacoma keys in hand and feeling well-rested, you could potentially drive the truck out of Gallup to continue your journey.

[[Leave Gallup|Leave Gallop]]
](if: $tacomaKeys and not $rested)[
You have the keys to the Tacoma, but your exhaustion could make driving dangerous. 

[[Leave Gallup|Not Rested]]
](else:)[
You'll need to find a functional vehicle and its keys to continue your journey northward.
]


:: Game Over {"position":"600,100","size":"100,100"}
bad ending text

[[Try Again | Menu]]


reset variables


:: I80 {"position":"100,1200","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/tTnzVJZG/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The journey along Interstate 80 takes you through a landscape transformed by decades of environmental change. What was once a major cross-country highway now serves as a record of humanity's gradual retreat—abandoned vehicles positioned like geological markers, their decay tracking the progression of collapse as you move westward.

The road itself tells a story of failed adaptation. In some sections, engineering marvels like bridges and elevated roadways stand intact, testaments to human ingenuity. In others, the highway has been completely reclaimed by natural processes—buckled by frost heaves, eroded by altered weather patterns, or buried beneath vegetation that evolved to thrive in roadside environments.

Wildlife has responded to humanity's absence with remarkable resilience. New migration corridors follow the highway's path, with adapted species using the elevated roadway as protection from predators or environmental hazards. The infrastructure designed to connect human settlements now serves ecological functions its creators never intended.

As you approach Jackson, the transformation becomes even more pronounced. The town appears to have been prepared as a waypoint like Buffalo, but natural reclamation has progressed further here. Buildings peek through vegetation that has evolved to grow with unusual speed and density, creating a hybrid environment where human architecture provides the underlying structure for new ecological systems.

The irony is inescapable—the highway system designed to demonstrate humanity's mastery over continental distances now serves as a trail through territories where human presence has all but vanished. The very infrastructure that facilitated environmental exploitation now provides a path through the consequences of that exploitation, a linear museum of civilization's overreach.

[[Enter Jackson|Enter Jackson]]


:: Investigate Communication Tower {"position":"4825,375","size":"100,100"}
The Communication Tower rises above the Solar Array Field, its slender form bristling with antenna arrays, satellite dishes, and monitoring equipment. Unlike pre-collapse communications infrastructure designed primarily for human information exchange, this system appears built to facilitate machine-to-machine interactions—autonomous systems coordinating without human intermediaries.

Climbing the maintenance ladder to the primary transmission platform, you find a sophisticated relay station that continues to process and transmit data between Alamosa's various automated systems. Status indicators show active connections to the research facilities, power distribution network, and environmental monitoring systems throughout the region.

Most intriguing are the long-range transmission components oriented toward distant coordinates. These powerful directional antennas maintain connections far beyond Alamosa—including one aimed precisely at what your map identifies as Yellowstone. Whatever was established here continues to communicate with systems at your destination, exchanging data for purposes you can only guess at.

See transmissions from Yellowstone—not human communications but machine protocols, regular pulses of data that suggest automated systems there remain functional. The exchanged information appears to include environmental readings, power status updates, and more complex encrypted packages whose purpose is unclear.

The communication tower represents perhaps the most profound form of isolation—systems continuing to exchange information about a world largely devoid of human presence, measuring and analyzing and adjusting without awareness of the absence of those they were designed to serve. The machines speak to one another across hundreds of miles, maintaining connections while the species that created them has largely disappeared.

Yet even here, natural processes have begun integration with the technological. Birds have nested among the antenna arrays, their bodies adapted to tolerate the electromagnetic fields. The tower's foundation has been partially incorporated into the root system of engineered plants that draw power directly from its grounding system. The boundary between the artificial and natural continues to dissolve, not through human design but through the persistent adaptability of life.

[[Return to the solar array field|Solar Array Field]]


:: Investigate the Director's Office {"position":"5475,375","size":"100,100"}
The Director's Office occupies the top floor of the medical center's administrative wing, its large windows offering panoramic views of the transformed landscape beyond Alamosa. Unlike utilitarian research spaces, this office was designed to project authority—expensive furnishings, art on the walls, advanced technology integrated seamlessly into elegant design.

The isolation of leadership is physically embodied in this space, separated from both the subjects of experimentation and the researchers conducting it. From this remove, it would have been easier to view people as data points, their suffering as necessary sacrifice, their autonomy as secondary to perceived greater goods.

As you search the office, you locate the power hub access key (REQUIRED ITEM) and records linking these experiments to the "Yellowstone Initiative." The documents reveal that this facility was one node in a continent-wide program to develop human adaptations suitable for various post-collapse environments, with Yellowstone serving as the integration point for successful candidates.

(set: $powerHubKey to true)

Most revealing is a personal journal belonging to the facility director, its entries showing the psychological evolution that paralleled the physical changes being induced in subjects. Early entries express ethical concerns and commitments to voluntary participation; later ones justify increasing coercion as necessary for human survival; final entries embrace a vision of directed human evolution that completely disregards individual consent.

The last entry, dated days before the apparent evacuation, reads: "The candidates are progressing faster than anticipated. Three subjects have achieved stable adaptation to all test environments. Preparation for transport to Yellowstone is underway. The next phase of humanity will emerge from what we've accomplished here, regardless of the cost. Future generations will understand the necessities that guided our hand."

Nature's response to this hubris is visible through the office windows—the carefully engineered adaptations have escaped containment, merging with wild systems to create hybrid ecosystems that follow their own evolutionary paths, indifferent to the director's grand vision. The authority this space was designed to project has dissolved into irrelevance, its power flowing back into natural systems that never recognized human hierarchies to begin with.

[[Return to the medical center|Rio Grande Medical Center]]


:: Investigate the Power Distribution Hub {"position":"5225,400","size":"100,100"}
The Power Distribution Hub is an industrial facility attached to the Engineering Department, its utilitarian design prioritizing function over aesthetics. Massive transformers and switching equipment fill the main floor, while control systems occupy a glass-enclosed monitoring station overlooking the operation.

Despite decades without human maintenance, automated systems have kept much of the power infrastructure operational. Self-diagnostic programs identify and isolate failures, rerouting power through functioning pathways and adjusting distribution priorities according to pre-programmed hierarchies. The systems were designed with remarkable foresight—accounting for their inventors' absence in their very design.

Locate the main circuit breakers for campus power, but they require a special key to access. Without this key, you can observe but not modify the power distribution network that continues to maintain critical systems throughout the campus.

(if: $powerHubKey)[
Using the key from the medical center, you access the main control panel. This gives you the ability to redirect power to different areas of the campus or to external systems. A schematic shows that the entire power grid is connected to a larger network extending beyond Alamosa—potentially all the way to Yellowstone.

(set: $canOpenGate to true)
](else:)[
You'll need to find a key or access code to control this system. Perhaps it exists somewhere else in Alamosa.
]

What's striking is how the technological systems have adapted to function without human intervention, developing their own balance with the environment. Solar arrays automatically adjust to maximize efficiency as panels degrade. Wind turbines modify their operation based on changing weather patterns. The power grid has evolved its own form of homeostasis—not through biological processes but through algorithmic adaptation.

This technological evolution parallels the biological one occurring throughout the changed world, but with a crucial difference. While natural systems have seamlessly integrated human-engineered components into new balanced ecosystems, these technological systems remain fundamentally separate—maintaining empty buildings and processing data for analyses no one will ever read.

The contradiction is stark: humans created technology to insulate themselves from nature, then tried to engineer their own biology when technology proved insufficient. In their absence, nature simply incorporates both approaches into its processes, indifferent to the original intent. The power continues to flow, supporting systems that monitor empty rooms, while outside, life adapts and continues without regard for human plans.

[[Return to the university campus|Adams State University Research Center]]


:: Investigate the Transport Terminal {"position":"5800,350","size":"100,100"}
The Transport Terminal once served as Alamosa's connection to the outside world—a hub for ground transportation with facilities for emergency airlift operations. Its design reflects the transition from routine civilian travel to crisis evacuation management, with modifications showing how its purpose evolved as the collapse accelerated.

The main concourse has been redesigned for efficient processing of large groups, with automated scanning stations for genetic testing, medical evaluation, and resource allocation assessment. Barriers create one-way flow through the facility, preventing return once categorization was complete. The physical layout itself tells the story of how human movement became increasingly controlled and directed.

Discover evacuation manifests with destinations marked as "Yellowstone Sanctuaries." These records show how individuals were sorted based on genetic profiles, technical skills, and adaptation potential, with only a small percentage designated for sanctuary placement. Others received assignments to "support settlements" or "transitional facilities"—euphemisms for locations with significantly lower long-term survival probability.

The manifests become increasingly selective over time, with final transports limited to individuals with very specific genetic markers or specialized knowledge directly relevant to the sanctuary programs. The percentage of the general population eligible for evacuation to primary sanctuaries dwindled from double digits to fractions of a percent as criteria tightened.

Most revealing are the security protocols implemented in the terminal's final operational phase—armed personnel stationed at key points, containment areas for those who protested their categorization, and psychological management techniques for maintaining order during the selection process. The transportation hub became effectively a processing center for directing human genetic material rather than serving human needs.

Now the facility stands ready for evacuees who will never arrive, its systems powered and maintained by the same automation that preserves other functions throughout Alamosa. Announcement systems still activate on schedule, directing nonexistent travelers through empty security checkpoints toward transportation that no longer arrives or departs—the ultimate expression of designed purpose without possibility of fulfillment.

[[Return to the commercial district|Commercial District]]


:: Investigate the communications room {"position":"375,2350","size":"100,100"}
The communications room occupies a secure section of the building, its infrastructure designed for reliable information exchange even under crisis conditions. Multiple redundant systems—satellite links, hardened landlines, and specialized radio equipment—suggest this facility served as a critical node in the evacuation management network until the final stages of organized response.

Access partial records of evacuation routes with references to "western approach protocols" for Yellowstone. These communications detail the carefully controlled flow of selected individuals through checkpoints like Buffalo, with specific instructions for verifying genetic profiles and specialized knowledge before authorizing continued westward travel.

The progression of message traffic tells the story of collapsing social infrastructure—early communications discuss maintaining public order and processing large population groups, while later ones focus exclusively on tracking specific high-priority individuals designated for sanctuary placement. The transition reflects the narrowing focus from general evacuation to selective preservation.

What's most revealing is how information itself was increasingly restricted and compartmentalized as the crisis deepened. Different classification levels determined who received what information, with complete situational awareness reserved for those at the highest levels of the response hierarchy. Those implementing protocols on the ground operated with increasingly limited context, following directions without full understanding of the broader situation.

The final communications detail the shutdown of Buffalo's processing operations, with instructions for sealing and preserving critical infrastructure for potential future use. Designated individuals were assigned responsibility for completing preservation protocols before departing for their own assigned destinations—some to Yellowstone, others to secondary sites with acknowledged lower survival probabilities.

The isolation created by this information control—different groups operating with different understandings of the same crisis—mirrors the physical isolation that would follow, as human populations became increasingly separated and communication networks failed. The seeds of humanity's fragmentation were planted long before the actual collapse.

[[Return to the company headquarters|Energy Company Headquarters]]


:: Investigate the food storage {"position":"2225,450","size":"100,100"}
The warehouse's massive food storage area once held enough supplies to feed thousands. Most perishables are long gone—consumed or rotted during the initial crisis. What remains are scattered preserved items in specialized packaging designed for extreme long-term storage.

You find educational materials explaining how food preservation methods evolved as the flooding intensified. As traditional agriculture collapsed, techniques were developed to extend shelf life from years to decades. These methods combined ancient preservation knowledge with modern technology—vacuum sealing, radiation treatment, and specialized inert gas environments.

A partially intact display explains how diet needed to adapt to changing environmental conditions, with new nutritional guidelines reflecting both available food sources and the body's changing needs in the altered atmosphere.

You gather some of the still-viable preserved food packets, noting their "Military-Grade Preservation" labels and 100-year shelf life claims. These will supplement your supplies for the journey ahead.

[[Return to the warehouse|Costco Warehouse]]


:: Investigate the hotel offices {"position":"4400,250","size":"100,100"}
The administrative section of the hotel contains offices once used for management, accounting, and event planning. Unlike the public spaces designed to impress guests, these utilitarian rooms offer a more honest glimpse of daily operations.

In the manager's office, you locate a key to the railroad maintenance shed (REQUIRED ITEM). The key hangs on a board alongside others, labeled clearly for staff use. This connection between the hotel and railroad highlights how closely linked different aspects of the town's infrastructure once were—a web of interdependence that unraveled simultaneously when the collapse came.

(set: $maintenanceShedKey to true)

Filing cabinets contain personnel records, maintenance schedules, and financial documents—the administrative backbone of an enterprise that assumed its own continuity. The final months show increasingly desperate measures to maintain normal operations despite supply shortages and staff departures.

A series of memos documents the hotel's transition to an evacuation center, with rooms allocated based on "Categorization Status" rather than ability to pay. The bureaucracy of the old world adapted to serve the apocalypse, maintaining its procedures and paperwork even as the foundations of society crumbled.

What's most revealing is a framed mission statement on the wall, promising "a tradition of excellence for generations to come." The confidence in human permanence—the certainty that there would always be guests to serve and profits to make—now seems like a form of delusion.

The isolation of these offices from the knowledge of their own approaching obsolescence feels almost tragic. While the natural world prepared to adapt and transform, human institutions continued their routines until the very end, unable to imagine their absence from the world they believed they controlled.

[[Return to the hotel entrance|The Strater Hotel]]


:: Investigate the maintenance area {"position":"25,1625","size":"100,100"}
The maintenance area occupies a large service wing of the visitor center, its utilitarian spaces designed to support the transportation infrastructure connecting Jackson to the broader region. During the collapse, these facilities were repurposed from general vehicle maintenance to supporting evacuation operations, and finally to preserving specific transportation capabilities deemed critical for future travel to sanctuary locations.

Discover vehicle modification plans for "high-altitude operation" and "water traversal capability." These technical documents detail how conventional vehicles were adapted to function in the transformed environment—elevated air intakes for areas with atmospheric contaminants, reinforced undercarriages for compromised roadways, and hybrid propulsion systems for crossing terrain where conventional fuel might be unavailable.

The modification specifications become increasingly specialized as the crisis deepened—early adaptations addressing general environmental challenges, later ones tailored to specific routes and conditions known to exist along sanctuary approaches. The final designs focus exclusively on the journey to Yellowstone, with detailed modifications for navigating the particular environmental hazards documented along that path.

Most revealing is how the maintenance priorities shifted as resources dwindled—from supporting general mobility throughout the region to preserving specific travel capabilities along designated evacuation routes, and finally to maintaining only the minimal infrastructure necessary for future travelers making the journey to primary sanctuaries.

The maintenance facilities now stand as monuments to technological adaptation in the face of environmental transformation—the tools and equipment necessary for modifying standard transportation preserved alongside the knowledge required to implement those modifications. The preservation suggests an understanding that future travelers would need to adapt their means of transport to conditions that would continue to evolve long after organized operations ceased.

[[Return to the visitor center|Visitor Center & Transportation Hub]]


:: Investigate the modern gallery {"position":"2175,775","size":"100,100"}
The modern gallery was added during the climate crisis years, documenting the transformation of the landscape and human responses to it. Art pieces depict the changing environment—from the familiar deserts of the Southwest to the altered landscapes that emerged as weather patterns shifted dramatically.

Striking paintings show the transformation of what was once desert into a strange new environment—some areas becoming partial seascapes as water levels rose, others developing unique crystalline formations as ancient salt deposits were exposed and reshaped by unusual weather patterns.

Photographs document the evacuation of coastal cities, the establishment of inland refugee centers, and the gradual adaptation of survivors to their new reality. The images grow progressively more unsettling, showing subtle physical changes in people—enhanced breathing structures, modified skin pigmentation, altered proportions—all apparently developments that emerged (or were engineered) in response to the changed environment.

A timeline traces the progression from "natural adaptation" to "guided evolution," with Yellowstone marked as the culmination of this process—a controlled environment where "humanity's next chapter" would unfold, protected from the worst extremes of the transformed world outside.

The final installation is a sealed display case containing what appears to be a modified human genome sequence, labeled "Homo sapiens adaptus—Yellowstone Variant." A note indicates this represents the "stabilized adaptation profile" developed for long-term survival in the new environmental conditions.

[[Return to the museum|Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center]]


:: Investigate the outdoor outfitter {"position":"750,2350","size":"100,100"}
The outdoor outfitter occupies a large space at the plaza's edge, its inventory focused on equipment for wilderness survival and navigation. During the evacuation, this facility was repurposed from recreational retail to practical support for those traversing increasingly hostile environments, and ultimately to a preservation site for specialized equipment needed by future travelers to Yellowstone.

Locate equipment designed for the "altered Yellowstone environment," specialized gear adapted for conditions that didn't exist before the collapse. The modifications reflect specific challenges anticipated along the route and at the final destination—higher radiation levels, altered atmospheric composition, unstable geological activity, and transformed ecosystems.

Protective clothing designed to shield against environmental hazards hangs alongside monitoring equipment for detecting threats invisible to human senses. Navigation tools calibrated for regions where magnetic fields have been disrupted by geological changes rest beside communication devices designed to function without broader network infrastructure. Each item represents adaptation to specific aspects of the transformed world.

Most revealing are the evolutionary trajectories visible in the equipment designs—early versions focusing on temporary protection from adverse conditions, later ones assuming permanent exposure and prioritizing sustainable operation in the new normal. The progression mirrors the broader shift from believing the environmental changes could be reversed to accepting them as the permanent reality requiring biological and technological adaptation.

Field manuals preserved alongside the equipment document the changing conditions around Yellowstone specifically, with detailed information about the transformed ecosystem and potential hazards. The precision of these materials suggests extensive monitoring continued even as general infrastructure collapsed, with data collection focused increasingly on the sanctuary areas as resources dwindled.

The isolation assumed for users of this equipment—travelers moving alone through an environment hostile to conventional human biology—is built into every design aspect. Self-sufficiency, durability, and operation without technical support became the primary engineering considerations, anticipating a world where mutual aid could no longer be assumed and individual survival depended entirely on preparation and adaptation.

[[Return to the plaza|Mountain View Plaza]]


:: Investigate the rail yard {"position":"3600,350","size":"100,100"}
The rail yard extends behind the main depot, a complex of tracks, switches, and service buildings once bustling with activity. Nature has begun the slow process of reclaiming this industrial space—pioneer species crack through concrete, metal rusts, and wooden structures succumb to rot and insect activity.

Among the collection of railcars parked on side tracks, you discover one that stands out—a passenger car converted into a mobile laboratory. Heavy modifications include solar panels, reinforced windows, and specialized ventilation systems.

Inside, the laboratory car contains sophisticated equipment for environmental monitoring and biological testing. Logbooks indicate it was used for "Subject Tracking and Retrieval" operations throughout the region, with coded references to "adaptation candidates" and "genetic viability assessments."

The final entries describe increasing difficulty maintaining contact with the "Central Adaptation Authority" as communication networks failed. The team apparently abandoned the mobile lab after running out of critical supplies, attempting to reach Yellowstone on foot with their most promising test subjects.

This discovery adds another piece to the puzzle—it appears that even as organized evacuation broke down, scientific teams continued working to identify individuals with genetic profiles suited to whatever program awaited at Yellowstone. The effort to shape humanity's future continued even as its present collapsed around it.

The laboratory stands as testament to the priorities that emerged in crisis—not saving everyone, but selecting who would represent humanity in whatever came next. The ethical implications of such choices hang heavy in the abandoned space, questions with no one left to answer for them.

[[Return to the railroad depot|Durango & Silverton Railroad Depot]]


:: Investigate the testing chambers {"position":"1000,1500","size":"100,100"}
The testing chambers occupy a secure wing of the energy facility, their specialized environments designed for evaluating experimental technologies under controlled conditions. During the collapse, these installations were repurposed from developing incremental improvements to conventional energy systems to testing radical new approaches better suited to the transformed environment.

Discover apparatus designed to measure "adaptation potential" in human subjects. These technologies appear to have evolved from environmental monitoring systems, modified to assess specific biological responses to changing conditions—respiratory efficiency in altered atmospheric composition, metabolic adjustments to different energy sources, and cellular resilience against increased radiation.

The testing equipment shows progression from conventional medical monitoring to increasingly specialized measurement of adaptive responses—early systems tracking general health metrics, later ones focusing on specific mutation markers, and final configurations designed to identify and quantify traits deemed advantageous for survival in the transformed world.

What's most disturbing is how these chambers transitioned from technological testing to human testing—the infrastructure for evaluating energy systems repurposed to evaluate human adaptation potential. The final configuration suggests these facilities served as assessment centers for candidates being considered for sanctuary placement, with admission priority given to those showing promising adaptive responses.

The testing protocols preserved in associated documentation reveal increasingly invasive procedures as the crisis deepened—initial assessments based on non-invasive monitoring, later evaluations requiring biological sampling, and final protocols involving deliberate exposure to environmental stressors to measure adaptive potential under duress.

The chambers now stand as monuments to how thoroughly scientific ethics were abandoned as survival prospects narrowed—human subjects increasingly treated as biological material to be measured and categorized rather than individuals with inherent rights and dignity. The sophisticated equipment preserved here represents the technological capacity for precise measurement turned toward determining which humans deserved the opportunity to continue existing as the broader population was effectively abandoned.

[[Return to the energy facility|Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility]]


:: Investigate tourist shops {"position":"3950,375","size":"100,100"}
The tourist shops along Main Avenue stand as hollow reminders of a time when this mountain town's primary identity was as a destination rather than a place of practical habitation. Faded signs advertise authentic local experiences that were anything but authentic—commodified versions of frontier life packaged for visitors who came and went without understanding the land.

Inside one relatively intact shop, you discover maps marking "Safe Routes to Highland Sanctuaries" with Yellowstone prominently featured. These weren't typical tourist maps but evacuation guidance created during the early crisis years, disguised as recreational information to prevent panic.

The maps confirm what you've already pieced together—Yellowstone was designated as a primary sanctuary location, with specific routes maintained longer than others during the collapse. Annotations indicate "genetic screening checkpoints" along the way, supporting the theory that only selected individuals were permitted at these final refuges.

Amidst the worthless souvenirs and trinkets—the plastic representations of natural wonders that outlast the wonders themselves—you find these pragmatic survival tools. The irony isn't lost on you; the shop once sold illusions of the natural world to people who were actively destroying it, and in its abandonment, it finally served a purpose connected to actual survival.

Outside, the real mountains stand indifferent to human memory or meaning, their geology operating on timescales that make our civilizational collapse merely a moment. They will remain when even these ruins have been fully reabsorbed into the landscape.

[[Return to Main Avenue|Main Avenue]]


:: Investigation Inside {"position":"1275,100","size":"100,100"}
After some effort, you manage to force open a rear window and climb into the farmhouse. The interior is dusty but surprisingly intact—this place wasn't looted as thoroughly as most buildings from the collapse era.

The rooms tell the story of a family that prepared for disaster. Shelves are stocked with containers that once held preserved food. Water filtration systems have been installed throughout the home. Maps on the walls show the changing geography as waters rose elsewhere in the country.

In what appears to be an office, you find journals documenting the family's struggle to maintain the farm as conditions worsened. The final entries describe a government evacuation order and debate about whether to leave or stay.

Inside a desk drawer, you find a small metal key with unusual teeth. A tag attached to it reads "Irrigation Control."

(set: $farmKey to true)

You pocket the key, suspecting it might be related to the machine you saw outside.

[[Return to the farm exterior|Abandoned Farm]]


:: Jackson Options {"position":"700,1200","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Bv9ZGwT5/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Jackson stands before you as a carefully prepared waypoint on your journey to Yellowstone. Unlike the random patterns of abandonment seen elsewhere, this town shows evidence of deliberate preparation for future travelers—specific buildings preserved, information concentrated, and resources cached in accessible locations.

This selective preservation creates a profound sense of isolation—not just physical solitude, but temporal isolation from both the civilization that built this place and whatever awaits at your destination. You stand between worlds, guided by the foresight of people long gone toward a future they could only imagine.

The preserved structures seem to have been chosen specifically for their relevance to your journey. Administrative buildings contain navigational information, research facilities hold data about environmental conditions, and transportation infrastructure has been maintained just enough to facilitate westward movement toward Yellowstone.

What's most unsettling is how precisely this preparation seems targeted to your needs. As if those who established this waypoint knew exactly what information and resources you would require decades later, suggesting a level of planning that transcends normal emergency response. Your journey feels less like exploration and more like following a predetermined path—raising questions about your own role in whatever plan was set in motion before the collapse.

Four key locations have been clearly marked as significant:

[[Visit the Johnson County Courthouse|Johnson County Courthouse]] - The administrative center that might contain official information.

[[Explore the Visitor Center & Transportation Hub|Visitor Center & Transportation Hub]] - A facility designed to orient travelers and facilitate movement.

[[Investigate the Agricultural Research Station|Agricultural Research Station]] - A scientific facility studying adaptation to changed conditions.

[[Examine the Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility|Bighorn Energy Conversion Facility]] - An industrial complex that powered the region.

[[Assess your readiness to continue|Check if good to go J]] - Consider whether you've gathered enough information to proceed toward Yellowstone.


:: Jicarilla Apache Nation Reservation {"position":"2750,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/k42bhHqQ/h.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Toyota Tacoma handles the challenging terrain well as you journey north from Gallup. The landscape gradually transforms around you—the barren expanses giving way to more rugged highland areas where pockets of resilient vegetation have established themselves in the altered climate.

As you enter what was once the Jicarilla Apache Nation Reservation, you notice signs of more recent human presence than anywhere you've encountered so far. Stone markers bearing indigenous symbols line the road at intervals, seemingly placed there deliberately to guide travelers.

The air feels different here—cleaner somehow, with a crispness that suggests higher elevation. In the distance, you spot what appears to be carefully managed forest areas where new growth has been encouraged, unlike the devastated timberlands you passed earlier.

Near a flowing stream, you notice a small garden of plants you don't recognize—species that appear to have been deliberately cultivated, perhaps adaptations of traditional medicinal plants modified to thrive in the changed environment.

There are no people visible, but the feeling of being observed follows you as you drive deeper into the reservation. The well-maintained road and subtle signs of stewardship suggest that some organized community might still exist here, operating unseen but vigilant.

As you approach a junction where the road splits toward either Durango or Alamosa, you find a stone cairn with two symbols carved into separate rocks: a spiraling path leading west, and a rising sun to the east. A choice lies before you, each path leading toward Yellowstone but through dramatically different territories.

[[Examine the junction more closely|Decision Point 1]]


:: Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum {"position":"100,2100","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/vTNM2P8V/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum occupies a substantial stone building near the center of town, its architecture designed to preserve history long before anyone understood how complete that preservation would need to be. The structure has weathered the decades of abandonment remarkably well, with only minimal damage from the elements.

Inside, the museum presents a striking contrast—exhibits about the regional past displayed alongside hastily assembled information about the collapse and evacuation. Glass cases containing Native American artifacts and pioneer memorabilia stand beside emergency bulletins and evacuation maps. The juxtaposition creates an unintended exhibit about humanity's hubris—our assumption that our history would continue indefinitely alongside evidence of its abrupt curtailment.

Most revealing are the final additions to the museum—a series of displays documenting Buffalo's role as an "Eastern Processing Station" for candidates heading to Yellowstone. Maps, photographs, and explanatory text detail how this town served as a checkpoint where individuals were evaluated, categorized, and either directed onward or diverted to "alternative settlement locations" with significantly lower survival probabilities.

The museum has become a monument to how quickly priorities shifted as the crisis deepened—from preserving cultural heritage to preserving specific human genetic lineages. The final exhibits were clearly assembled with the understanding that they might someday serve as explanation to survivors like yourself, traveling this path years after those who created these displays had departed.

[[Explore the main room|Explore the main room]] - Examine the central exhibits about Buffalo's history and its final role.
[[Check the historical maps collection|Check the historical maps collection]] - Look for detailed geographic information about routes to Yellowstone.
[[Examine the local history displays|Examine the local history displays]] - Learn about Buffalo's transition during the collapse.
[[Search the archive room|Search the archive room]] - Investigate the museum's research materials.

[[Return to town center|Buffalo Options]] - Explore other areas of Buffalo.


:: Johnson County Courthouse {"position":"350,1375","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/SxzQ3pMN/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Johnson County Courthouse stands at the center of Jackson, its imposing stone architecture designed to project governmental authority and permanence. Now it presides over emptiness, its formal façade overlooking streets reclaimed by vegetation evolved to thrive in the altered climate.

Unlike many government buildings that were stripped of useful materials during the collapse, this facility appears to have been deliberately preserved—its infrastructure maintained, its information systems protected from degradation, and its physical structure reinforced against environmental damage.

Inside, the public spaces show evidence of repurposing during the final phase of organized response. Courtrooms were converted to briefing centers, administrative offices transformed into data processing facilities, and archives repurposed to preserve information deemed crucial for future recovery or adaptation.

The preservation effort focused specifically on geographical and administrative information related to Yellowstone—maps, land surveys, emergency protocols, and governmental directives. The selection suggests this facility was maintained not to preserve general governmental function but to serve as an information waypoint for travelers like yourself, moving toward Yellowstone years after general evacuation ceased.

The isolation of formal government structures from the human communities they were designed to serve creates an unsettling emptiness—systems of authority and administration preserved in perfect detail but rendered meaningless without the social context that gave them purpose and legitimacy.

[[Search the county records|Search the county records]] - Examine land and property documentation.
[[Explore the emergency management office|Explore the emergency management office]] - Investigate crisis response facilities.
[[Check the sheriff's department|Check the sheriff's department]] - Look through law enforcement resources.
[[Access the basement archives|Access the basement archives]] - Investigate historical documentation.

[[Return to town center|Jackson Options]] - Explore other areas of Jackson.


:: Leave Durango {"position":"4150,650","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/tTdjyqqp/i.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With your Tacoma repaired and supplies restocked, you prepare to leave Durango behind. As you drive through the empty streets one last time, you're struck by how quickly a place designed entirely for human purposes loses its meaning in human absence.

The buildings stand as monuments to what we valued—commerce, entertainment, historical preservation—but without people to assign and maintain these meanings, they revert to mere arrangements of materials, slowly being reclaimed and redistributed by natural processes indifferent to human intention.

Following your map, you head north out of town. The road winds through increasingly rugged terrain, the pavement deteriorating until it becomes little more than a suggestion of the route. Your modified Tacoma handles the challenges well, its newly repaired systems functioning as intended.

As you climb higher into the mountains, something about the landscape ahead seems strange—not quite matching what the map suggests should be there. A low mist hugs the valley floor, obscuring details, but there's a quality to it that feels unnatural.

The further you drive, the thicker the mist becomes, until visibility is reduced to just a few feet ahead. Strange shapes loom at the edges of your perception, never quite resolving into recognizable forms. The air takes on an odd, metallic taste.

As you consider turning back, a sudden wave of dizziness washes over you. Your grip on the steering wheel loosens as your vision tunnels. The last thing you register is a strange sensation of movement that doesn't match the vehicle's progress—as if reality itself is shifting around you.

Then you feel the world go to black...

[[Wake Up|Maze Entrance]]


:: Leave Eagar {"position":"950,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/MHkSJc2s/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With the map showing the route north, the solar charging unit, and the ATV ignition chip all secured, you return to the abandoned gas station where you found the electric ATV. The vehicle represents your best chance at making the journey to Yellowstone.

You connect the solar charging unit to the ATV's battery system and place it in direct sunlight. After several hours, the charge indicators show enough power to begin your journey. You insert the ignition chip, and the ATV's systems come to life with a soft hum—the sound of possibility in a silent world.

Loading your gathered supplies securely onto the vehicle, you take one last look at what remains of Eagar. This place preserved you until you awakened, but it holds no answers about your past or your purpose. Those, you hope, await at Yellowstone.

The map indicates your next destination should be Fence Lake, New Mexico—a settlement that may offer additional supplies and information for your journey. With the sun still high in the sky, you set out, leaving Eagar and its mysteries behind.

The ATV handles the rough terrain well, its electric motor nearly silent as you navigate out of town and onto what once was a highway north. The transformed landscape stretches before you—beautiful and terrible in its alien familiarity.

[[Begin your journey to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Intro]]


:: Leave Fence Lake {"position":"1600,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/PrpwM92T/g.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With your ATV recharged using the new solar panel and the gas can secured to your cargo rack, you're ready to continue your journey north. According to your map, Gallup, New Mexico lies ahead—a larger settlement that might hold more substantial resources and information.

You take one final look at Fence Lake. The abandoned buildings standing amid the barren landscape tell a story of adaptation and ultimate failure—except for that strange automated house, still maintaining itself for occupants who never returned.

As you drive away, the evidence you've gathered so far circles in your mind. The patterns become clearer with each discovery: selective evacuation based on genetic criteria, adaptation initiatives centered around Yellowstone, and increasingly desperate measures as the climate crisis accelerated beyond control.

Whatever awaits you at Yellowstone, it seems less like a natural sanctuary and more like something deliberately engineered—perhaps humanity's last organized attempt to secure its future.

The ATV hums quietly beneath you as you follow the faded highway north, the terrain gradually changing from flat, cracked earth to more rugged landscape. The journey to Gallup will take several hours, giving you time to process what you've learned and prepare for whatever lies ahead.

[[Continue to Gallup|Gallup]]


:: Leave Gallop {"position":"2525,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/hGPxVjY3/g.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With the Toyota Tacoma keys in hand and feeling well-rested, you're ready to continue your journey north. You return to the mechanic shop and lower the truck from its hydraulic lift, checking its systems one final time before departure.

The vehicle runs remarkably well considering its age—a testament to both its rugged design and the careful modifications made to help it survive in the post-collapse world. Its expanded fuel tanks and reinforced construction should serve you well on the challenging road ahead.

According to your map, your next destination is the Jicarilla Apache Nation Reservation—once sovereign tribal land, now one of the few areas where organized communities might still exist. From there, you'll need to make a critical decision about your route toward Yellowstone.

As you drive away from Gallup, the pieces of your fragmented memory and the information you've gathered begin to form a clearer picture. Yellowstone was transformed into something far beyond a simple sanctuary—it appears to be the site of humanity's "controlled evolutionary leap forward," where specially selected individuals were gathered to adapt to Earth's new reality.

The question remains: were you sent there as part of this plan, or are you making this journey of your own free will? And what will you find when you finally reach your destination?

The truck's engine hums steadily as you follow the deteriorating highway north, leaving Gallup and its remnants of the old world behind.

[[Continue to Jicarilla Apache Nation Reservation|Jicarilla Apache Nation Reservation]]



:: Leave for Yellowstone {"position":"1600,1500","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/g0Hpbckd/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With the knowledge gained, you're finally ready for the last leg of your journey to Yellowstone. The mysterious sanctuary has evolved from rumor to definite purpose in your mind, and your own role—not a random survivor but a deliberately engineered being, "Subject Y-3742"—has become increasingly clear.

You gather your supplies and check your equipment one final time. The maps recovered from various waypoints have given you a clear route, and the medical supplies and specialized gear will help you navigate the increasingly strange environment between here and your destination.

The isolation that has defined your journey takes on new meaning now. You understand that your solitude wasn't accidental but designed—your path predetermined, your encounters with these preserved waypoints carefully arranged decades before you began your journey. Even your memories were engineered, suppressed to ensure you would follow the intended path without the burden of fully understanding what you are.

As you set out on the final stretch, the transformed landscape seems to mirror your internal state. Neither fully natural nor entirely artificial, both you and the environment represent hybrid states—deliberate interventions into natural processes creating something unprecedented. The genetic modifications detailed in your recovered file have apparently prepared you specifically for whatever awaits at Yellowstone.

In the distance, the mountains rise against the horizon—unchanged in their basic form but altered in their details, with vegetation patterns that would have been impossible in the pre-collapse world. They stand as monuments to the planet's indifference—geographical features that have witnessed civilizations rise and fall, continuing their slow geological evolution regardless of what humans build or destroy upon their flanks.

The road ahead winds through terrain where human infrastructure has been increasingly subsumed by new growth. The irony is inescapable—the species clever enough to transform an entire planet's climate proved incapable of surviving the very changes they triggered, leaving behind only these carefully preserved breadcrumbs and whatever awaits at Yellowstone.

[[Continue toward Yellowstone|Roads to Yellowstone]]


:: Long Drive {"position":"5700,650","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/QMrYDpHK/h.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With Alamosa behind you and the power systems redirected toward Yellowstone, you continue your journey north along Highway 285. The Tacoma performs well on the deteriorated roadway, its adaptive systems compensating for challenging terrain as you climb into higher elevations.

The landscape grows increasingly strange as you progress. Engineered organisms have established themselves along the highway corridor—plants with circuit-like patterns in their leaves collecting solar energy, fungal networks that appear to communicate through luminescent pulses, insects with modified physiologies serving ecological functions that never existed before the collapse.

Evidence of the final evacuation is visible along the route—abandoned vehicles at rest stops, emergency supply caches long since emptied, and occasionally, the remains of those who didn't complete the journey. These silent monuments to the human exodus become more frequent as you approach an area where Highway 285 descends into a valley that your map doesn't show.

As you crest a final ridge, you're confronted with a sight that stops you cold. The valley below, which should contain the continued highway route according to your map, is instead filled with water—a massive lake where no lake existed before. The roadway descends directly into its depths, emerging on the far shore miles away.

This inland sea was clearly not a planned reservoir—the partially submerged remains of structures and infrastructure indicate a catastrophic flooding event that transformed the regional geography after your map was created. Whether from dam failures, altered precipitation patterns, or some more deliberate engineering gone awry, this body of water now blocks the direct route to your destination.

You pull the Tacoma to a stop at a viewpoint overlooking this unexpected obstacle, considering your options. As you scan the horizon, something about the water's surface catches your attention—a strange pattern of movement near the center, as if the entire body of water is being affected by some massive force beneath.

Before you can fully process what you're seeing, the distant water begins to rise—not gradually, but in a massive wall that defies natural behavior. A wave is forming, impossibly large and moving with unnatural coordination, as if the water itself has developed a collective intelligence. It surges toward the shore where you've stopped, gaining height and momentum with each passing second.

You barely have time to process the impossibility of what you're witnessing—a tsunami hundreds of miles from any ocean—before making a desperate decision.

[[Brace for impact|Flood]]


:: Look Inside barn {"position":"1600,200","size":"100,100"}
The barn's interior is dimly lit by shafts of sunlight penetrating through gaps in the roof. Farm equipment stands covered in dust—much of it modified to operate in the changed climate conditions.

Among the tools and machinery, you find journals and technical diagrams. The pages describe how some regions paradoxically dried out despite global flooding, due to disrupted weather patterns and ocean currents. What was once the humid eastern United States became a desert, while parts of the Southwest experienced unprecedented rainfall before ultimately drying out completely.

The notes detail the farm owner's desperate attempts to adapt irrigation systems to the volatile conditions. References to "water wars" and "corporate resource seizures" suggest that conflict over remaining fertile land intensified as the crisis deepened.

This adds another piece to the puzzle—it seems access to Yellowstone was restricted based on some kind of genetic criteria.

[[Return to the farm exterior|Abandoned Farm]]


:: Look at Machine {"position":"1425,100","size":"100,100"}
You approach the tarp-covered machine and carefully pull away the covering. Beneath is a sophisticated piece of equipment that appears to be some kind of automated wildlife management system—a combination of water dispenser, feed station, and what might be monitoring equipment.

The control panel bears the logo of "AgriGen Adaptation Technologies" and is secured with a keyhole that matches the shape of the key you found in the farmhouse.

(if: $farmKey)[
The key from the farmhouse should fit this lock.

[[Use the key on the machine|use machine]]
](else:)[
You'll need to find a key to activate this system.
]

[[Return to the farm exterior|Abandoned Farm]]


:: Look through children's room {"position":"0,750","size":"100,100"}
The collapsed wall has left this child's bedroom exposed to the elements, creating an eerie tableau of abandoned innocence. Faded cartoon characters peel from the walls, and broken toys lie scattered among the debris.

What draws your attention is a series of educational materials—books, posters, and a small tablet device long since drained of power. These items offer glimpses of how the world changed in the years leading up to the final collapse.

A geography textbook shows dramatically altered coastlines, with most of Florida and significant portions of the eastern seaboard submerged beneath rising oceans. The western coast has similarly retreated, though the highlands remain. Major cities are marked as "Submerged," "Partially Submerged," or "Abandoned."

A science poster illustrates the "New North American Climate Zones," showing desertification across much of the former United States, new inland seas where Great Plains once stretched, and expanded "Habitable Highland Regions" including areas around Yellowstone.

These materials seem designed to educate children born after the initial catastrophes, teaching them about a world already transformed rather than what was lost.

[[Continue exploring the residential area|Residential Area]]


:: Look under the lift {"position":"2375,200","size":"100,100"}
You carefully maneuver under the suspended Tacoma, wary of the ancient hydraulic system that holds it aloft. The underside of the vehicle confirms its extensive modifications—reinforced skid plates, protected fuel lines, and what appears to be a secondary fuel tank for extended range.

In the shadows beneath the lift, you notice something that doesn't belong with the mechanical equipment—a small waterproof container tucked into a recess in the floor. Opening it reveals a journal wrapped in protective plastic.

The journal belongs to Martinez, apparently the mechanic assigned to the Tacoma. The entries describe increasing pressure from authorities to complete the vehicle preparations, alongside growing rumors about what was happening at the Yellowstone sanctuary.

The most interesting entry reads: "Finished the Tacoma today. It's beyond spec now—could make it all the way to Canada if needed. Suarez from the evacuation office let something slip about Yellowstone. Said it's not just a sanctuary—it's some kind of 'controlled evolutionary leap forward.' They've built a self-sustaining biodome there, but only for people with specific genetic markers. The rest of us are just out of luck, I guess. Unless I take matters into my own hands. The truck is ready. I have supplies. If I can just get past the checkpoints..."

The final entry is hurried: "They're closing the northern routes tonight. It's now or never. Keys in my room at the Sands. If anyone finds this and I didn't make it—the truck's yours. Good luck."

[[Return to the mechanic shop|Abandoned Mechanic Shop]]


:: Main Avenue {"position":"3900,125","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/rwzMW2JN/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Main Avenue stretches before you, its once-bustling sidewalks now cracked and sprouting with resilient alpine plants that have evolved to thrive in the altered climate. Shop windows are mostly shattered, their displays long since looted or decayed. Yet the buildings themselves remain, monuments to a civilization that thought itself permanent.

What strikes you most is the silence. Places designed for human commerce and socialization now stand as hollow shells, serving no purpose except as shelter for small creatures and climbing surfaces for persistent vegetation. An elegant balance has been restored—the town is returning to the earth from which its materials were extracted.

Strange flowering vines have colonized much of the street, their biology clearly altered from pre-collapse species. They produce brilliant blue blossoms that seem to track the sun with uncanny precision. Nature's innovations continue, indifferent to humanity's absence.

Several locations along the avenue might be worth exploring:

[[Check the auto parts store|Explore the auto parts store]] - A small shop specializing in off-road vehicle components.

[[Visit the hardware store|Check the old hardware store]] - A traditional hardware store that might contain tools needed for repairs.

[[Look through abandoned vehicles|Search abandoned vehicles]] - Several vehicles remain parked along the street, potentially containing useful items.

[[Browse the tourist shops|Investigate tourist shops]] - Once-bustling souvenir stores that might contain maps or information.

[[Study the historical displays|Examine street museum displays]] - Public exhibits about Durango's history that were designed to withstand exposure to the elements.

[[Return to town center|Durango Options]] - Explore other areas of Durango.


:: Maze Entrance {"position":"6700,0","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/x8tVJ6NZ/j.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Consciousness returns slowly, like swimming up from deep water. Your head throbs with a dull ache as you blink awake in the driver's seat of your Tacoma. Disorientation grips you—the last thing you remember was driving through that strange mist north of Durango, and then... nothing.

Now you find yourself parked in a dense forest unlike anything you've seen before. Ancient trees with trunks wider than your vehicle stretch skyward, their canopy so thick that only occasional shafts of sunlight penetrate to the forest floor. The quality of light is strange—not quite the yellow-white of normal sunlight, but tinged with an amber hue that casts everything in an otherworldly glow.

Your navigation system is dead, the screen displaying only static. When you check your map, you find it doesn't match any of the terrain visible through your windshield. Nothing about this place corresponds to where you should be.

Stepping out of the truck, you're struck by the silence. No birds call, no insects buzz—just the occasional rustle of leaves in a breeze you can barely feel. The air smells of pine and something else, something metallic and unfamiliar.

Paths lead in different directions through the trees, none of them wide enough for your vehicle. You'll have to continue on foot, at least until you can determine where you are and how to get back on route to Yellowstone.

The forest stretches in all directions, identical and maze-like. You feel a strange certainty that finding your way through this place is now your only option forward.

[[Enter the forest maze|Start Maze]]


:: Maze2 Entrance {"position":"7400,0","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/fLzHtD7w/j.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Consciousness returns slowly, accompanied by a throbbing pain at your temple. You open your eyes to find yourself slumped in the driver's seat of your Tacoma—somehow intact despite the crushing force of the wave that should have destroyed it. The dashboard lights glow faintly, casting an eerie blue illumination across the interior.

Outside the windows, darkness is broken only by strange bioluminescent growths that cling to towering canyon walls rising hundreds of feet on either side. The narrow passage ahead is just wide enough for your vehicle, winding between sheer cliffs of black stone veined with glowing minerals that pulse with a rhythm suggesting organization rather than random distribution.

You check your navigation system, but it displays only static, occasionally resolving into geometric patterns similar to those you glimpsed within the wave before losing consciousness. Your map is equally useless—this maze of canyons doesn't appear on any charts you possess.

When you step outside to get your bearings, the air feels dense and carries an unusual metallic scent. The ground beneath your feet is unnaturally level for a canyon floor, almost like a constructed roadway. As you scan your surroundings, your light catches a reflection from a small metal object partially buried in the soil.

Kneeling, you uncover what appears to be a warning marker with a universal symbol for explosive hazards. Beneath it, etched in precise letters: "BOUNDARY ENFORCEMENT PROTOCOL ACTIVE — NAVIGATIONAL DEVIATION RESULTS IN COUNTERMEASURE DEPLOYMENT."

The message is clear—this canyon system is rigged with explosives to ensure travelers stick to designated paths. Whether this was implemented during the evacuation to direct refugees or after the collapse to channel adapted organisms remains unclear, but the danger is very real.

Back in your Tacoma, you start the engine, which hums to life with surprising reliability. The canyon stretches before you, a maze of passages through which you must navigate while avoiding the deadly countermeasures buried beneath the surface.

[[Begin navigating the canyon maze|Start Maze2]]


:: Menu {"position":"300,100","size":"100,100"}
(b4r:"solid")+(b4r-colour:yellow)[(text-colour:yellow)[''Journey to Yellowstone'']]
By Ryan Godburn





























[[Start|Story Start]]


:: Modern House {"position":"1425,425","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/TwdbqPH4/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Unlike the other buildings in Fence Lake, this structure appears to have been maintained long after the others were abandoned. It's a single-story prefabricated building with reinforced walls, intact windows, and a sophisticated array of solar panels on its roof.

As you approach, you notice security cameras mounted at the corners—though whether they're still functioning is unclear. The front door has an electronic lock with a keypad. 

Unlike the decay evident elsewhere, this building seems almost pristine, as if someone—or something—has been maintaining it. You hear mechanical sounds coming from inside, like automated systems still operating.

[[Try to enter the house|Step Inside]]
[[Check around the back|Check Backside]]
[[Return to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Options]]


:: Mountain View Plaza {"position":"800,2100","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/2jcz55dS/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Mountain View Plaza occupies what was once the commercial heart of Buffalo—a shopping center designed to serve the daily needs and wants of a functioning consumer society. Now it stands as a study in repurposed infrastructure, its spaces transformed from commerce to survival operations during the final phases of organized evacuation.

Retail shops were converted to supply distribution centers, restaurants repurposed as mass feeding stations, and entertainment venues transformed into temporary housing for evacuees awaiting processing. The physical alterations tell the story of rapidly shifting priorities as the crisis deepened—decorative elements removed to create more functional space, luxury goods replaced by survival essentials, consumer choice giving way to centralized allocation.

Most striking is how completely the commercial purpose of this place was abandoned as collapse approached. The corporate branding and marketing materials that once dominated the visual landscape were systematically removed, replaced with utilitarian signage directing evacuation operations. The transition reflects the ultimate irrelevance of consumer capitalism when facing existential threat—the supposed permanence of market economies revealed as entirely contingent on environmental stability.

The plaza now stands in a state of suspended animation—not collapsed or decayed but deliberately preserved, its spaces arranged to serve future travelers following the same path you now take. The isolation of this place from its original purpose creates an unsettling liminality—neither fully the commercial center it was built to be nor entirely the emergency facility it became in its final active phase.

[[Check the bookstore|Check the bookstore]] - Examine what might have been a travel information point.
[[Explore the general store|Explore the general store]] - Look for supplies useful for your journey.
[[Search the pharmacy|Search the pharmacy]] - Investigate potential medical resources.
[[Investigate the outdoor outfitter|Investigate the outdoor outfitter]] - Check for specialized equipment.

[[Return to town center|Buffalo Options]] - Explore other areas of Buffalo.


:: Museum of Mountain Living {"position":"4400,400","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Zqdm7PX9/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Museum of Mountain Living occupies a purpose-built structure on the edge of town, its architecture blending traditional mountain aesthetics with modern reinforcement. Unlike many buildings, it shows signs of having been intentionally preserved during the early crisis years—perhaps deemed culturally significant enough to warrant protection even as resources grew scarce.

The museum was originally dedicated to documenting human adaptation to mountain environments throughout history—from indigenous practices through frontier settlement to modern recreation. This focus on adaptation took on new significance during the climate collapse, and exhibits appear to have been updated to address the accelerating changes until very near the end.

Inside, the space is divided between the main gallery focused on historical adaptation, a research annex dedicated to contemporary studies, and a mechanical room that housed the building's systems. Many artifacts remain in their display cases, protected from the elements and casual scavenging by security measures that outlasted their designers.

[[Explore the main exhibit hall|Explore the main gallery]] - Examine the historical displays about mountain living.
[[Visit the research section|Search the research annex]] - Investigate scientific studies conducted near the end.
[[Check the building's mechanical systems|Check the mechanical room]] - Look for useful components or tools.

[[Return to town center|Durango Options]] - Explore other areas of Durango.


:: Not Rested {"position":"2350,25","size":"100,100"}
fell asleep while driving, crashed, and died.

[[Game Over]] 


:: Open Gate {"position":"4850,600","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/nhmSNdct/g.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With both the power hub key and specialized maintenance tools, you're able to access the Solar Array Field's primary control systems and redirect power to the external transmission node. The process requires careful calibration—these systems were designed to operate under human supervision, not to be modified by a lone traveler decades after installation.

As you complete the final authorization sequence, massive switches engage beneath the array field. You feel the vibration through the control room floor as conduits long dormant begin carrying power to systems beyond Alamosa. Status displays show energy flowing toward the northwest—directly aligned with Yellowstone's position on your map.

The communications array activates simultaneously, establishing a stronger connection with whatever systems still function at your destination. Data packets begin transmitting—your presence and actions here are being reported to something or someone at Yellowstone. The automated systems recognize you as significant, your journey as authorized, your progress as monitored.

A final confirmation message appears on the main screen: "CANDIDATE Y-3742 EN ROUTE. ADAPTATION PROGRESSION SATISFACTORY. YELLOWSTONE SANCTUARY PREPARING FOR INTEGRATION."

Outside, you notice changes throughout Alamosa as the power redistribution affects various systems. Lights activate in buildings that were previously dormant. Security protocols engage and disengage in patterns suggesting preparation for your departure. The settlement seems to be responding to your actions, adjusting itself according to some predetermined program triggered by your interaction with its core systems.

Whatever awaits you at Yellowstone is now aware of your approach. The automated infrastructure that has guided your journey—perhaps from the moment you awoke in that hibernation pod—continues to direct you toward your destination with mechanical patience. The question remains whether you are traveling of your own volition or simply following a path laid out for you long before you became conscious of it.

[[Continue your journey north|Long Drive]]


:: Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center {"position":"1900,750","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/bwRkGbmt/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center stands apart from other buildings in Gallup, its architecture incorporating elements of indigenous design alongside modern materials. Originally dedicated to preserving the cultural heritage of the region's Native American communities, the museum appears to have taken on additional significance during the crisis years.

Inside, exhibits are arranged in four main sections: the Native American cultural displays, archaeological artifacts, historical archives, and a modern gallery added during the climate collapse era. Though some displays have been damaged or removed, much remains intact—suggesting this knowledge was valued even as society crumbled.

The building has clearly served multiple purposes over the years. Evidence shows it functioned as both a cultural repository and a research center during the crisis, with scientific equipment installed alongside cultural artifacts.

[[Explore the Native American exhibits|Explore the Native American exhibits]] - Examine indigenous knowledge and artifacts.
[[Check the archaeological displays|Check the archaeological displays]] - Look at ancient findings from the region.
[[Search the archives|Search the archives]] - Investigate historical records and documents.
[[Visit the modern gallery|Investigate the modern gallery]] - View exhibits from the climate collapse era.

[[Return to Gallup|Gallup Options]] - Explore other locations in the city.


:: Repair Truck {"position":"4000,650","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/brzjw7rb/h.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

With all the necessary components gathered, you set to work repairing the Tacoma. The process is methodical—first replacing the degraded filtration system with the one you salvaged from the museum, then reinforcing the cooling system with components adapted from the locomotive, all using the specialized tools found in the hardware store.

The work takes several hours, but the result is worth the effort. The truck now runs more efficiently than before, its systems better equipped to handle the challenging conditions that lie ahead. You've essentially created a hybrid of different technologies—combining parts never intended to work together into a functional whole.

There's something deeply human about this act of adaptive reuse—taking materials from a collapsed civilization and reconfiguring them to serve present needs. It's a microcosm of how humanity has always functioned, building each generation's tools from the remnants and knowledge of those who came before.

Yet it also highlights our dependence on these created systems. While other species adapt biologically to changing conditions over generations, humans rely on technological adaptation—faster but requiring constant maintenance and resource inputs. When those systems failed at a global scale, most humans couldn't adapt quickly enough biologically to survive the changes they had triggered.

With the repairs complete, you pack your tools and prepare to leave Durango behind—another abandoned place that has served its final human purpose by providing what you needed to continue your journey.

[[Depart from Durango|Leave Durango]]


:: Residential Area {"position":"150,600","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Z5j4vKpR/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The residential area consists of a dozen homes spread across what once was a small subdivision. Most structures have partially collapsed under the weight of time and harsh conditions, but a few remain standing, their windows long shattered and doors hanging from hinges.

Desert plants have reclaimed the yards, and some houses show signs of previous scavenging. The largest home at the end of the cul-de-sac appears more intact than the others. One house has an unusual feature—what looks like a small bunker entrance in the backyard.

Near the center of the subdivision, you notice a series of mailboxes, some still containing faded papers. In one yard, what appears to be a child's room is partially exposed where a wall has collapsed.

[[Investigate the backyard bunker|Search the backyard bunker]] - Check the underground shelter.
[[Explore the largest house|Explore the largest house]] - Search the best-preserved building.
[[Look through the mailboxes|Check mailboxes]] - Examine old correspondence.
[[Search the children's room|Look through children's room]] - Examine the exposed living quarters.

Once you've finished exploring:
[[Return to town exploration| Eagar, AZ Options]] - Explore other locations in Eagar.


:: Rest for the night {"position":"1900,200","size":"100,100"}
After securing the motel room against possible intruders, you allow yourself the luxury of a proper rest. The bed is dusty but intact, far more comfortable than sleeping in your ATV or on the ground.

As exhaustion overtakes you, fragmented memories surface in your dreams—clinical facilities, people in white coats discussing "adaptation protocols," your body submerged in some kind of solution while monitoring equipment beeps steadily nearby.

You hear a voice: "Subject Y-3742 shows promising adaptation markers. Cognitive functions preserved despite accelerated cellular modifications. Recommend transfer to Yellowstone for final integration phase."

You wake with a start, the dream fading but leaving you with the unsettling feeling that your journey to Yellowstone isn't a coincidence—you were sent there for a reason, perhaps programmed to seek it out even after your memory was somehow wiped.

Despite the disturbing dreams, your body feels refreshed and your mind clearer. The rest was necessary, and you're now better prepared to face the challenges ahead.

(set: $rested to true)
(if: $tacomaKeys and $rested)[
  (set: $canLeaveGallup to true)
]

[[Return to the motel lobby|Desert Sands Motel]]


:: Rio Grande Medical Center {"position":"5475,125","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/mkr6qBhd/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Rio Grande Medical Center looms at the edge of town, its complex of buildings surrounded by a perimeter fence topped with sensor arrays that still activate as you approach. Warning indicators flicker to life, though whatever security response they were meant to trigger no longer functions.

Unlike typical hospitals, this facility appears to have been designed primarily for research rather than patient care. Specialized wings branch from the central building, each with distinct containment protocols and environmental controls. Some sections have been hermetically sealed, their windows opaque, while others show signs of catastrophic failures—doors forced open from within, containment barriers breached.

The most unsettling aspect is how the building itself has been modified with biological components. Engineered tissues form parts of the exterior walls, respiratory pore structures filter air entering ventilation systems, and what appear to be neural networks run through the structure like a nervous system. The line between building and organism has blurred, creating something that seems simultaneously alive and manufactured.

Warning symbols you don't recognize mark certain areas, alongside standard biohazard and radiation indicators. The facility was clearly handling dangerous materials—experiments that needed to be isolated from the outside world. Now those containment systems run on automated power, some failing, others adapting in unexpected ways to maintain their function without human oversight.

[[Search the Reception Area|Search the Reception Area]] - The administrative center that would have processed incoming patients or subjects.
[[Explore the Research Wing|Explore the Research Wing]] - A specialized section where experimental treatments were developed.
[[Check the Quarantine Wards|Check the Quarantine Wards]] - Isolation areas for those undergoing treatments or exhibiting concerning symptoms.
[[Investigate the Director's Office|Investigate the Director's Office]] - The administrative center that would have overseen operations.
[[Access the Basement Laboratory|Access the Basement Laboratory]] - A secure facility beneath the main building.

[[Return to Alamosa|Alamosa Options]] - Explore other areas of the settlement.


:: Roads to Yellowstone {"position":"1800,1500","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/kGz3vBwN/b.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The final approach to Yellowstone takes you through landscapes where the line between deliberate preservation and natural reclamation has become increasingly blurred. The route follows what was once a major highway, though the asphalt is now fractured and overgrown with vegetation that seems to incorporate both natural evolution and engineered adaptation.

Parts of the journey require you to navigate through areas with subtle warning signs—increased radiation levels, unusual atmospheric composition, and biological anomalies that your modified physiology seems specifically designed to withstand. Standard humans could not travel this path without specialized protection, yet you find yourself processing these conditions with surprising resilience.

The isolation deepens as you move farther from the established waypoints. The preserved information caches and maintained infrastructure give way to wilderness that has been transforming without human witness for decades. Yet even here, you occasionally find evidence of preparation—path markers designed to weather centuries of exposure, bridge reinforcements ensuring passage remains possible, and monitoring equipment still functioning despite the absence of anyone to receive its data.

As the terrain rises toward the Yellowstone plateau, you note increasing evidence of the environmental transformation that necessitated the sanctuary initiative. Species displaying clear adaptive mutations inhabit ecological niches that didn't exist before the collapse. Plants with characteristics of both natural and engineered origin form communities that process the altered atmospheric conditions with remarkable efficiency.

In these hybrid ecosystems, nature demonstrates its fundamental resilience—not preserving the specific forms that existed before human intervention, but incorporating those interventions into new systems that function according to the same basic principles of adaptation and resource utilization. The Earth continues, indifferent to whether humans recognize the resulting forms as "natural."

The road begins to climb more steeply, and you sense you're approaching a boundary of some kind. Ahead, a structure becomes visible—not the ruined infrastructure you've encountered elsewhere but something maintained, functioning, clearly marking the formal entrance to whatever Yellowstone has become.

A sudden doubt crosses your mind. You could turn around now, reject the path laid out for you by those who engineered your existence and seek your own way in this transformed world. Or you can continue forward, accepting the purpose for which you were created and discovering what awaits within the sanctuary.

[[Continue to Yellowstone entrance|Yellowstone Entrance]]
[[Turn back|Turn Around]]


:: Rummage through debris {"position":"775,450","size":"100,100"}
You carefully shift through the pile of debris in the corner of the store, raising clouds of dust that catch in your throat. Most of what you find is worthless—rotted packaging, broken plastic goods, fragments of ceiling tiles.

However, buried beneath a collapsed shelf, you discover a water-damaged journal. Many pages are illegible, but several entries remain readable. They describe how corporate water restrictions led to regional conflicts as the climate crisis worsened.

One passage stands out: "AquaTech has full control of the springs now. They're rationing to three liters per person per week—not enough in this heat. People are getting desperate. The highlands are calling, but how many of us will make it? The convoys north are being ambushed regularly now..."

The final entry is dated shortly before the final collapse: "There's talk of safe haven at Yellowstone. Something about a self-sustaining facility built before everything went bad. I don't know if it's real or just another false hope. If you're reading this and I didn't make it, maybe you'll have better luck."

This confirms the Yellowstone destination might be more than just a random coordinate. Something is waiting there—or was, at least.

[[Continue exploring the gas station |Abandoned Gas Station]]


:: Search Maintenance Shed {"position":"4825,250","size":"100,100"}
The Maintenance Shed stands near the perimeter of the array field, a utilitarian structure housing the equipment and supplies needed to keep the solar infrastructure functioning. Unlike the control station's sophisticated technology, this space has a more practical, hands-on character—workbenches, tool storage, and repair docks for the maintenance robots.

The shed's interior is immaculately organized, kept in precise order by the automated systems that continue to operate according to their programming. Tools hang in designated positions, spare parts are catalogued and stored in numbered bins, and maintenance schedules are displayed on monitors that have never powered down.

Discover specialized tools needed to repair the primary inverter (REQUIRED ITEM)—precision instruments designed specifically for the advanced technology of the array system. These tools incorporate both traditional mechanical components and more unusual elements that interface directly with the semi-biological aspects of the array technology.

(set: $maintenanceTools to true)

What makes this space particularly unsettling is its readiness—as if the human maintenance teams might return at any moment to resume their duties. Coffee cups sit by workstations, personal items remain in lockers, a half-completed repair log waits for entries that will never come. The abruptness of human departure is preserved here like an insect in amber.

Yet nature has begun its subtle reclamation even in this controlled environment. Engineered fungi have established colonies in the dark corners, breaking down synthetic materials into compounds they can metabolize. Small adapted insects have found their way inside, building nests from the paper of maintenance manuals. The boundary between the maintained and the wild thins with each passing year.

In one corner, you find the remains of what appears to have been a human attempt to modify the maintenance robots—tools scattered as if the work was abandoned mid-process, diagrams showing alterations to their programming. Notes nearby express concern about "autonomous decision expansion" and "priority hierarchy shifts"—suggesting someone noticed the systems beginning to evolve beyond their parameters and attempted to intervene before the final evacuation.

[[Return to the solar array field|Solar Array Field]]


:: Search abandoned vehicles {"position":"3850,375","size":"100,100"}
Several vehicles remain eternally parked along Main Avenue, frozen in time from the day of final evacuation. Most have been thoroughly stripped of useful components, their doors hanging open and interiors exposed to decades of weather.

You search methodically through the rusted hulks, finding little of value. The scene is a graveyard of petroleum-age technology—machines that once symbolized freedom and progress now slowly dissolving back into the earth. The metal oxidizes, plastic degrades, and glass returns to sand; nothing is wasted in nature's economy.

One small electric delivery van appears to have been overlooked by previous scavengers, perhaps because its battery technology was already obsolete before the collapse. Inside, you find only bits of unusable scrap metal, nothing that would help repair your truck.

What strikes you most is how these vehicles—once the pride of their owners and the backbone of daily life—now serve as planters for wind-carried seeds. Grasses grow from accumulated soil in wheel wells, and in one sedan, a small evergreen sapling has taken root in the back seat, pushing persistently toward sunlight through a shattered window.

The vehicles represent the hubris of a civilization that believed its infrastructure was an improvement upon nature rather than a temporary imposition. Now nature reclaims their components at its own pace, indifferent to human timescales or intentions.

[[Return to Main Avenue|Main Avenue]]


:: Search behind counter {"position":"1300,700","size":"100,100"}
You make your way behind the postal counter, where workers once sorted mail and assisted customers. Dust-covered monitors and sorting equipment sit idle, frozen in time.

In a drawer beneath the main counter, you find an employee handbook with handwritten annotations. The notes describe how postal workers maintained an "Emergency Communication Network" after internet and cellular systems collapsed—using traditional mail routes to connect surviving communities.

One section of the handbook, titled "Continuity of Government Communications," has been heavily annotated. The notes suggest that postal workers were enlisted to deliver sensitive communications between government facilities during the final stages of organized evacuation.

A scribbled note in the margin reads: "Route 87 to YS discontinued except for priority genetic candidates—authorization codes required. All other northern routes terminated."

This supports what you've been piecing together—access to Yellowstone was tightly controlled, with only selected individuals—"genetic candidates"—permitted to make the journey.

[[Return to the main post office area|USPS]]


:: Search behind the counter {"position":"775,250","size":"100,100"}
You carefully make your way behind the dusty counter, stepping over fallen ceiling tiles and shattered glass. The cash register has been pried open long ago, its contents taken. But tucked beneath a shelf, you find a metal box that seems to have been overlooked.

Inside is a crumpled map showing the route north with locations marked. It's faded but still legible, with handwritten notes about safe passages and danger zones. This could be invaluable for navigating the changed landscape ahead. Most crucially, it shows the route through New Mexico toward what was once Colorado.

(set: $map to true)
(if: $map and $solarChargingUnit and $ignitionChip)[
  (set: $canLeaveEagar to true)
]

You notice scribbled annotations about "AquaTech checkpoints" and "radiation zones" along certain routes. The previous owner seems to have been planning an escape north, but whether they succeeded remains a mystery.

[[Continue exploring the gas station |Abandoned Gas Station]]


:: Search the Communications Center {"position":"5925,225","size":"100,100"}
The Communications Center occupies a hardened facility at the district's center, its architecture designed to withstand extreme conditions and maintain function through environmental instabilities. Multiple redundant systems ensure continuous operation, and independent power sources keep critical functions online regardless of external conditions.

Inside, you find a sophisticated network hub that once coordinated all information flow within Alamosa and connected the settlement to the broader emergency response infrastructure. Broadcast equipment, data servers, and communication terminals fill the main operations floor, maintained in perfect readiness by automated systems that continue their programmed maintenance routines.

Discover broadcast logs of public announcements gradually shifting from "temporary evacuation" to "permanent relocation." The carefully crafted messages show how information was managed to prevent panic while gradually preparing the population for the reality that most would never return to their homes. Early announcements emphasize the temporary nature of measures; later ones introduce concepts of "long-term adaptation" and "selective resource allocation."

The progression reveals how access to accurate information became increasingly restricted, with different message sets targeted to different population categories. Those designated for sanctuary placement received specific preparation instructions; others received vague reassurances while actual support diminished. The stratification of information paralleled the broader stratification of survival opportunity.

Most disturbing are the security protocols establishing information quarantine around research facilities—preventing those working within from communicating concerns to the broader population, and restricting external awareness of experimental outcomes. The communications infrastructure, designed to connect people, was weaponized to isolate and compartmentalize knowledge at the moment when shared understanding was most critical.

Now these systems broadcast their messages to empty streets, announcement speakers activating on schedule to deliver updates no one will hear. Emergency alert systems monitor for crisis conditions without human observers to respond. The infrastructure of communication persists without content worth communicating—form divorced from function in the ultimate expression of technological isolation.

[[Return to the commercial district|Commercial District]]


:: Search the Engineering Department {"position":"5175,525","size":"100,100"}
The Engineering Department occupies a reinforced concrete structure with minimal windows and extensive security measures. Unlike the Bioscience Building's organic integration, this facility represents a more mechanical approach to adaptation—developing technologies to shield humans from the environment they had damaged beyond repair.

The main workshop floor contains half-completed machines—atmospheric processors, water purification systems, and modular habitation units designed for extreme conditions. Robotic assembly arms hang motionless above conveyor belts, their tasks eternally interrupted. The contrast between their precise engineering and their ultimate futility is a testament to humanity's misplaced faith in technological solutions to existential problems.

In a sealed documentation room, you find plans for the city-wide power grid and notes about the "emergency containment protocols" that activated when power failed. These protocols apparently initiated a controlled shutdown of certain research areas while maintaining critical life-support and preservation systems. The engineers who designed these failsafes clearly anticipated the possibility of human absence—preparing their technology to outlast them.

What's most striking is how the department's projects evolved from mitigation to adaptation. Early designs focused on reversing environmental damage; later ones accepted the changed world as permanent and instead sought to modify human habitats or humans themselves to survive within it. The transition marks the moment when humanity conceded defeat in its struggle against the consequences of its own actions.

A map on one wall shows a network of similar facilities across the continent, with Yellowstone marked as the "Primary Integration Center." Notes indicate that technologies developed here were intended for deployment there—your destination appears to have been the focal point of whatever adaptation strategy these researchers were pursuing.

Outside the main building, experimental renewable energy prototypes continue to function in the empty campus courtyard, generating power for nobody, their quiet hum a requiem for the civilization that created them.

[[Return to the university campus|Adams State University Research Center]]


:: Search the Reception Area {"position":"5475,250","size":"100,100"}
The reception area was designed to project calm and authority—a space to process incoming patients while hiding the more unsettling research conducted beyond its pleasant facade. Now it stands as an unintentional monument to institutional deception, its soothing colors and comfortable furnishings masking the ethically questionable work once performed here.

Information displays still cycle through reassuring messages about "adaptation assistance" and "evolutionary therapy"—euphemisms for procedures that the patient logs reveal were far more invasive and experimental than these gentle terms suggest. The dissonance between public presentation and actual practice is preserved in this frozen moment of institutional history.

At the reception desk, you find patient logs showing increasing cases of "Adaptive Mutation Syndrome" following exposure to floodwaters. The clinical notes document physical changes that would once have been considered monstrous but were classified here as "potentially advantageous evolutionary responses"—gill-like structures developing on some patients, skin changing to process oxygen differently, altered neurological patterns.

Most concerning are the acquisition records—many "patients" were brought in involuntarily after exhibiting spontaneous adaptations, their conditions treated as property of the research program rather than personal medical issues. The final months show a shift from helping those with unexpected mutations to actively seeking subjects with specific genetic markers for "directed adaptation initiatives."

Nature has begun to reclaim even this sterile space. An engineered fungal species has established itself in the ventilation system, releasing spores that have settled on surfaces throughout the room. The organism appears to be processing the chemicals in the building materials, breaking down synthetic compounds into forms more usable by the new ecosystems emerging outside.

The isolation felt by those processed through this system—removed from society, reclassified from persons to subjects—echoes in the empty space, a reminder of how institutions can separate individuals from both community and natural connections, all while claiming to act for the greater good.

[[Return to the medical center|Rio Grande Medical Center]]


:: Search the archive room {"position":"150,2375","size":"100,100"}
The archive room lies behind the public exhibition spaces, accessible through a staff door that stands ajar. Unlike the carefully maintained displays, this area shows signs of hasty reorganization during the final days—materials sorted not for historical preservation but for practical use by those who might come after.

You discover journals from the evacuation period describing "westward convoys to the Yellowstone sanctuary." These firsthand accounts provide details about the final phases of organized movement toward the sanctuary sites, when fuel and vehicle shortages forced evacuees to travel in consolidated groups under government protection.

The journals describe how Buffalo transformed during this period—from an ordinary small town to a critical waypoint in the evacuation infrastructure. Resources were increasingly centralized, with distribution controlled by authorities managing the evacuation. As the crisis deepened, access to transportation and supplies became directly tied to individual categorization, with those designated for sanctuary placement receiving priority.

Most revealing are accounts of the final days, when general evacuation ceased and only specifically selected individuals continued to be processed through Buffalo toward Yellowstone. The authors describe a profound shift in atmosphere—from chaotic emergency response to methodical implementation of predetermined protocols for preserving selected genetic lineages and specialized knowledge.

A final entry, dated just days before the archive was sealed, reads: "The last convoy departed this morning. Those of us who remain have been instructed to complete preservation protocols and then proceed to secondary sites according to our designations. Buffalo will stand empty but ready for those who might follow years from now—the carriers of whatever humanity becomes after we're gone."

The isolation experienced by those final inhabitants—maintaining systems for future travelers while knowing they themselves were not among the chosen—echoes in the empty archive. Their work ensured that information vital to your journey would survive decades of abandonment, a final service to a future they would never see.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Jim Gatchell Memorial Museum]]


:: Search the archives {"position":"2175,675","size":"100,100"}
The archives section contains historical records from the region, carefully preserved in climate-controlled cases. Maps, personal accounts, government documents, and photographs tell the story of the Southwest from the 19th century through the climate collapse.

Of particular interest are records of indigenous "high ground settlements" that became models for post-flood survival colonies. These traditional sites, often located in elevated areas with natural springs, had been continuously inhabited for centuries—their designs incorporating natural cooling, water conservation, and protection from extreme weather.

Government documents from the early crisis years show how these traditional settlement patterns were studied and incorporated into "Sanctuary Initiative" plans. Yellowstone was apparently selected partially because indigenous knowledge identified it as a "place of power and renewal" that would remain viable despite changing conditions.

One file contains correspondence between anthropologists and government officials debating the ethical implications of the "directed adaptation" approach. Some argued that deliberately accelerating human evolution was playing god; others insisted it was the only way to ensure survival on a transformed planet.

[[Return to the museum|Red Rock Museum & Cultural Center]]


:: Search the backyard bunker {"position":"300,750","size":"100,100"}
The bunker entrance is a heavy metal hatch set into concrete, designed to withstand extreme conditions. It takes all your strength to pull it open, revealing a ladder descending into darkness below.

Using your multi-tool's small light, you carefully climb down. The air is stale but breathable. The bunker is small—just one room about ten feet square—but well-stocked with survival supplies. Most perishables have long since rotted, but sealed containers hold some still-useful items.

On one wall, preserved newspaper clippings tell the story of the "Mountain West Resource Wars" that followed the coastal flooding. As refugees fled inland, conflicts over diminishing resources intensified. You read about water rights battles, food distribution riots, and eventually, evacuation orders to designated "sustainable zones."

One clipping mentions "Operation Higher Ground"—apparently a government initiative to relocate select citizens to fortified highland facilities. The article is incomplete, but it appears Yellowstone was one of these designated safe zones, reinforcing the message from your hibernation pod.

You gather what useful supplies you can find—some water purification tablets and a small first aid kit—before climbing back to the surface.

[[Continue exploring the residential area|Residential Area]]


:: Search the control room {"position":"650,750","size":"100,100"}
The control room contains banks of dusty monitors and control panels, once used to manage water collection, purification, and distribution throughout the region. Most systems are dead, but emergency backup power still illuminates a few essential displays.

You examine the facility schematics on the wall, showing how rainwater and groundwater were collected, treated, and rationed during the crisis years. Notes scribbled on the diagram indicate that water quality deteriorated as the crisis worsened, requiring increasingly complex filtration.

As you inspect the main control panel, something catches your eye—a small access panel partially hidden beneath a fallen ceiling tile. Opening it reveals a secure compartment containing what appears to be an electronic key or chip. This could be useful. 

(set: $ignitionChip to true)
(if: $map and $solarChargingUnit and $ignitionChip)[
  (set: $canLeaveEagar to true)
]

[[Continue exploring the facility|Water Collection Facility]]


:: Search the county records {"position":"375,1500","size":"100,100"}
The county records office occupies a secure wing of the courthouse, its space filled with precisely organized documentation of land ownership and usage throughout the region. During the crisis, this facility was repurposed from property administration to monitoring the accelerating changes in territory and geography as environmental systems destabilized.

Find land surveys showing how the geography changed after the floods, with notes about "uninhabitable zones" and "adaptation-friendly territories." The progression of these documents tells the story of rapidly shifting habitability—areas once considered prime real estate becoming uninhabitable due to changed conditions, while previously marginal territories gained new value based on their stability or resource potential.

What's most revealing is how property concepts evolved as the crisis deepened—early documents maintaining traditional ownership frameworks even as the land itself transformed, later ones shifting toward categorization based on habitability and adaptation potential rather than legal title. The final surveys abandon property concepts entirely, simply mapping territories according to their capacity to support human survival under various adaptation scenarios.

The most recent documentation focuses specifically on the territories between Jackson and Yellowstone, with detailed analysis of environmental conditions along potential travel routes. The precision suggests continuous monitoring continued until very near the end of organized operations, with the data preserved specifically to guide travelers like yourself years later.

The isolation inherent in these materials—land records without owners, property without society, geography without politics—reflects the fundamental irrelevance of human legal constructs when faced with environmental transformation. The careful lines drawn on survey maps became increasingly meaningless as the natural world redefined territory according to its own emerging patterns.

[[Return to the courthouse|Johnson County Courthouse]]


:: Search the equipment shed {"position":"1275,2350","size":"100,100"}
The equipment shed stands beside the main trailhead building, its sturdy construction designed to secure valuable tools and supplies. During the evacuation, this facility was repurposed from recreational support to critical resource cache for those continuing westward after general infrastructure failed.

Discover specialized gear for monitoring "atmospheric anomalies" along the western route. These instruments were designed to detect environmental hazards invisible to human senses—radiation zones, areas of toxic gas concentration, regions with dangerously altered atmospheric composition—allowing travelers to navigate safely through increasingly hostile terrain.

The equipment reflects technological adaptation to the transformed environment—conventional designs modified to function under post-collapse conditions, with emphasis on durability, independence from support infrastructure, and operation without specialized knowledge. Each piece represents an attempt to extend human capability in the face of challenges that conventional biology couldn't overcome.

Most revealing are the evolutionary iterations visible in the equipment designs—early versions focused on temporary protection and data gathering, later ones assuming permanent exposure and prioritizing survival functionality above all else. The progression mirrors the broader shift from scientific monitoring to practical survival tools as the collapse accelerated.

The final additions to this cache focus specifically on the route to Yellowstone, with specialized equipment calibrated for known hazards along that specific path. The precision suggests continuous monitoring and adaptation of these resources until very near the end of organized operations, with the knowledge gained incorporated into the tools left for future travelers.

The isolation anticipated for users of this equipment—moving through dangerous terrain without support infrastructure or expert guidance—is built into every design aspect. Self-containment, intuitive operation, and fault tolerance became primary engineering considerations, anticipating a world where technical assistance could no longer be assumed and individual survival depended entirely on preparation.

[[Return to the trailhead station|Bighorn Trailhead Station]]


:: Search the front desk {"position":"1875,50","size":"100,100"}
You make your way behind the dust-covered front desk. The old computer terminal is dead, but a large leather-bound registry book sits open, its last entries dating to just before the final collapse.

As you flip through the pages, you notice a dramatic spike in check-ins during the weeks before communications went down. Hundreds of names crowd the normally sparse pages, most with northern destinations listed beside them.

Notes in the margins, written in increasingly frantic handwriting, tell a story of mass migration. "Three more government convoys heading north today." "Family of 6 arrived on foot from Phoenix—water situation critical there." "Military checkpoint established on northern highway—turning people back without clearance codes."

The final entry, dated just days before the total collapse, reads: "Evacuation transport promised for essential personnel never arrived. Highway north blocked by landslide. Manager taking remaining staff to try eastern route tomorrow."

Beside the registry, you find a stack of government notices about "Mandatory Relocation Protocols" and "Adaptation Candidate Processing." One document lists "Yellowstone Sanctuary" as a designated relocation site for "Category A Adaptation Candidates" only, with strict genetic qualification requirements.

[[Return to the motel lobby|Desert Sands Motel]]


:: Search the information desks {"position":"25,1500","size":"100,100"}
The information desks stand in strategic locations throughout the visitor center, their design optimized for efficiently directing travelers to appropriate resources. During the collapse, these stations were repurposed from tourist assistance to evacuation processing, and finally to supporting the specific information needs of future travelers to Yellowstone.

Discover logs of evacuee processing with genetic categorization ratings. These records document how individuals were evaluated and sorted based on adaptation potential, specialized knowledge, and genetic profiles deemed suitable for the sanctuary initiatives. The progression shows increasingly restrictive selection criteria as resources dwindled and sanctuary capacity became more limited.

The desk contents reveal how information itself was rationed during the final phases—different categories of travelers receiving different levels of disclosure about conditions at destination sites, alternative routes, and long-term prospects. Those designated for primary sanctuaries like Yellowstone received comprehensive briefings; others were given increasingly limited information corresponding to their categorization status.

Most disturbing are the final processing protocols, which shifted from evaluating existing traits to actively preparing selected individuals for what awaited them at sanctuary destinations. Briefing materials reference "psychological adaptation preparation" and "cognitive conditioning for transition acceptance"—suggesting that even those chosen for Yellowstone were not being fully informed about what awaited them there.

The information systems now stand preserved but static—screens frozen on final displays, communication equipment maintained in operational condition but disconnected from any functioning networks. The isolation of these information nodes from the broader knowledge infrastructure they once accessed creates a stark reminder of how completely the systems of shared understanding collapsed alongside the physical environment they were designed to interpret.

[[Return to the visitor center|Visitor Center & Transportation Hub]]


:: Search the lobby {"position":"4100,250","size":"100,100"}
The Strater Hotel's lobby was once a showcase of Victorian opulence, designed to impress arriving guests with its handcrafted woodwork and period furnishings. Now it stands as an unintentional museum—a preserved moment from before the collapse.

At the reception desk, the guest registry remains open, its final pages filled with entries from the evacuation period. Examining these records, you find evidence of how Durango served as a "Highland Transfer Station" during the mass migrations. Notes beside guest names include cryptic codes: "C-3 Yellowstone," "B-2 Grand Teton," "D-1 Relocation Pending."

A logbook behind the counter contains staff notes documenting the hotel's transformation from luxury accommodation to emergency housing. The tone grows increasingly desperate as resources dwindled and evacuation transport became unreliable.

The final entry reads simply: "Government transport arrived for Category A and B guests. Category C transport cancelled. Staff advised to seek alternative evacuation. May God help those left behind."

The grand lobby, with its elaborate furnishings designed to project permanence and stability, now serves as testament to how quickly human social structures collapsed when environmental systems failed. The luxury that once symbolized humanity's triumph over nature now decays under nature's patient reclamation.

What's most striking is the absence—spaces designed for human congregation now standing empty for decades, their purpose rendered obsolete. The hotel was built on the assumption of continued human presence, an assumption that proved as impermanent as the society that made it.

[[Return to the hotel entrance|The Strater Hotel]]


:: Search the pharmacy {"position":"900,2350","size":"100,100"}
The pharmacy section occupies a secure area of the plaza, its inventory carefully preserved through specialized storage techniques. During the evacuation, this facility transitioned from commercial prescription service to medical support for the processing operations, and finally to a resource cache for future travelers along the Yellowstone route.

Discover medications developed for "transition symptoms" with labels matching different physical changes. These specialized pharmaceuticals were designed to address adverse effects associated with the accelerated adaptation processes being implemented in certain individuals designated for sanctuary placement.

The medications catalog a disturbing range of physiological changes being induced or managed—respiratory system modifications, metabolic alterations, neurological adjustments, and immune system recalibrations. The clinical language masks the profound nature of these interventions—human biology being deliberately modified to increase survival probability in the transformed environment.

Most concerning are the progressive development dates on these medications, showing how the adaptation procedures became increasingly aggressive as the crisis deepened. Early formulations addressed minor symptoms and gradual transitions; later ones managed more fundamental physiological restructuring with higher risk profiles but potentially greater adaptive benefits.

The final pharmaceutical protocols focus on stabilizing and supporting adaptations already induced rather than initiating new ones—suggesting that by the end of organized operations, the fundamental work of transforming selected humans had been completed. Those continuing to Yellowstone were not simply relocating to a sanctuary but carrying within their altered bodies the blueprint for humanity's next evolutionary stage.

The isolation of medical knowledge evident in these materials—specialized information restricted to those implementing the adaptation program—reflects the broader fragmentation of human understanding during the collapse. The expertise necessary to comprehend these procedures, let alone reverse them, died with the specialists who developed them, leaving only their transformed subjects to carry the results forward.

[[Return to the plaza|Mountain View Plaza]]


:: Search the research annex {"position":"4525,525","size":"100,100"}
The research annex contains more recent studies conducted during the climate crisis years, focused on understanding and facilitating human adaptation to rapidly changing conditions. Unlike the main gallery's historical perspective, this section documents scientific work conducted with urgent present needs in mind.

Here you discover files profiling different genetic responses to the flood environments—clinical studies of how human physiology reacted to altered atmospheric conditions, changed disease vectors, new food sources, and increased radiation. The research catalogs both harmful and potentially beneficial mutations observed in populations across different regions.

Most disturbing are the references to "directed adaptation initiatives"—attempts to accelerate beneficial evolutionary changes through genetic intervention rather than waiting for natural selection. These programs apparently began as voluntary medical treatments but became increasingly systematic as the crisis deepened.

One study profiles "respiratory adaptation candidates" who developed enhanced oxygen processing capabilities—potentially beneficial in high-altitude environments with changing atmospheric composition. Another documents increased radiation resistance in populations exposed to damaged nuclear facilities—an unintended but potentially advantageous mutation.

All of these studies reference Yellowstone as the "Primary Integration Environment" where successful adaptation candidates would be consolidated. The facility is described as "humanity's controlled evolutionary leap forward"—a place where natural and directed adaptations would be combined to create humans capable of thriving in the changed world.

The clinical language masks the profound ethical questions these programs raised. Survival pressured humanity toward interventions previously considered unthinkable, blurring the line between adaptation and engineering new subspecies of human—decisions made by increasingly desperate researchers rather than through societal consensus.

[[Return to the museum entrance|Museum of Mountain Living]]


:: Search the research department {"position":"525,2350","size":"100,100"}
The research department occupies a secure wing of the building, its laboratory spaces and data centers designed for proprietary scientific work. During the crisis, these facilities were repurposed from energy research to supporting the Yellowstone Initiative, with evidence of close collaboration between corporate scientists and government programs.

Discover studies on "methane-adapted microorganisms" and their potential applications for human adaptation. The research explored how certain organisms naturally evolved to thrive in environments with altered atmospheric composition, with the goal of potentially inducing or accelerating similar adaptations in human biology.

The evolutionary implications are sobering—scientists acknowledging that humans could not survive in the transformed environment without themselves being transformed. The research represents a fundamental shift in approach—from attempting to preserve the environment in a state suitable for humans to modifying humans to suit the altered environment.

Most disturbing are the later research directions, which moved beyond merely understanding natural adaptations to actively inducing them. Experimental protocols detail methods for accelerating evolutionary processes, compressing changes that would naturally take thousands of generations into the span of decades or even years. The desperation driving this work is evident in the increasingly extreme approaches considered acceptable as the crisis deepened.

The final projects focused on specific adaptations believed necessary for long-term survival in the Yellowstone habitat—respiratory modifications for altered atmospheric conditions, metabolic adjustments for different available nutrition sources, and neurological changes to support altered sensory processing in the transformed environment. The research crossed boundaries that pre-collapse ethics would have prohibited, driven by the recognition that conventional human biology represented an evolutionary dead end in the world humanity had created.

[[Return to the company headquarters|Energy Company Headquarters]]


:: Search the research laboratories {"position":"825,1500","size":"100,100"}
The research laboratories occupy the central building of the complex, their spaces designed for the precise scientific work necessary to understand and modify plant genetics. During the collapse, these facilities were repurposed from conventional agricultural science to studying the fundamental biological mechanisms of adaptation, with applications extending beyond crop species to the broader questions of biological survival in rapidly changing conditions.

Discover studies on rapid evolutionary responses in plants, animals, and human tissue samples. These investigations document the adaptive mechanisms triggered by environmental stress—genetic expressions activated by specific conditions, epigenetic responses to novel stimuli, and accelerated selection pressures resulting in evolutionary changes within timeframes previously considered impossible.

The research progression shows increasingly interventionist approaches as the crisis deepened—observational studies giving way to induced adaptations, and finally to directed evolution protocols where specific traits were deliberately selected and amplified. The transition reflects the growing understanding that natural evolutionary processes would be too slow to respond to the rate of environmental change triggered by human activity.

What's most significant is how the research focus expanded beyond its original agricultural mandate to encompass broader questions of biological adaptation, including human biology. Later studies explicitly examine how principles discovered in plant adaptation might be applied to human populations facing similar environmental pressures, with direct references to the "accelerated human evolution initiatives" being implemented at sanctuary locations like Yellowstone.

Most concerning are the final research directions, which abandoned the goal of maintaining recognizable human biology in favor of exploring what forms of conscious life might successfully inhabit the transformed Earth—investigations that considered radical biological modifications that would create human descendants significantly different from their ancestors.

The laboratories now stand as monuments to the ultimate failure of technological optimism—the belief that sufficient knowledge and technical capacity could solve any crisis, even one as fundamental as rendering Earth incompatible with human biological requirements. The precision equipment preserved here represents the pinnacle of scientific capability deployed too late and with too limited a vision to prevent catastrophe.

[[Return to the research station|Agricultural Research Station]]


:: Solar Array Field {"position":"4825,125","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/gknTHzCj/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Solar Array Field stretches across what was once the eastern outskirts of Alamosa, thousands of panels arranged in precise geometric patterns that maximize exposure throughout the day. Unlike the haphazard decay evident in most abandoned infrastructure, these arrays show signs of ongoing maintenance—not by human hands, but by automated systems that continue their programmed functions decades after their creators' departure.

Robotic maintenance units move methodically between the rows, replacing damaged components, clearing debris, and adjusting positioning mechanisms. They operate with single-minded purpose, preserving the power generation capacity that sustains Alamosa's continuing automated functions. Their focused activity in the otherwise empty landscape creates an unsettling impression—purpose without consciousness, function without meaning.

The arrays themselves represent an evolution in solar technology—panels that appear to incorporate biological components, surfaces that self-clean through engineered microscopic cilia, positioning systems that mimic plant heliotropism. The integration of technological and biological approaches that characterizes much of Alamosa reaches its most advanced expression here, where the boundary between machine and organism has effectively dissolved.

[[Examine the Control Station|Examine Control Station]] - The central hub that manages the array's operations.
[[Search the Maintenance Shed|Search Maintenance Shed]] - A facility housing repair equipment and spare parts.
[[Check the Underground Conduits|Check Underground Conduits]] - Access tunnels beneath the array field.
[[Investigate the Communication Tower|Investigate Communication Tower]] - A structure relaying data from the array to other systems.

[[Return to Alamosa|Alamosa Options]] - Explore other areas of the settlement.


:: Start Maze {"position":"6700,150","size":"100,100"}
Standing at what feels like the entrance to this strange forest labyrinth, you take stock of your situation. The trees create natural walls, their massive trunks and tangled undergrowth forming corridors that wind between them. The forest floor is covered in a carpet of moss that seems to glow faintly, providing just enough light to see by.

You notice something odd about the layout—the paths seem almost deliberately arranged, too regular to be natural. Could this forest have been shaped somehow? Or is your mind simply trying to impose order on wilderness?

The canopy above is thick, allowing only rare glimpses of what might be sky. And what little you can see doesn't look right—the color is off, a deep indigo rather than blue, even though your internal clock tells you it should be midday.

Two paths lie before you—one leading left through a narrow gap between twisted trees, the other right past a distinctive rock formation covered in luminescent fungi.

Something tells you that your choices here matter—that finding your way through this maze is not just about physical navigation but something more significant.

[[Take the left path|1l]]
[[Take the right path|1r]]


:: Start Maze2 {"position":"7400,100","size":"100,100"}
With the Tacoma's headlights illuminating the narrow canyon ahead, you begin your journey through this labyrinthine system. The walls rise so high on either side that only a thin strip of strange indigo sky is visible far above. The bioluminescent organisms growing on the rock faces seem to respond to your vehicle's presence, brightening as you pass and then dimming behind you—as if tracking your progress.

The canyon floor is unnaturally smooth in places, suggesting this passage might have been at least partially engineered rather than formed by natural processes. Occasional markers embedded in the ground emit faint pulses of light, potentially indicating safe pathways through the minefield.

As you drive deeper into the maze, you notice a disturbing pattern—areas where the canyon walls show evidence of massive explosions, with shattered rock and scorched surfaces. These must be locations where previous travelers deviated from the safe path with catastrophic results.

The way ahead splits, offering two possible routes. To your right, a passage slopes gently upward, with more consistent marker lights along its path. Directly ahead, the canyon continues on a level plane but narrows significantly, with fewer visible safety indicators.

You must choose carefully—the wrong path could trigger the deadly countermeasures buried beneath the canyon floor.

[[Take the right path upward|r1]]
[[Continue straight ahead|d1]]


:: Step In {"position":"5375,500","size":"100,100"}
The decision made in an instant—perhaps driven by curiosity, perhaps by some deeper programming in your own modified genetics—you step into the Adaptation Chamber. The door seals automatically behind you, and the machine activates as if it had been waiting specifically for your arrival.

A computerized voice announces: "Candidate recognition confirmed. Adaptation protocol resuming. Phase three initiation."

Before you can reconsider, restraints emerge from the chamber walls, securing your limbs as a translucent fluid begins to fill the space. The liquid has an unusual quality—not quite water, not quite gel—and as it rises past your chest, you discover you can still breathe within it. The sensation is alien but not uncomfortable, as if your body remembers something your conscious mind does not.

The machine hums with increasing intensity, and you feel changes beginning at a cellular level—connections forming between your nervous system and the chamber's interfaces, information flooding directly into your mind. Images flash through your consciousness: laboratories, procedures, genetic sequences, and finally Yellowstone—not as it was, but as it was intended to become, a controlled environment for the next phase of human evolution.

You understand now that you were always meant to return here—that your journey to Yellowstone is the completion of a program initiated before the collapse. Your memories were suppressed to ensure you would navigate the changed world using your adaptations instinctively, gathering data that would be harvested upon your arrival at the sanctuary.

The realization comes too late. The chamber's processes accelerate beyond their designed parameters, your presence after so many years triggering failsafes that have themselves failed with time. The fluid begins to boil around you as power surges through systems never meant to operate autonomously for decades.

In your final moments of consciousness, you grasp the ultimate irony—the human attempt to control evolution through isolation from natural processes has failed not because the technology didn't work, but because nature incorporated that technology into its own systems. Your modified body becomes just another element returned to the cycle of adaptation and change that continues long after human plans have faded.

[[Game Over]]


:: Step Inside {"position":"1425,575","size":"100,100"}
You approach the front door and examine the keypad. After trying a few common combinations with no success, you notice a small interface panel beside the door with a fingerprint scanner.

Without warning, the panel lights up and scans you. A mechanical voice announces: "Biometric signature detected. Adaptation profile not in database. Security protocol activated."

Before you can react, concealed defense systems emerge from the walls. A high-pitched tone sounds, followed by the discharge of what appears to be a lethal gun.

The last thing you see before losing consciousness is a robotic maintenance unit emerging from a panel in the wall, its sensors focused on your fallen form.

[[Game Over]]


:: Story Start {"position":"375,375","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/XYnnvyZm/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The hiss of releasing pressure wakes you. A cold, sterile light burns through your eyelids as the hibernation pod's seal breaks. Your limbs feel heavy, unfamiliar—how long have you been asleep?

You struggle to sit up, fighting against muscles atrophied from disuse. The room around you is a mess of broken equipment and flickering emergency lights. Other pods line the walls, all empty or damaged beyond function. Yours seems to be the only one that completed its wake cycle.

As you stumble to your feet, fragments of memory return like distant echoes—rising waters, evacuation orders, the desperate rush to the highlands. But your own name, your history, your purpose... these remain frustratingly blank.

On a shattered console nearby, a single message repeats: "YELLOWSTONE SANCTUARY REMAINS VIABLE. ALL SURVIVING CANDIDATES PROCEED TO DESIGNATED COORDINATES."

Below the message is a map with a route marked from your current location—identified as Eagar, Arizona—northwest through what was once New Mexico, Colorado, Wyoming, and finally to Yellowstone. The journey looks arduous, stretching across transformed landscapes you barely recognize.

Whatever Yellowstone holds, it must be important enough that someone went to great lengths to preserve you for this journey.

As you take stock of your situation, you find a small pack with basic supplies—enough water for a few days, some emergency rations, and a multi-tool that's seen better days. You'll need to find more resources if you're going to survive the trek ahead.

[[Take A Quick Look of What is Around| Eagar, AZ Options]]

