:: Take the eastern route toward Alamosa {"position":"3200,75","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/C17QmzMB/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You turn eastward at the junction, the Tacoma's tires crunching on the rougher surface of this less-traveled road. The terrain gradually flattens as you descend from the highlands of the reservation, opening into vast plains that stretch toward the horizon.

What would have once been arid grasslands have transformed into something new and strange. Crystalline formations glitter across the landscape where mineral-rich water evaporated after the flooding cycles, creating alien geometries that catch and refract the sunlight. In places, the ground itself seems to pulse with subtle bioluminescence from fungal networks that have adapted to the changed soil chemistry.

The road passes through the remnants of small towns, most abandoned but some showing signs of having been repurposed long after the collapse. One settlement appears to have been converted into a research outpost, with sophisticated solar arrays surrounding a reinforced central building. Though there are no people visible, the equipment appears maintained.

The wildlife here has undergone more visible adaptations than what you observed in the highlands. You spot herds of antelope-like creatures with translucent patches on their flanks that might be primitive gill structures, allowing them to process the higher moisture content in the altered atmosphere. Overhead, flocks of birds move in mathematically perfect formations, their collective behavior suggesting enhanced neural connections.

As you continue eastward, the Sangre de Cristo Mountains come into view, their slopes showing strange banding patterns where different plant species have established themselves in response to the changing climate conditions. Rivers flowing from those mountains spread across the San Luis Valley in new patterns, creating a network of shallow lakes and wetlands where once there was drier terrain.

By late afternoon, you approach Alamosa. Unlike other settlements you've seen, this one shows clear signs of having been deliberately preserved and modified rather than simply abandoned. The buildings have been reinforced and adapted to the changed climate, with high-efficiency solar panels and water collection systems visible on many structures.

Most striking is the complex of buildings that must have once been Adams State University. The campus has been transformed into what appears to be a research center, with large greenhouse domes and antenna arrays suggesting ongoing scientific activity. Though no people are visible from this distance, lights flicker in some of the buildings, indicating power systems still function.

This place might hold crucial information about the changes that have shaped this world—and perhaps about what awaits you at Yellowstone.

[[Enter Alamosa|Alamosa Options]]


:: Take the western route toward Durango {"position":"3400,325","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/h4bDRrvT/a.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

You choose the western path, driving past the cairn and following the road as it begins to climb into more mountainous terrain. The Tacoma's engine works harder as the elevation increases, but the vehicle handles the stress admirably.

The landscape grows increasingly dramatic with every mile. Eroded canyons reveal layered strata of red, orange, and gold stone, their formations altered by decades of changed weather patterns. In some places, new waterways have carved passages where none existed before the climate collapse, creating unexpected oases where adaptive vegetation thrives.

You pass the ruins of smaller settlements, most abandoned long ago, their structures partially reclaimed by the wilderness. Occasionally, you spot evidence of more recent visitors—organized campsites, maintained sections of road, and once, a distant column of smoke that dissipates before you can determine its source.

Wildlife has returned to these highlands in strange new forms. You spot deer with unusually iridescent coats that seem to reflect sunlight in patterns that might be camouflage or might serve some other evolutionary purpose. Birds with wingspans far larger than their pre-collapse ancestors soar on thermal currents above the valleys.

As afternoon turns to evening, the San Juan Mountains come into view—their peaks both familiar and alien, with new growth patterns visible even at this distance. The snowpack on the highest elevations has a strange bluish tint, perhaps from mineral content or some adaptive algae that has established itself in the changed conditions.

The road winds through increasingly narrow passages until finally, as the sun begins to set, you spot Durango in the valley ahead—once a thriving mountain town, now a collection of partially collapsed structures with a few areas showing signs of more recent habitation.

What catches your eye immediately is the rail yard at the edge of town, where the historic narrow-gauge railroad once carried tourists through the mountains. Several railcars appear to have been repurposed into living quarters or workshops, with smoke rising from makeshift chimneys.

This place might hold resources for your journey, and perhaps more pieces of the puzzle that is drawing you toward Yellowstone.

[[Enter Durango|Durango Options]]


:: The Strater Hotel {"position":"4150,125","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/XYh3k11S/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Strater Hotel rises above Main Avenue, its ornate Victorian façade weathered but still impressive. Once the crown jewel of Durango's historic district, the hotel now stands silent, its rooms empty of the guests and staff that once filled it with life.

Inside, the lobby retains echoes of its former grandeur. Elaborate woodwork and period furnishings remain largely intact, protected from the elements by the building's solid construction. Dust covers everything in a uniform gray layer, softening the edges of the human-made environment and gradually erasing the distinctions between objects.

Without regular maintenance, the building has begun to develop its own ecosystem. Where the roof has leaked, mushrooms grow from saturated carpets. Swallows have built nests in the ornate crown molding, their droppings creating the foundation for small pockets of soil where windblown seeds have taken root.

The hotel seems suspended between its human past and its non-human future—still recognizable as a structure built for human use, but incrementally transforming into something that serves the needs of other species.

[[Check the lobby area|Search the lobby]] - Examine the main reception and common areas.
[[Explore the historic guest rooms|Explore the historic rooms]] - Investigate the accommodation areas on upper floors.
[[Visit the hotel restaurant|Check the hotel restaurant]] - Look for supplies in the dining facilities.
[[Look through the administrative offices|Investigate the hotel offices]] - Search for records or useful items.

[[Return to town center|Durango Options]] - Explore other areas of Durango.


:: Turn Around {"position":"1800,1275","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/nVPJJPZs/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

Something inside you rebels against the predetermined path, a surge of autonomy pushing back against the programming that has guided you this far. Perhaps it's the buried fragments of whoever you were before becoming "Subject Y-3742," or maybe it's an emergent property of the modifications themselves—an adaptive response that even your creators didn't anticipate.

You stop at the threshold of the sanctuary and turn away from Yellowstone, choosing uncertainty over the destiny engineered for you. The path back stretches before you, returning through environmental hazards and dwindling resources toward an outside world that wasn't designed to accommodate beings like you.

As days pass, your modified systems begin to show signs of strain. Your body was engineered specifically for conditions awaiting you at Yellowstone—adaptations that become liabilities in environments they weren't calibrated to withstand. Symptoms appear gradually at first, then with accelerating severity—cellular degradation that no conventional medicine could address.

The profound isolation of your choice becomes clear. Created as a link in a chain that would connect pre-collapse humanity to whatever was meant to evolve within Yellowstone, you've broken that connection by rejecting your intended purpose. Neither fully part of the world that was nor integrated into whatever might emerge from the sanctuary, you exist in a liminal state that proves ultimately unsustainable.

Your final moments come in a quiet glade far from both your origin and intended destination. As consciousness fades, you experience a strange sense of completion—not the fulfillment of your designed purpose, but the satisfaction of having exercised the most human of traits: the choice to determine your own path, even when that path leads to an end rather than a continuation.

The ecosystem around you will incorporate your modified biology, integrating the adaptations engineered in you into the ongoing processes of natural selection. In this way, you contribute to the planet's evolutionary progress even in rejecting the specific role humans designed for you—final proof that Earth's systems will continue with or without human direction, indifferent to intentions but responsive to materials.

[[Game Over]]


:: USPS {"position":"1225,575","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/nVwmBpK3/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The small brick post office building stands as a reminder of pre-collapse communication networks. Its front windows are broken, and the door hangs ajar, but the structure itself remains intact.

Inside, dust covers everything. Sorting tables still contain long-abandoned mail, their delivery interrupted by the final stages of societal breakdown. The walls bear faded posters about postal service updates, including notices about "Emergency Communication Protocols" that were apparently implemented as digital networks began to fail.

Behind the counter, you can see an office area with what might have been the postmaster's desk. A bulletin board on one wall is covered with notices, and a door beyond the counter appears to lead to a back room.

[[Check the undelivered mail|Check undelivered mail]] - Sort through the abandoned letters and packages.
[[Look behind the counter|Search behind counter]] - Examine the postal workers' area.
[[Read the bulletin board|Examine bulletin board]] - Review the posted notices and information.
[[Explore the back room|Access back room]] - See what might be stored in the rear areas.

[[Return to Fence Lake|Fence Lake Options]] - Continue exploring elsewhere.


:: Visitor Center & Transportation Hub {"position":"25,1375","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/VLhLKN5V/c.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The Visitor Center & Transportation Hub occupies a prominent location at the edge of Jackson, its architecture designed to welcome travelers and facilitate movement throughout the region. During the collapse, this facility was repurposed from tourism infrastructure to evacuation management, and finally to serving as a waypoint for future travelers making the specific journey to Yellowstone.

The main building contains information displays oriented toward different audiences—recreational tourists from the pre-collapse era, evacuees during the crisis period, and finally, travelers like yourself following this route years after organized evacuation ceased. The progression reflects the facility's evolution from supporting optional leisure travel to facilitating necessary survival movement.

Most striking is how the transportation infrastructure was selectively preserved—certain routes maintained while others were allowed to deteriorate, vehicles modified for specific journey requirements rather than general mobility, and fuel caches positioned along designated routes. The pattern suggests deliberate decisions about what connections would remain relevant in a post-collapse context focused on sanctuary access.

The hub now stands as a node in a drastically simplified transportation network—not the interconnected web of routes that once facilitated movement throughout the continent, but a linear path directing selected travelers toward Yellowstone. The contraction reflects the broader focus on preserving specific critical functions while surrendering the general infrastructure that supported normal societal operation.

[[Explore the main lobby|Explore the main lobby]] - Check the central information area.
[[Search the information desks|Search the information desks]] - Look through visitor resources.
[[Investigate the maintenance area|Investigate the maintenance area]] - Examine transportation support facilities.
