:: Yellowstone Ending {"position":"2400,1500","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/bNSjN9Nm/f.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The integration chamber lies at the heart of Yellowstone Sanctuary, a space where technology and biology have achieved perfect synthesis. Living circuitry covers the walls, processing information through cellular rather than electronic pathways. The air itself seems alive with microscopic organisms that form a communicative medium between all present.

You're guided to a central platform designed to interface directly with your modified systems. As you connect, your experiences during the journey flood into the sanctuary's shared consciousness—the transformed landscapes you traversed, the waypoints you discovered, the adaptations your body developed in response to environmental challenges. Your isolation ends as you become part of something larger, your individual journey contributing to the collective understanding of what Earth has become.

Through this connection, you finally access your complete memories—not just fragments glimpsed throughout your journey, but the full context of your creation and purpose. You were not born but engineered, one of many specialized beings dispatched to gather data about the transformed world that would inform humanity's continued adaptation. Your identity before modification exists as an archived template, preserved but effectively replaced by what you have become.

The guide explains the sanctuary's true nature: "When we understood that environmental collapse was irreversible, we faced a choice—attempt to preserve humanity in its familiar form and perish, or direct our evolution toward forms that could thrive in the world we had inadvertently created. Yellowstone became the crucible for this transformation—not an ark preserving the past, but a laboratory creating the future."

Through your neural connection, you perceive the scope of changes already implemented. The beings around you represent multiple generations of directed evolution, each more adapted to the transformed Earth than the last. They retain human consciousness and values, but their physical forms and perceptual frameworks have diverged dramatically from the species that dominated the pre-collapse world.

"You will join us now," the guide continues, "your experiences integrated, your adaptations incorporated into our continuing evolution. Through you and others like you, we maintain connection with the wider world rather than becoming isolated within our sanctuary. This is humanity's continuation—not as conquerors of nature but as participants in its processes."

As the integration completes, you experience a profound shift in perspective. Your individual journey—the isolated trek across a transformed continent—expands into a collective experience spanning decades of adaptation and change. You remain yourself, but that self now exists within a community of beings navigating an unprecedented evolutionary transition.

The irony becomes clear in this moment of transcendent understanding: in their desperate attempt to survive the consequences of environmental devastation, humans have been forced to surrender the very attributes that drove them to dominate and transform the planet. The beings emerging from Yellowstone will carry human consciousness forward, but their relationship with Earth's systems will be fundamentally different—integrated rather than oppositional, adaptive rather than controlling.

Earth continues its planetary journey through space, indifferent to the names or forms of the species that inhabit its surface. The sanctuary represents not humanity's triumph over natural processes but their final acceptance of participation within them—an integration that may ultimately prove more sustainable than the civilization that collapsed under the weight of its own transformative power.

As your consciousness expands to encompass both your individual journey and the collective future unfolding within Yellowstone, you understand that you have reached both an ending and a beginning—the conclusion of humanity as it was, and the emergence of whatever it is becoming.

[[Return to Main Menu|Menu]]


:: Yellowstone Entrance {"position":"2000,1500","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/ZRYSbkCN/d.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The entrance to Yellowstone Sanctuary rises before you, a structure that defies the pattern of abandonment and decay you've encountered throughout your journey. Unlike the waypoints that preserved information and resources while accepting their eventual reclamation by natural processes, this facility appears actively maintained—its surfaces clean, its systems operational, its purpose ongoing rather than historical.

A gate of sophisticated design spans the roadway, incorporating both advanced technology and biological elements that seem to process information through living tissue rather than electronic components. Sensors activate as you approach, scanning your physical form with invisible fields that produce a momentary tingling sensation throughout your body.

After a pause, the gate emits a tone that resonates oddly with something in your modified physiology. An automated voice addresses you: "Subject Y-3742 identified. Stage 3 adaptation confirmed. Welcome to Yellowstone Integration Center. Proceed to processing."

The gate opens, revealing a roadway that winds deeper into what was once Yellowstone National Park. Unlike the wilderness outside, the environment here shows signs of deliberate cultivation—engineered ecosystems where plant and animal species display adaptations far more advanced than those you've encountered elsewhere, as if evolutionary processes have been accelerated beyond their natural pace.

The isolation you've experienced throughout your journey takes on a new quality here—not the absence of human presence but the presence of something that has evolved beyond conventional humanity. The sanctuary appears to be populated, but whether the entities moving among these advanced ecosystems are still recognizably human remains to be determined.

As you proceed through the gate, you understand that you're crossing a threshold between the world that was—still recognizable despite its transformation—and whatever humanity has been becoming in this controlled environment. Your identity as "Subject Y-3742" now supersedes whatever you might have been before, as you prepare to integrate with the community for which you were specifically engineered.

The road curves ahead, leading toward structures visible in the distance—the heart of the sanctuary where answers await about both your purpose and the ultimate fate of the species that created you to carry their legacy in modified form.

[[Proceed into Yellowstone|Yellowstone Tour]]


:: Yellowstone Tour {"position":"2200,1500","size":"100,100"}
<img src="https://i.postimg.cc/Px6kN6wR/e.png" alt="Starting Image" style="display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;">

The road leads you deeper into Yellowstone Sanctuary, revealing an environment that represents the culmination of humanity's desperate adaptive measures. What was once a national park has been transformed into a controlled evolutionary laboratory where the boundary between natural and engineered has dissolved entirely.

Domed structures rise from the landscape, their surfaces incorporating living tissues that process sunlight and atmospheric gases with extraordinary efficiency. Between these buildings, carefully designed ecosystems display plant and animal species that have been modified to thrive in conditions that would be lethal to their pre-collapse ancestors. Everything here exists in dynamic equilibrium—not preserved in a static state but constantly adapting within controlled parameters.

As you approach the central complex, you encounter the sanctuary's inhabitants for the first time. They move with fluid grace through environments that would be hostile to conventional human biology, their forms displaying the same integration of natural and engineered components you've observed throughout the sanctuary. These beings represent what humanity has become through directed evolution—recognizably descended from the species that once dominated Earth but transformed to suit the planet they inadvertently created.

A guide approaches—a being that appears more extensively modified than others, with features suggesting specialized adaptation for communication with new arrivals. When it speaks, the words resonate not just through your ears but directly with the modified neural structures in your brain, creating a form of communication more immediate than conventional language.

"You have traveled far, Y-3742. You represent the final integration cohort—the last of those we dispatched to gather experiential data through the transformed outside territories. Your journey completes the adaptive cycle we initiated when environmental collapse became irreversible."

The guide leads you through a series of environments, each displaying different aspects of the sanctuary's function. Research facilities where biological and technological innovation continues despite the collapse of wider human civilization. Habitation zones where modified humans live in communities structured around their specific adaptations. Cultivation areas where food species with extraordinary properties are developed and grown.

Throughout the tour, you gradually comprehend the true purpose of your journey—not merely to reach Yellowstone, but to experience the transformed world in ways that would inform the continuing adaptation of sanctuary inhabitants. The memories and physiological data from your travels will be incorporated into the ongoing evolutionary process, ensuring that whatever humanity becomes will maintain connection to the world outside rather than diverging into an entirely separate existence.

As the tour concludes, you're brought to a central chamber where your integration will be completed—the final step in the purpose for which you were created.

[[Complete your integration|Yellowstone Ending]]


:: d1 {"position":"7400,200","size":"100,100"}
You choose to continue straight ahead, guiding the Tacoma through the narrowing canyon. The walls press closer on either side, occasionally forcing you to retract the side mirrors to avoid contact with the rock face. The markers become sparser, requiring careful attention to avoid straying from the safe path.

The bioluminescent organisms here form different patterns—less random and more structured, with geometric arrangements that suggest deliberate cultivation. Some configurations resemble warning symbols or directional indicators, though whether these were created intentionally or represent the organisms' natural growth patterns remains unclear.

As you progress, you notice unusual acoustic properties in this section of the canyon—sounds from the Tacoma's engine reflect and amplify in unexpected ways, sometimes seeming to come from ahead rather than around you. Occasionally, you hear what might be echoes of other vehicles or machinery, though nothing is visible in your limited field of vision.

The passage widens suddenly into a small circular chamber with three potential exits—one continuing downward at a steeper angle, one that appears to curve to the left, and another that branches to the right. Each looks equally viable, with no obvious indicators of which might be safer.

[[Take the descending path|d2]]
[[Follow the leftward curve|l2]]
[[Choose the right branch|r2]]


:: d2 {"position":"7400,300","size":"100,100"}
You steer the Tacoma down the steeper descending path, the vehicle's weight requiring careful braking to maintain control on the decline. The canyon walls close in further here, creating an almost claustrophobic passage where your side mirrors occasionally scrape against the rock despite your best efforts.

The bioluminescent organisms grow more sparsely in this section, leaving longer stretches illuminated only by your headlights. The darkness reveals new details—crystalline formations embedded in the rock that reflect and refract light in complex patterns, creating momentary illusions of movement and depth that make navigation even more challenging.

The floor of this passage shows disturbing evidence of previous travelers who didn't succeed in navigating safely—debris that might have once been vehicles, though transformed by time and the strange properties of this place into barely recognizable forms. More concerning are areas where the ground has been scorched or cratered—clear indications of detonated mines.

The path levels briefly before offering two choices—a continued descent that appears to spiral deeper into the canyon system, or a left-hand branch that might reconnect with other passages you've glimpsed.

[[Continue descending|landmind]]
[[Take the left branch|l4]]


:: d2a {"position":"7300,300","size":"100,100"}
You navigate into a section of the canyon that descends in a gradual spiral, creating disorienting perspectives where walls seem to curve impossibly and dimensions become difficult to judge accurately. The Tacoma's navigation system, already compromised, begins displaying increasingly erratic information, occasionally projecting holographic warnings that flicker into existence before dissolving.

This area shows more extensive signs of artificial modification—support structures embedded in the canyon walls, monitoring equipment still operational despite decades of abandonment, and floor surfaces too uniform to have been formed by natural processes. Whatever purpose this place served, it required significant engineering to create and sustain.

The bioluminescent growth here forms patterns of remarkable complexity, arranged in configurations that resemble data visualizations or topographic maps. Some sections appear to display real-time information about conditions throughout the canyon system, suggesting the organisms function as a distributed monitoring network, sensing and communicating changes in their environment.

The spiral passage eventually opens into a small chamber with three potential exits—one continuing the descent, another that appears to lead back to previous sections you've explored, and a third that branches in a new direction.

[[Continue the descent|d2b]]
[[Return to familiar territory|r2]]


:: d2b {"position":"7300,400","size":"100,100"}
The descending path grows increasingly technical, requiring careful navigation as the Tacoma's wheels find purchase on the steep grade. The canyon narrows to a confined space where the walls press close enough that you can observe minute details of the strange amalgamation of natural rock and engineered components that form this place.

In this section, technological elements predominate over the natural—data conduits embedded in the stone still pulse with information transfer, monitoring devices track your progress with mechanical precision, and occasional holographic markers flicker into existence to display warning symbols or navigation aids before dissolving back into nothingness.

The descent seems to be taking you toward some core function of this elaborate system—a central processing hub or control center perhaps, though whether still operational or long abandoned remains to be seen. The increasing density of technology suggests approaching something of critical importance to whatever purpose this place once served.

As you navigate a particularly sharp turn, the pathway ahead suddenly reveals a troubling sight—evidence of a massive detonation where the safe path appears to have been compromised. The cratered floor and scorched walls indicate one of the countermeasures was triggered, obliterating both the threat and the passage itself.

You have little choice but to back up and seek an alternative route.

[[Return to the previous junction|d2a]]


:: l2 {"position":"7500,200","size":"100,100"}
The Tacoma navigates the leftward curve with precision, its enhanced suspension compensating for the uneven terrain. This passage is wider than the others, allowing more comfortable maneuvering, though the safety markers are inconsistently placed, requiring constant vigilance.

The canyon walls here show evidence of water erosion—smooth, undulating surfaces carved by flowing liquid over long periods. Occasionally, small rivulets still trickle down the rock face, collecting in shallow pools that reflect the bioluminescent light in hypnotic patterns. The water itself has an unusual quality—slightly viscous and with a subtle luminescence of its own.

As you progress, the canyon ceiling begins to lower, the towering walls gradually converging overhead until they form a natural tunnel. The enclosed space amplifies the sounds of your vehicle and creates unusual acoustic effects—whispers and echoes that seem to carry fragments of conversation or data transmissions, though no clear words are discernible.

The tunnel branches, offering two choices—continuing forward on your current level or a path that ascends toward what appears to be an opening to the upper canyon system.

[[Continue on the current level|d1]]
[[Take the ascending path|u1]]


:: l3 {"position":"7600,100","size":"100,100"}
The upward path grows steeper, challenging the Tacoma's impressive climbing capabilities. The tunnel walls here are unmistakably engineered—smooth surfaces inlaid with technological components that still function, lights activating as you pass and monitoring devices tracking your progress with silent precision.

You emerge onto a high ledge overlooking a vast section of the canyon system below. From this vantage point, the true scale and design of the maze becomes apparent—not random passages but a deliberately constructed network forming patterns too large to discern from within. The bioluminescent growth viewed from above creates complex geometric shapes that suggest meaning or purpose beyond mere navigation.

The ledge continues forward but narrows significantly, barely wide enough for your vehicle. Alternative paths branch to the left, where the ledge widens into what appears to be a constructed platform, and downward via a steep but navigable ramp that rejoins the lower levels.

As you consider your options, you notice movement in the canyons below—not human figures but something else, entities that seem to flow along the passages as if partly solid and partly liquid. Whether these are evolved life forms or something created through technological means is impossible to determine from this distance.

[[Continue along the narrow ledge|l3a]]
[[Investigate the platform to the left|u1]]


:: l3a {"position":"7600,200","size":"100,100"}
You carefully maneuver the Tacoma along the narrow ledge, its wheels uncomfortably close to the precipitous drop on your right side. The path is barely wide enough for your vehicle, requiring absolute precision in your steering. One miscalculation could send you plummeting into the depths below.

The ledge curves gradually, following the contour of what appears to be a massive central chamber in the canyon system. From this elevation, you can observe more of the strange entities moving through the lower passages—their movements suggesting purpose rather than random wandering. They appear to be maintaining or interacting with the bioluminescent networks, perhaps serving some role in the ecosystem that has developed here.

Embedded in the rock beside the path, you notice monitoring equipment still functioning after decades—cameras that track your movement, sensors that appear to be analyzing your vehicle or perhaps you yourself. The level of technological preservation suggests this place was considered vital enough to warrant extraordinary measures for continued operation without human presence.

The narrow ledge eventually connects to a broader pathway offering three options—continuing at your current elevation, descending via a winding ramp, or taking a tunnel that cuts through the rock face to your left.

[[Maintain your current elevation|l3b]]
[[Descend to the lower levels|l3c]]
[[Enter the tunnel|l3a]]


:: l3b {"position":"7600,300","size":"100,100"}
You continue along the broader pathway, maintaining your current elevation. The route provides a commanding view of the canyon system below, revealing more of its artificial nature. What first appeared to be natural formations now clearly show evidence of extensive engineering—the entire network designed to channel movement along specific routes while monitoring whatever passes through.

The pathway is marked with symbols embedded in the stone, glowing with the same bioluminescence that permeates the canyon. These markings bear resemblance to circuit diagrams but incorporate organic elements—a written language that bridges technology and biology, perhaps developed during the final era of human presence to communicate with both mechanical and organic systems.

As you progress, you observe more of the flowing entities in the canyons below, their movements becoming more organized and purposeful. They appear to be converging toward a central point deep in the maze, suggesting some form of collective activity or response to an unseen stimulus.

The path splits again, offering choices that will determine your approach to whatever lies at the center of this elaborate system—continuing forward along your current path, ascending to an even higher vantage point, or beginning a gradual descent that would eventually rejoin the main canyon floor.

[[Continue forward|l3a]]
[[Begin the descent|l3c]]
[[Ascend higher|l3]]


:: l3c {"position":"7600,400","size":"100,100"}
You guide the Tacoma onto the descending path, following a series of switchbacks that gradually return you to the lower levels of the canyon system. The route appears deliberately designed for vehicular descent, with berms and guardrails at the sharpest turns—another indication that this entire network was engineered for controlled movement.

As you descend, the bioluminescent growth becomes more prevalent, covering larger sections of the canyon walls in pulsing patterns that seem to respond to your presence. The organisms appear to be communicating, transmitting information about your movement through the network in visual signals that ripple ahead of your path.

The descent provides changing perspectives on the canyon system, revealing sections that weren't visible from above. You notice structures embedded in the walls that resemble research installations or monitoring stations, their designs suggesting they were built to operate autonomously for extended periods without human maintenance.

Near the bottom of your descent, you observe one of the flowing entities up close for the first time. It moves with unsettling grace, its form neither fully solid nor liquid but something in between—a composite organism that appears to incorporate both biological and technological components in a seamless integration. It takes no notice of your vehicle, focused entirely on its interactions with the bioluminescent networks.

The path levels out, rejoining the main canyon floor and offering two potential routes forward.

[[Return to a familiar section|l3b]]


:: l4 {"position":"7500,300","size":"100,100"}
The left branch provides a momentary respite from the constant descent, leveling out into a wider section where the canyon walls recede slightly. The additional space allows you to stop the Tacoma briefly, checking its systems and considering your progress through this bewildering maze.

This area shows signs of having been used as a waypoint or checkpoint during whatever operations once took place here. Embedded in the canyon wall is what appears to be a monitoring station, its displays still functional though displaying information in formats you can't fully interpret—data streams about environmental conditions, movement tracking, and what might be biological metrics.

The bioluminescent growths in this section form more deliberate patterns, arranged in what resemble status indicators or directional markers. Their positioning suggests they were either deliberately cultivated this way or have adapted to mimic the technological systems they've grown alongside—another example of the blurring between natural and artificial in this transformed world.

As you prepare to continue, you notice a subtle vibration through the steering wheel—not from the engine but transmitted through the ground beneath your wheels. The sensation increases gradually, suggesting large-scale movement or activity somewhere deeper in the canyon system.

The path ahead splits once more, offering two options that appear equally traveled.

[[Follow the rightward path|d2]]
[[Take the path straight ahead|s1]]


:: landmind {"position":"7400,400","size":"100,100"}
You guide the Tacoma onto the side passage, the vehicle's tires crunching on a surface that feels different from the relatively smooth paths you've traversed thus far. Too late, you notice subtle signs of disturbance in the soil pattern—circular depressions and unnatural regularities that suggest buried objects beneath.

A harsh mechanical click resonates through the canyon, followed by an ominous whirring sound from directly beneath your vehicle. The warning at the maze entrance suddenly takes on immediate, terrible relevance—you've strayed from the safe path onto a mined section of the canyon floor.

Time seems to slow as your mind processes what's happening. The countermeasure device buried beneath you has activated, its triggering mechanism engaged by the weight of your vehicle. The whirring sound intensifies as ancient but still-functional machinery prepares to deliver its lethal purpose.

You have perhaps a second to react—not nearly enough time to move the Tacoma clear of the danger zone. In the final moment before detonation, you glimpse movement at the edges of your vision—the flowing entities you observed earlier, now rapidly retreating from the imminent explosion.

The blast tears through the canyon, the concussive force amplified by the confined space. The Tacoma absorbs the initial impact, its reinforced undercarriage disintegrating as the explosion lifts the vehicle from the ground. Secondary detonations cascade through the area as adjacent mines trigger in sequence.

Your journey ends here, another victim of the boundary enforcement protocols established to control movement through this transformed landscape—systems outlasting their creators and continuing their programmed functions long after their original purpose became meaningless.

[[Game Over]]


:: r1 {"position":"7300,100","size":"100,100"}
You steer the Tacoma to the right, following the upward-sloping passage with its more consistent safety markers. The canyon walls here are less sheer, with occasional ledges and recesses breaking their uniformity. The bioluminescent growth is more abundant, casting a blue-green glow that reduces your reliance on the vehicle's headlights.

As you ascend, you notice patterns carved into the rock face—not natural erosion but deliberate markings that resemble circuit diagrams or mathematical equations. Some appear ancient, while others have the precision of modern machinery. The juxtaposition suggests this place has held significance long before the collapse, perhaps repurposed by those who engineered the current hazardous navigation system.

The passage begins to curve sharply, doubling back on itself in a way that makes maintaining orientation difficult. Your instincts tell you that you're traveling in circles, but the continuous upward slope contradicts this impression. It's as if the space itself doesn't conform to normal geometry—another impossibility in a world where such impossibilities have become commonplace.

Ahead, the path splits again, with no clear indication which route might be safer.

[[Return to the main canyon entrance|Start Maze2]]


:: r2 {"position":"7300,200","size":"100,100"}
You steer the Tacoma along the right-hand path, which curves gently while maintaining a relatively level grade. This section of the canyon appears more naturally formed than others you've traversed, with fewer obvious signs of engineering or modification. The walls show traditional erosion patterns alongside the ever-present bioluminescent growth.

The relative naturalness of this passage is deceptive, however. Occasional patches of ground show subtle differences in coloration and texture—areas where the soil has been disturbed and replaced. The regular spacing of these patches suggests they mark the locations of the buried countermeasures mentioned in the warning at the maze entrance.

Moving with extreme caution, you follow what appears to be a safe route between these dangerous areas. The Tacoma's sensors occasionally emit warning tones when you drift too close to the edge of the established path, suggesting the vehicle itself has some capability to detect the threats beneath the surface—perhaps a feature added during its adaptation for post-collapse conditions.

The passage widens into another junction point, offering two potential routes forward. Both appear equally viable, with no obvious indicators of which might be safer or lead more directly toward an exit from this labyrinthine system.

[[Continue along the rightward curve|d2a]]
[[Take the path ahead|d1]]


:: s1 {"position":"7500,400","size":"100,100"}
You choose the path leading straight ahead, following what appears to be a main artery through the canyon system. The passage widens significantly here, suggesting it was designed as a primary route for larger vehicles or higher traffic volumes during whatever operations once occurred in this place.

The walls in this section bear unmistakable signs of engineering—surfaces too precise to be natural, reinforced with materials that have withstood decades of exposure. Embedded sensors still activate as you pass, their purpose unclear but their operation suggesting autonomous power sources that continue to function without human maintenance.

As you progress, the character of the bioluminescence changes, becoming more uniform and controlled. Rather than the organic sprawl seen elsewhere, here the growth forms perfect lines along the canyon walls, illuminating the path with steady light that requires no supplement from your headlights. The integration of biological and technological elements reaches its most sophisticated expression in this central passage.

Ahead, you begin to see increasing brightness—not the confined glow of the bioluminescent organisms but something more substantial. The canyon appears to be opening into a larger space, the tight confines giving way to what might be an exit from this labyrinthine system.

The path presents a final choice—continue straight toward the apparent exit, or take a side passage that loops back into the canyon system.

[[Head toward the exit|Exit Maze]]
[[Take the loop back into the maze|landmind]]


:: u1 {"position":"7500,100","size":"100,100"}
You guide the Tacoma onto the ascending path, the engine straining slightly against the increased incline. The tunnel transforms as you climb, its walls transitioning from natural rock to something that appears partially engineered—surfaces too smooth and regular to be formed by natural processes alone.

Light filters from above, not the thin strip of sky visible earlier, but a different illumination source embedded in the tunnel ceiling. These lights pulse with a regular rhythm, potentially serving as navigational aids or monitoring systems. Their design suggests functionality surviving decades without maintenance—another example of technology outlasting its creators.

The air quality changes as you ascend, becoming less dense and losing the metallic quality present in the lower canyons. Whatever environmental conditions were being maintained or monitored below seem less prevalent here, suggesting you're approaching a boundary between different zones of this elaborate maze.

The tunnel widens into another junction, offering two potential routes—continuing upward toward what appears to be the upper levels of the canyon system, or branching left along a horizontal passage lined with more elaborate bioluminescent formations.

[[Continue upward|l3]]
[[Take the left passage|l2]]


:: use machine {"position":"1600,100","size":"100,100"}
You insert the key into the control panel and turn it. The machine hums to life, lights flickering across its display. After a moment, a mechanized voice speaks:

"AgriGen Wildlife Adaptation Monitoring Station. Last maintenance: October 12, 2088. Station operational but low power. Beginning attraction sequence."

A small reservoir on the side of the machine fills with water—apparently drawn from some underground source. The machine emits a low sound, barely audible to your ears.

After several minutes of waiting, you notice movement at the edge of the property. A deer approaches cautiously, but something about it is strange. Its fur has an unusual iridescent quality, and its antlers seem to have crystalline formations growing from them.

The deer approaches the water station and drinks, seemingly unaffected by your presence. As it finishes and turns to leave, something falls from its modified antlers—a thin, flexible panel that lands on the ground.

You cautiously retrieve it after the deer departs. It's a high-efficiency solar panel, likely a newer technology than what you found in Eagar. This could significantly improve your ATV's range.

